      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>SILVER PENCILLED HAMBURGHS.</p>
        <p>From the "Feathered World."</p>
        <p>ITAM BURGHS.</p>
        <p>Where are the Hamburghs gone? It is now almost an unconmon sight to find a few specimens of this grand old breed, at least in these parts. I suppose this beautiful variety has given place to some of the newer breeds? And one wonders why this should be so, for amongst all breeds there are none to surpass the Hamburgh for beauty. As layers they excel, surpassing by far many of the more mcdern breeds. The above picture represents a pair of the Silver Pencilled variety. Besides these we have the gold pencilled, black, silver spangled, and gold spangled. A flock of Hamburghs in any of the foregoing colours are a sight to behold indeed. They are a very charming breed of fowls. It goes without saying that one does not expect fine table qualities and beauty of feather markings, as well as good laying qualities combined in so small a fowl. Nevertheless, if not a large breed ,their flesh is of a good quality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>FOR EXHIRITIOg PURPOSES.</head>
        <p>FOR EXHIRITIOg PURPOSES.</p>
        <p>As an exhibition bird the Baniburgh stands at the top of the tree. The best show specimens of to-day are almost perfect in colour. In this breed there is plenty of room for the amatuer. It is a variety which holds out plenty of possibilities and with careful selection you could sour get into the front rank as an exhibitor. Just let me tell you what one prominent breeder has to say aboirl them. Writing for the " Feathered World" year book Mr. Charles Holt amongst other things says • With regard to the future prospects of the Hamburgh fancy a great deal must be undertaken if we intend that Hamburghs shall not be overwhelmed by new breeas, which seem to crop up year by year. The Haruburgh Club has done nothing to encourage the breed, and it Ibehoves those breeders who stick to</p>
        <p>their guns to devise some means of infusing new blood into the ranks. Hamburghs afford as good an opening for amateurs as any brted in existence. They are eaiy to breed, easy to rear, and easy to exhibit. There 18 plenty of room at the top, good specimens are Always saleable. Hamburghs have no superiors for eg production. "I can support all that Mr. Holt has to &amp;ay about this beautiful breed and can with confidence recomend their claims to the amateur. They do not give so many disapointments as some of the newer breeds. What I mean is, that you can reasonably expect a large percentage of the chicks to turn out true to colour. Good luck then to the Hamburgh and ,may they find many new PribPrelltB. 311 INT.IM S.</p>
        <p>Those of many readers who are badly fixed for accommodation and yet desire to keep a few fowls need not despair. Try your hand with the l wee ones.' They afford one a great amount of pleasure. Besides they are not outsiue consideration flora the profit side, especially when taken up for exhibition pur- poses. Bantams of mat varieties are useful from a utility point of view. As layers the best will be found among the following. old English Game, Wyandotte, Humburgh, Scotch Grey and Black Rosecombs. And what of their table qualities? Well! they are only small of course, but I would far rather have a nice plump game bantam than a pigeon. They are profitable to keep. They are small eaters, good layers and although the eggs are small they are of a fine rich flavour. Surplus stock is easily disposed of. I often notice that where a few bantams are kept that there is nearly always a demand for them from neighbours, chiefly being bought for children's pets. TOE EXHIBITION SIDE.</p>
        <p>When considering the exhibition side of Bantam keeping I may tell you that there is almost as much i money in them as n the larger breeds of poultry. Sittings of eggs fetch equal to most of the other breeds, and for individual specimens you have to pay pounds for a good one. 'lhe writer of these lines has heard a bargain discussed whets 5_30 was involved for a single specimen, and if I remember rightly it was Mr. E. C. Stretch, of poultry fame who once sold a Pile Game bantam cock for £5O. A visit to any show will at once prove to the novice that the bantam plays a very prominent part in the exhibition side of poultry farming. Here you will find a splendia classification. I have never yet visited a poultry show where bantams were not catered for. There is a great deal to learn about their habits and management. If any one of my readers thinks of taking up bantams as a hobby, if be will write to me, I shall be pleased to put him on the right track. There is a little book called "Bantams as a hobby " which should be in the hands of all who intend taking them up. I would tell you where to get this and also give you all the advice I could. I should need several columns to</p>
        <p>do anything ljk justice to the midgets, oii you will drop me a line to the " Examiner " office 1 will do all I can to assist you. Meanwhile just notice the Bantam section of a poultry show when next you visit one. 'Here you will find plenty to interest you and you will see something new I feel sure. ROW TO CURE DROODIES.</p>
        <p>To cure a hen of —being- broody, place her in a wooden crate with only a floor of laths. Here she will have nothing substantial to rest on. Let the crate be so situated that the occupant is able to see the rest of the stock. This will tend to make her uneasy, and help her to forget so much sooner her broody tendencies. Feed her sparingly, but give her plenty of clean water to drink. In a few days the brocrainess will be gone, and at the same time you will not have harmed the bird. Some people resort to very cruel methods in trying to cure broody hens. These are not at all necessary. It is a very simple matter to make a crate on the lines already pointed out. Always keep this handy and just drop "Mrs. 'Broody " in gaol whenever there are no eggs to incubate. There is no need to be cruel in the process of curing a hen of brcodv propensities.</p>
        <p>BREAD CRUSTS. These are a very useful diet for poultry. They should first of all be steeped in boiling water and then covered with as much milk as will soak into the bread. I know of no better soft food for young growing chicks. It is a good plan to save the crusts until you get enough for about five meals. 'f hen you can give the above diet as a change on alternate days. The</p>
        <p>crusts take no harm by be"mg kept. As a matter of fact—providing they are kept dry— they improve with keeping for a little while.</p>
        <p>QUERIES.</p>
        <p>"A Beginner" writes: I have a few hens which I am going to kill off this back end in one run. I have also some chickens (one month old) in another small run which I wish to transfer to the same run. How long should I let the run be idle and what is the best thing to do with it before I place the chickens in it? Answer :—lf yours is a grass run it should be ready to receive the chicks in two weeks time after the follow- ing treatment. Give the surface a sprinkling over with new fallen lime. If the rain continues it will wash the lime in • if the weather is dry, it will be necessary to give the run several gocd wateringa, so as to help the lime in its purifying prccess. If yours is just an earth run, fork the surface soil "up and sprinkle lime int.) the soil, then sow some mustard seed. The object of sowing the mustard seed is that it will revel in the rich soil and thus sweeten and purify the run. Turn the chicks into the run when the mustard is about 2ins. high.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LIBERAL DANCE.</head>
        <p>A LIBERAL DANCE.</p>
        <p>Under the patronage of the Prime Minister and Mrs. Asquith, the Strand Liberal Association is giving a dance on Wednesday, June 12th, at the Botanical Gardens. among those taking an active interest in the dance are the Marchioness of Crew e. Earl Spencer, the Countess of Granard, Lady Cowdray, Lord and Lady Weardale, Lady Nunburnholme, Lady Sheffield, Lady Mond, Lady Perks, Sir Charles and Lady Schwann, Lady Morris, Mrs Winston Churchill, Mrs A H Crosfield, Mrs Harcourt, Mrs 3 A Pease and Mrs Cecil liarmsworth.</p>
      </div>
