by 21-11. bni,,thellgame of the match proved to be a good win , rels for J W Rigby disposed of A Forster l'ln;en ais was the most decisive ivictory of the ec01.„"4 reflects much credit on the lLiberal man. 14h-44142 ef the various men had been keenly watched ' *ltieralri-,"gh the majority of the spectators knew the 081 "ad vvon they were surprised to hear that it Y a seven point victory. -- V. SENIOR CONSERVATIVES. ip.',letp:+'.althe team were entertained by the Senior Iret,`be -;,'lverr on the Sankey-street green and although oe'n^tiv,ZrOvions encounter away from home the Con- -115d'0? ~'LatinT,l lost by 84 points they bad the advant:ige eta .."55 rind reduced the majority of the wire- -0.0 ~. , o thj:ll% 3 points. -- ' ,oh Rreen •,e/.. Rho Puzzled some of the Rylands' men and ,ttfa Dot . were fortunate to win in the previous games tt 'oe h..everely to the test. W Cottam, who did 80 ' Ikiv:llßtip:l3 ,Ovcrl land, had all his work cut out to beat 18011t5 ''' oy two points The Conservative bad the esi'dia, f,R,P °I:, to the 25th end when the score was 16-14 Ithil4d b"'• Cottam then equalised and at the 30th . "et ~,iID-18. Hughes only added another chalk, a, --°rY came to his opponent at the 33rd end, 111 I.t , 0? t,od , 00 1, ge '. ibit? oi/cira,,a "all over" H Williams (Ryland's) and $„ -.:- after 26 ends, it was easy to be seen 'lonn tOrray-street men knew every inch of the land ((t"lnext game W hlillington disposed of G ,IN3 ntora Y ands) by 21-6. The following game was ii, elienly t !qoiting l character for H Langley (Ryland's) real and I,ernPhant over J Holland by the odd chalk. i 144, °I 20 a.,' from the first and at the 23rd end he had a 9' h' However before he could get his single . 'he e,.,"rari run out tbe winner by the odd point much 4._e . 1. ; a Th ' ‘4 of the Conservative supporters. 100 • o o -- Ittl4rVallaaB°)A gained a magnificent victory for the tinkloiNe (trtivyei: for he defeated A Stringer by 21-9. %V lit'd of i rl,lBl was quite no to his usual f