        <p>li4Q° l'ad absolutely nowhere and ttoptsp4r4gate-lane men were two winning cards 1 W Brown (King een-‘-••"lug the ones in which ... isreturned in their krll,°4 Vg-, heat W Sirnister by -1-I.llaend the one in ki..kg4tßa Ashbrook disposed of highest core for a INgliß end nor three points. Regan with an The ig' b F Clare who bestg lip, vieen player was a run of lb 9 Most w H. Eyes. . u vtki te„ e --- against J Hands tl 6t th 'lBO up a bit of a show . °We °Oth nut 1 . Hands, Gqi oer, - end they peed with 14 eacb e elfs B°°n added three doubles and a single to "gie and ran out the winner by six chalks. Wj4,lle Oro, )11k •,P "'lit g re --- and t tell '4 ° erer on the King\ Queen side was . ts recorded in It "wtorir who bad i 3 112 I'l ero.lihilst only a trio of . his opponent, .1 \V Jolley scored 21 'zilZillt tall. ir -- • 41g IL - "cad tram won easily by 103 points thus e agglegate win to 139 points. Tla--- ebaaef ,ife.,:a i lqb 2v .1 UN 10It CONSERVATIVES. men visited the Tilela talked of , __,_ y'elktg R'"Ll on Saturday and having I won ny t bet°re at hurtle they kii'e;r they would have a ial;r7l theta to win on the aggregate. !le ,lieltl4o for 14 1 -‘t 1.4 °P d II f the Conservatives, . ,g e aim , „ elle we or I,e4t2ovtd„.; Aleibourne by five points, the game has had been NO tht•:_°`,,er 29 ends. R Knight who much for,,yre•Vions week by four points showed trk ilhlb. 'l,eod disposed of H Bennett (Conservatives)but 17 •'° ne "nuett led f the 14th to 25th ends h elitk rt ),,, , , ..., . from e elt,i". 0 • '-l&amp;lit t‘night made the scares sequal with end Ills „, by IZ,rung a couple of points on the next e N.. ,!16arhext le, 1,7 ht on ett. 8 Bennett reduced the lead to one .. Ittu.-at Via and at the 30th end the score was 19-..,-u i 1 , 'C-night got in a good single near the and won by a couple of points. Ntkitli (0 ---- °lll3' ('1 a b"'eterY) was in his usual good form . and eNest, t -roadhurst b `l.-15 The Conservatives kn. et) t'llr indi • y - .defeats were so ,letit, d th_ yidual games but the the final mothr? to, 47 .i. 5 of such greatness that. ~. tlnd m e Ceraetery team was only 13 points. It v•a3'ed a good game and ran up • very close</p>
        <p>IL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>the score was 14-6 in favour of Cooper. Before the wireman could get his one for game Williams had run his score to thirteen although he bad only one double to his credit in the whole of the game.</p>
        <p>A similar game between P Phillips (ttylands) and J Cartwright resulted in a two point win for the wireman after 20 ends had been contested. Phillips had the best of the first portion of the game, but towards the finish Cartwright showed very good form and at the sixteenth end led by 13-9. 113wever, this proved his last score, and Phillips took his total to fifteen in the next four lengths with two doubles and two singles.</p>
        <p>When the second players took the green Fletcher's had a lead of a dozen points and R Price (Fletcher's) disposed of T Platt by 15-12, thus making the lead 15. Rylands gained eleven points on the next game but the lead after the ninth game was still eleven points for Fletchers.</p>
        <p>The rival captains, T Taylor (Rylands) and J Ellison were both in fine form, and the game between them was a most exciting one for the Rylands' captain only lost by a trio of points. pe scores were nearly even up to the 19th end when Ellison led by 13-12. This proved to be Taylor's last score and with two singles Ellison was "borne."</p>
        <p>Fletcher's gained a couple o f two point victories in the last games, T Bridge defeating J Thompson 15-13 and F Henderson disposing of W Carter by a similar majority.</p>
        <p>Fletchers, therefore enter the second round with a great deal of hope for having defeated Rylands by 18 points is an achievement worthy of all praise and should give the players ,encouragement in future encounters.</p>
        <p>Orford Shed can in no way consider themselves badly handicapped for on Saturday they received 35 points whilst their opponents (Crosflelds) were only conceded fifteen points.</p>
        <p>Aftera tight game E Wilcox (Crocfields) disposed of M. Cunningham by 15-10 in eighteen ends and this proved the commencement of a long list of wins for the soipery men for in the first seven games no fewer than six men were victorious. D Higham beat J Povey by five points after 18 end. W Andrews (captain, Orford) showed splendid bowling and defeated W Lawton, jun, by 15-3 after 19 ends. This proved to be the only win recorded for Orford on the first series of players and had the latter part of the team not showed exceptionally good form the result would probably have been recorded in a different manner.</p>
        <p>A good game was witnessed when .1 Daviss (Crosfields) gained a four pint victory over W Skelhorne. Skelhorne 14ad the best of the first portion of the game and at the ninth end he led by 8-4. Davies, however, fund his length and at the 16th end he had a lead of 13-8. Skelhorne added three points in the next two ends, the score being 13-11 in favour of Davies. With a good double score Davies won by four points.</p>
        <p>After having led by 6-1, 7-2, 9-0, and 10-8, W Higham eventually lost to T Latherland by three points. A keen strnggle wig anticioued between J Jefferson (Crogleld's) end T Percival and the result, a one point victory for Crosfleld's man, shows .hat there was no big difference in the form of the p layers. Percival had a lead of 14-9 at the 21st end but this proved to be his last score and Jefferson got two good doubles and two singles in and won by the odd chalk.</p>
        <p>The same between J Middleton (Crostlelds captain) and J Gilbert was of a similar character, although in this case the victory of one point was in favour of the Orford man. The remaining gArnei were of an inter- esting character and the Orford men Won by 22 points.</p>
        <p>Despite the fact that they were only handicapped at 35 points to their opponents receiving 70, the Rich- mond's team bed little difficulty ia beating the Guest Bros. team by 15 points.</p>
        <p>The stovemen were only beaten on two individusl games these being the ones in which J Allender was beaten by the captain of the opposing team, J Hardman by 15-0 and the one in watch W Birchill lost to J Roberts with only nine points on his card. Six of the twelve games were easy wins for Richmonds, and there were Gaily a few of Guest's men who ran up anything like a show. It Bennett (Richmonds) had all his work cut out to dispose of W Hankey for the Guests man showed good form and bowled well. Hankey led by 12-7 at the 14th end and at the 19th end the game stood at 14-13 in his favour. Bennett, how- ever, got his measure and added two chalks in the next length and thus won by the odd one.</p>
        <p>One of the most remarkable games of ae afternoon was the one in in which W D quail (Guests) lost to It Gallop by the odd point. Dsgnall had scored eleven to his opponent two at the 1201 end but soon after he had added two points Gallop ran the game to 13-14 and gained:the last two chalks on the.22ad end and won by a single point.</p>
        <p>The last game vr,s decided in favour of Guests, J Roberts defeating W Birchen by 15-9 after 20 ends. Btrchall looked like winding at the commencement of the game for he soon ran the score to 6-1 in his favour. However, with singles at the 11th, 15th and 17th ends he brought his final score to nine, Roberts winning by six points.</p>
        <p>With such a victory against a large handicap, Rich- monde should do wall in future encounters and given a fair share of luck, they should finish very near the top.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Peter Peacock Challenge Cup.</head>
        <p>Peter Peacock Challenge Cup.</p>
        <p>No happier idea for the encouragement of snort and friendly intercourse of the party workers has ever been carried into effect than this competition which increases in popularity as the weeks go by. The bowlers have been loud in their commendation of the thoughtfulness of Mr. Peacock in presenting such hand- some trophies and the representatives of the various wards are putting forth the most strenuous efforts to become the first holders. Saturday was an exception- ally interesting afternoon as Mr. A. H. Crosfield and a large number of the party leaders visited the green and addressed words of encouragement to the com- petitors. Mr. Crosfield had an extremely hearty reception and delivered a characteristic address on the advantages of these outdoor recreations in which he is such a firm believer and which he has done so much to further in Wariington.</p>
        <p>-- Four interesting matches in connection with the second round of this competition were played and although the green was running rather heavy some good bowling was witnessed.</p>
        <p>-- The first match between Howley D and Orford A was a very exciting one for the Orford men only won by a few points. There were some very sweeping victories on either side and after eight games had been played the Orford men were 33 points ahead of their opponents. However, in the last four games the Howleyites gained 26 points over their opponents and the Orford team were only seven points to the good on the game.</p>
        <p>Armitage (Orford) was responsible for some beauti- ful bowling and disposed of Hatton by 13.6. Ttie next game was nothing less than a farce for Parsons (Howley) beat Hares by 13-0 having scored six doubles and a single in seven ends. J Berry (Howley) lost to F Brandwood by five points after 14 ends. Tne game between Derbyshire and Fogg (Orford) was the closest of the match and that is not saying much for the Orford man gained five points for his side in the encounter. Derbyshire was on good land in the first lengths and at the sixth end led by 6-2. Fogg soon equalised the scores and at the 12th end the game was 9 each. Two doubles and one single in the next three lengths gained for Fogg his majority, and he won by 13-9.</p>
        <p>The other games were of a less interesting character and 13-4, 13-2, 13-3, and 13-0 wins for either side were prominent until the finish.</p>
        <p>St. Austins A gained a fine 18 point victory over their rivals St. Austins D. There were only six individual games played and Cotterill (D) led off well for his side by defeating Houghton by 13-7 after 13 ends. In the next game, however, F Gray (A) after having led by 10-2 finally won by 13-6 after 15 ends had been played. The best game of the bunch was between H Gray (A) and W J Payne who eventually won by the odd chalk. Gray was on good land at the commencement and after ten ends led by 9-5. Payne made the scores equal with 9 in three length and again at the 17th end the score was 11 each. Gray led by the odd point on the next end but on the 19th length Payne got a good couple of woods in and won 13-12.</p>
        <p>Griffiths (A) had only three points to his credit whilst his opponent Spillsbury had scored 13 in twelve ends. Bennett suffered a similar defeat at the bands of Shaw (D) and Elwell (D) was disposed of by Timmins to the tune of 13-1.</p>
        <p>St. John's D did well in their match with Whitecross A for they won by 41 points. Wooley (St John's) put up a good fight with Thomas but, however, his efforts were not altogether successful and he came off the green with only nine points to his credit.</p>
        <p>-- Davies (Whitecross) pained aOl3O point victory over Bennett after 16 ends. The players kept together from the first and at the 15th end Bennett led by 12-11. Davies however, found the jack with a couple and won 13-12. 'ln the later stages of the match Howard (Whitecross) lost to Dale by a similar majority. He had every appearance of being the winning man at the 12th end when be led by 10-4 but Dale used his know- ledge of the land to the best advantage and won on the 20th end.</p>
        <p>-- One of the remaining games is worthy of mention and that is the one in which Budge (Whitecross) lost to Shakeshaft by the odd chalk after 20 ends. The game was of a touch and go character all through and at the 19th end the score was 12 each. Sbakeshaft however,</p>
        <p>got the deciding wood on the next length. In the last game Alcock (St John's) gained a magnificent twelve point victory over Austin at the ninth end and the St John's team now enter the third round with every feeling of confidence although they will have no sweet thing on for they are drawn with Whitecross F or Latchford A.</p>
        <p>The Juniors were very poorly represented in the match between junior Liberals and Lat-Thfor B and the Latchford men gained an easy 68 point win. If the other players had come up to the example set by the first junior Taylor, the score would have been entirely different for he disposed of J Cliff by 13-7 after fourteen ends had been contested. In the next game W T Eccles (juniors) disposed of Cox by 13-2 after only nine ends. Delaney (juniors) was the first man to lose for his side and James had little difficulty in winning by seven points. Bradshaw (Latchford) beat Woodhouse by 13-2 and Booth (juniors) had a blank sheet in his encounter with Dawson who had scored his thirteen in seven ends.</p>
        <p>These were the only cards returned in this match six games being walk-overs for the Latchford men.</p>
        <p>There are some good draws to be decided to-day both in the second and third round and a good attendance on the Bank Quay Green is expected.</p>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH MEN'S BOWLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The opsning main of the above club took place on the Rectory green on Saturday. There was a good entry of members, and some very keen games played, especially in the semi-finals and final. lbe final game between Messrs Walter Dennett and T J Hopkins was expected to provide some excitement for their less fortunate members, but Dennett proved in rare form,and Hopkins unable to maintain his earlier performances, was dis- aprointing, being beaten somewhat easily 15-7. The prizes were very kindly distributed by Mr R W Antwis s follows :-Ist, Walter Dennett ; 2nd, T J Hopkins ; Prd, J R minim ; 4th, Wm Dennett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CHESHIRE LINES BOWLERS PICNIC.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE LINES BOWLERS PICNIC.</p>
        <p>The members of the Cheshire Linea General Stores Staff held their first annual picnic and bowling main at the Bridge Inn, Gateacre, on Saturday last, and after some very exciting games the result as as follows:- 1, P Sinclair; 2, J Gleave ; 3, T Phair ; A G Warhurst ; 5, W H Bell ; 6. E G Hughes ; 7, E H Hackett ; 8, J Forster ; 9, J Whittaker ; 10,A Rathmill. After the main the parry were kindly entertained to a substantial tea by Mr. S. Saxon Barton. stores superin- tendent, which was admirably served by Mr J Wilson, jun.,anu his genial staff of waiters. After tea a smoker was held in the large pavilion adjoining the green and some capital songs were rehdered by Messrs Arber, Forster, McKay, Crozier and Higham. The party arrived back at Warrington about 10 o'clock after having a most enjoyable outing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>CORPOR kTION EMPLOYEES AT BOWLS.</head>
        <p>CORPOR kTION EMPLOYEES AT BOWLS.</p>
        <p>The fourth annual picnic and bowling main promoted under the auspices of the Corporation Gas Works Fitting Department took place on Saturday, the venue being the Swan Hotel, Bollington. The journey was made by 'bus,the party leaving the works at 1.30,and the drive, favoured by perfect weather, proved most enjoy- able. Bollington was reached in good time and the main was commenced at 3.30. The preliminary rounris having been fought out, an adjournment was made to the hotel, where an excellent knife an. fork tea was provided by the host and hostess (Mr and Mrs Cowen). Returning to the green, the final rounds were decided in which some excellent bowling was seen. The lest eight of the prize winners were :-1, A Mather ; 2, F Todd ; 3, T Johnson ; 4, J Peers ; 5, J Ward ; 6, J Irwin ; 7, E Byrne ; 8, G Warburton. The committee who had made the excellent arrangements for the party, which numbered about 40, were Messrs J Watson, D M Barker, 10 Todd, W H Knight, El Lea, and A E Clough.</p>
        <p>TRAGIC INCIDENT ON A BOWLING GREEN. A tragic incident mlrked the match between Lanca- shire and Cheshire in the British Amateur Parks Association at Stamford Park, Altrincham, on Saturday. Mr. J. Greetthalgh, vice-chairman and captain of Lancashire, who had been officiating as one of the referees, prior to playing in his heat, was suddenly taken ill shortly ilfore six o'clock and died on the green before the arrival of Dr. Hickey. The sad event cast a gloom over the prr feed- ings and the remainder of the players were at once Withdrawn from the green and the match abendoned. Mr Greenhalgh was about to measure the game in which be was acting as referee, when he fell forward on his lace on the green. He was assisted to a seat, and as his condition appeared to be serious Dr Hickey was sent for. Death however, took place before his arrival The body was conveyed to the mortuary in the police ambulance. The deceased was a prominent member of the Albert Park team when they won the Salford championship in 1910. His home was at 34, Clarence- street, Lower Broughton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CRICKET NOTES,</head>
        <p>CRICKET NOTES,</p>
        <p>, Warrington may justly plume themselves on their fine performance on Saturday when they inflicted a drubbing on such formidable opponents as Prescot, The home eleven have got into the winning way and their notable achievements of the last two week-ends hare perceptibly revived the spirits of their supporters. , Last week we bad to accord a special rueed of praise to the Rev. H. Gray for his brilliant 1 )wling, and in the match with Prescot he was again chiefly instru- mental in bringing about the downfall of the visitors and the triumph of his side. The pitch was just to his liking and he bowled with such sureness and precision that 8 wickets fell at the small cost of 25 runs. The L.,:i?,ot batting was woefully weak and hardly to beer artred with Warrington's standard. In the field the War- ringtonians did keen work and no chances were allow d to go d-begging. J Callon batted splendidly for his OS which were rapidly compiled by free hitting. He gave several chances but, luckily for him, the srsitors were not sufficiently wide awake to take them. The Prescot bowling was good but the Warrington wielders of the willow more than held their own and put together the satisfactory total of 133 for 6 wickets, as compared with the visitors' 71. B Silcock was early at the wicket and this seemed to give him more con- fidence, his innings realizing a'useful 18. S Partridge also did well for 14. The decisive reverse evidently took the Prescot contingent by surprise and they did not conceal their disappointment.</p>
        <p>Warrington 2nd were weak in the fielding depart- ment and allowed Prescot 2nd to amass 233 for nine wickets, R. Bircball heading the scores with GS. To this the Warringtonians replied with 161 for four wickets, J Bradburn maintaining the brilliant form which has characterised his play this season and knocking up 67. Two useful not out innings were contributed by W V Turton and H Dplan whose scores stood at 30 and 24 respectively.</p>
        <p>To-day's fixtures:— Warrington v. St. Helens. S. Helens 2nd v. Warrington 2nd. Monday : Manchester v. Warrington at Old Trafford.</p>
        <p>The tit-bit of the Warrington and District League competition was the keen fight between Sankey hnd Warrington Conservatives the outcome ot which was that the champions sustained their first reverse of the season by 6 runs. Sankey having first knock, amassed a capital 84, thanks mainly to a brilliant second wicket stand by Porter (33) and T Whitfield (12). Hunter took seven wickets for the Conservatives. The champions opened disastrously, losing 4 wickets for 3 runs, but Green (32) and Constable (23) put a brighter aspect on the game, which had an exciting finish.</p>
        <p>Another keen encounter was that between Cairo-street Unitarians and Whitecross,the wire men being accounted for by a majority of 6. The best batting performance was by W W Hill, who made a fine 36 for Whitecross. The Rev Dr Mellor is an active member of the Cairo- street team, and on Saturday he did useful work with the ball.</p>
        <p>-- Rylands' Recreation had not much difficulty in dis- posing of Wilderspool, whose efforts realised 55 as com- pared with the wire eleven's 81. Briscoe made the highest score of the match, with 23 for Wilderspool, while Renwick knocked up 15 for the winners. Waywell again wrought great havoc with the ball, and'his services in this depertment tire invaluable to.Rylands's.</p>
        <p>Orford recorded a victory at the expense of St. Helens Cable Company by a majority of 23. The scoring on the whole was poor, F Tanner (17) being the most successful for Orford, while J Raynor (16) headed the list for the winners.</p>
        <p>The League table to-date is as follows The following is the League table to date : P. W. L. D. Pts. Sankey Warrington Conservatives 5 ... 3 ... 1 ... 1 ... 7 St Helens Cable VVhitecross Cairo-street Orford Rylands Walkers Wilderspool</p>
        <p>T,-day's League fixtures are : Walkers v Whitecross Isankev v St Helens Cable Co. Orford v Warrington Conservatives Cairo-street v Wilderspool</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Institute proved superior to Latchford Park by a margin of 35, C Harrison's bowling, which accounted for 6 wickefa for 13 runs, being the chief factor in the victory. T A Knight (16) and %V Knight (15) gave the leading batting displays.</p>
      </div>
