      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ASSOCIATION</p>
        <p>(N.A. CENTRE). There was a great falling off of anglers last week-end, the majority finished up after the holidays until open- ing season. However, those who stayed over the week end were rewarded by good catches of eels and jack,but these who only visited the different streams during Sunday were disappointed with their catches. They fully expected bags of eels, anything from three to six pounds, after the rain we had during the wtsk. The streams certainly looked all the better for it and the colour of the viatlr was just the right thing that was wanted, but in spite of everything in the favour of the angler the fishing was not as expected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>GOWY. . _</head>
        <p>THE GOWY. . _</p>
        <p>Fewer anglers visited here than for several weeks past. Those who made the journey on Sunday hal a very unpleasant day, rain falling soon after noon until train time, and the angling was not up to expectations. Members were ready for the return journey long before the time. One eel was taken well over 11b. This is something out of the ordinary, especially for the Gowy. Jack and trout were not to be had in spite of the water looking better than it has done since the close season, ands several members paid it their last visit for some weeks to come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>TIIE DEE.</head>
        <p>TIIE DEE.</p>
        <p>Chester WHS visited again by one or two members, but they report that the tide was about three feet too high for angling and the catches made were caught when the tide was receding. One member fished here in the early part of the morning and then took train to Mickle Trafford and fished the Gowy for the remainder of the day, returning home with a fair bag of eels. We hear that a couple of flounder were taken but the member thought them too small to weigh in. However there are none marked up yet, and there is only this week end to try for the special prize.</p>
        <p>LONG BA.NN. Several members visited here and by reports to hand they have had fair sport with the eels several return- ing with more than half a dozen and ;he least of them turning the scale at 40z. Norton will be the place most favoured when open season arrives, it being close to home, and a trial here will give members a good idea how the roach have fared during the close season. If it suits the new members there will be fine competition this season for the £2 specials that are offered. Club night Monday June 10th at 8 o'clock, this being last night for cards to be taken out before the opening season June 15th. A.S.</p>
      </div>
