      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY JUNE 8, 1912.</p>
        <p>The result ot the North</p>
        <p>THE OLD West Norfolk election CONSERVATIVE, has caused some mem-</p>
        <p>bers of the Conser- vative party to think furiously. One of these calling himself" An Old Conservative," sets forth his views in one of the leading news- papers of the party. He is of opinion that the shelving of the Tariff Reform question is a bad blunder. We should be much more inclined to agree with him if the Conser- vatives in North-West Norfolk had not so greatly increased their poll. It is highly improbable that they could have polled another vote under any circumstances. The idea that some Conservative electors re- frained from voting with their party, or voted on the other side, because Tariff Reform was not placed among the vital issues, is clearly one that will not bear examination. The Conservatives of North- West Norfolk may be eager Tariff Reformers; but the figures of the election entirely fail to support that view. The party polled its highest possible, and the mere observer must conclude from the fact that if the question of Tariff Reform influenced the result of the election in any degree, the avoidance of the subject by the Conservative Candidate removed an obstacle from the path of those Unionists who are still Free Traders.</p>
        <p>The whole of the contentions of the Old Conservative are based on the presumption that the party might even have won the seat if a different programme had been placed before the electors. .This reasoning may be set aside as being obviously without founda- tion, but it is nevertheless deeply interesting to note what sort of policy for the moment would,in the opinion of the Old Conservative, have led to MR. HEMMERDE'S defeat. We are assured that the absence of any con- structive Conservative programme is a defect which no beating of the orange drum in England or railing against "advancing Socialism " will remedy. Then follows the surprising statemeut that the ordinary British elector ( the referencp we suppose, being in this instance to the Conservative elector ) does not care twopence whether Ireland has Home Rule or not. Nor, we are assured, is Welsh Disestablishment a burning question among thousands of Churchmen who have lived to see the remarkably vigorous life of the Disestablished Protestant Church in Ireland. What is needed, says the Old Conservative, is some sign that if the Conservatives came into power they will do something sane and effectual to grapple with problems which demand solution. The last Government is blamed for not dealing with Old Age Pensions, for not passing an Insurance Act, and for not having reformed the House of Lords and the poor law. It ought, that is to say, in the interests of self-preservation, to have done just those things which, according to MR JODRELL, the electors of North-west Norfolk protested against by their votes. -</p>
        <p>TA. sane- and effectual policy, it seems, would be something corresponding to what MR. DISRAELI described when he said that SIR ROBERT PEEL found the Whigs bathing and ran away with their clothes. The Conservatives are asked whether they are too hidebound and insular to learn a lesson from the Conservatives of Belgium, who had the sagacity to extract from the Socialist programme all the items worthy of attention, adopt them, carry them out on Conservative and religious principles and leave the foes of religious and civil order with only their visionary theories to work upon. "Let English Conservatives adopt this line with Labour and social questions, and there will be no more unsatisfactory North-West Norfolk elections for many years to come." We wonder what the Old Conservative considers Conservatism to be.</p>
        <p>If he does not suddenly MR F. E. SMITH develop into a Socialist —LIBERAL there are strong possi- bilities that Mr. F. E. SMITH will die a Liberal or an advanced Progressive of some sort. Every now and then his convictions seem to thrust aside his politics, as in the remarkable article from his pen which appears in the "Oxford and Cambridge Review" for this month. He admits frankly that there is a deep and wide fissure in the ranks of Toryism. There is the section which stands for "a policy of negation and inactivity "and there is a growing section of the party which" finds itself in acute disagreement with these views." "The growing discontent of the working classes cannot be dismissed with a few punitive recommendations." That discontent has been increased by the pread of ostenta- tious luxury, and 'motor-cars have manu- factured more Socialists than all the eloquence of Mr. Keir Hardie and Mr. Lansbury." Mr. Smith draws atttention to the grave facts revealed by Mr. Booth and Mr. Rowntree as to the condition of the people, and, while doing lip service to Tariff Reform, bluntly admits that he has never beleived that remedy would end the evils. He does not indicate what are the remedies he looks for, but he insists that the Unionist Party must stand for social reform, that it must be prepared to spend money, and that money must come from " that luxurious class " which " lives entirely for pleasure." Meanwhile, Mr. Lloyd George is going a bit ahead of Mr. Smith with a policy of extracting more and more from the pockets of "that luxurious class."</p>
      </div>
