      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>[ES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>[ES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The quiet little hamlet of Hatton is brought into prominence this week, through the retirement of Rev. T. H. Hunt, an ex-president of the Primitive Methodist Connexion, and a native of the village, who had been in the ministry for half a century.</p>
        <p>A Conservative of the old school, and of the type which manufactures Socialists by the thousand is Dr. Harrison, J.P., of Garstang. He has a firm opinion about workingmen and their proper sphere in life, and according to a report of a speech made by him on Wednes- day in reference to the London strike "he would employ the armed forces of the Crown to shoot down the peaceful picketers like dogs." Pleasant person to be a J.P. isn't he? * * * _ _</p>
        <p>At the request of the committee of the Bold Street Cripples' Guild, Councillor Bellian has agreed to organise another motor trip for all the crippled children in the town. The run will take place on Thursday afternoon, June 20th, to Oulton Park, the seat of Sir Philip Egerton. At least 40 cars will be re- quired and Councillor Bellian appeals for the co-operation of owners either in lending cars or sending subscriptions for the hire of them. The outing gives an immense amount of pleasure to a most unfortunate and deserving class, many of whom would never see the country side but for this kindly annual effort in their interest. * *</p>
        <p>Preparations for the administration of the Insurance Act in this district are proceeding apace. The Insurance Commissioners have decided that a provisional or temporary committee should be formed in each locality to take the necessary steps for putting the Act into operation. The committee in Warrington will consist of forty members of whom the Corporation will nominate eight, two being ladies. The Friendly Societies and other organizations of insured persons will of course, be represented. Warrington will be the centre of the North- Western division of England, comprising the whole of Lancashire and Cheshire, under part 11. (unemployment) of the Act. Temporary offices have already been secured in Bank Chambers, Cairo Street and eventually the large building in Bridge Street, formerly occupied by Messrs Greenhalgh and Co Ltd, will be made the headquarters.</p>
        <p>The statue of King Charles I. has long been threatened with removal from its present position at Charing Cross, and this, it seems, is shortly to be carried out. Every year, on January 30th, it is the centre-piece of a quiet festival, and is hung with floral offerings, in memory of the "Martyr King." The statue has a remarkable history, and we may be sure that wherever it may go, it will continue to be sacred to the Jacobites in the annual celebration of their political creed. It is generally regarded as one of the finest equestrian statues in London. Cast in 1633 it had not been erected when the Civil War broke out, and was sold by Parliament to a brazier named John Rivett, to be melted down. This he pretended he had done, and sold souvenirs to both friends and foes of the Stuarts. But all the time he had kept the statue intact, and after the Restoration it was produced uninjured, and erected in its present position in 1674. ce *</p>
        <p>U. P. R. stands for Unionist Private Register, an example of political jobbery of the worst kind. A circular to Cornish Tories points out the advantages of the Private Register, entry to which costs only " the trifling sum of fid." Among the advantages which are cooly statt.d are: "Your name in the Unionist private register," says the circular in large print, "is a fortification, not only of your political position, but of your business and private life." The registry is to be published only to avowed Unionists, "so that every member of the party may see at a glance who are and who are not his political colleagues. This information will be of immense value to you, not only politically but in your business and private life." Could anything exceed the above for political cheek and chicanery ? What it all means is : tell us you are a Unionist, and we will make it worth your while to remain one ! * *</p>
        <p>Speaking at Cardiff the other day, Mr. F. E. Smith, said "there is not and there never will be a question of the Unionist Party dropping Tariff Reform." Neverthe- less, Unionists are pretty generally cold- shouldering the fiscal policy at by-elections. Tariff Reformers are decidedly disgruntled at the strategy which led to the dropping of Tariff Reform in North-west Norfolk. The Pall .Mall Gazette sees in the reduced Liberal majority only " a very sober achieve- ment " for Unionism. The badness of the result is attributed to the veto on Tariff Reform. "We shall look to the directors of Unionist organisation for some account of this suicidal strategy. Was it the outcome of local aberration, or was it followed with the cognisance or at the instance of headquarters. It is a matter upon which every member of the partyi,has a right to be informed, for every member of thp party has suffered by it. Such midsummer madness demands the most searching investigation!' The Gazette's wail notwithstanding, Tory electioneers will continue to fight shy of Tariff Reform wherever they find the public tired of it— a pretty general experience.</p>
        <p>I If there be anything in the law of averages Mr. Hemmerde, the victor of North-West Norfolk, should be assured of a long and unmolested tenure of his seat, for he has had to fight no fewer than seven contests in twelve years. There can be very few parallels to such an experience, though, on the other side of the House, Mr. Joynson Hicks had five fight§ in the years between 1900 and 1911. Other members who have had varied experiences of the electorate are Mr. Walter Long and Lord Charles Beres- ford. Mr. Long has sat for six and Lord Charles for five different divisions. * * We understand that a number of the friends of the Rev. John Yonge, outside his own Church, are desirous of showing their appreciation of the worth and public work of the rev, gentleman during the thirty-five years he has been a citizen of Warrington. An opportunity is given to all who desire to share in this appreciation by the opening of a fund of which Mr. F. A. Harris, Whitefield- road, Stockton Heath, is the treasurer. Any amount, however small, will be acknow- ledged by Mr. Harris. The Mayor, Dr. Joseph, will present to Mr. Yonge, in the name of the citizens of Warrington, the pro- ceeds of this effort on Tuesday, the 18th inst., at the gathering to be ,held in Wycliffe Church on that date. All intending contributors should therefore send in their amounts without delay. * * There are mysteries in the transmission of sound that have not yet been fathomed. For example, the guns of the " Orion " are said to have been heard ninety-seven miles away, though inaudible to those much nearer. And it is on record that the reverberation ot the battle of Waterloo reached many places in Kent, though General Colville and his detach- ment heard nothing of it a dozen miles away. Whistles, hoots, syrens, and noises of all kinds have been tested by the Trinity House in order to find a noise which will be infallibly heard for a certain distance. In vain. They may all be heard for a dozen miles and inaudible at half a mile. Many of the disasters about our coasts are doubtless due to that mysterious "soundless zone " in which the human ear cannot catch the warn- ing. But no one has yet 'discovered the reason of that deaf spot or prophesied its varying locality. * * 0 Gradpally the second class is being abolished on the railways. The London, Brighton, and South Coast is the latest to announce that it will get rid of the inter- mediate class. Soon it may be expected to disappear the kingdom over. Then we may look for a revival of nomenclature (says the " Westminster Gazette.") The third class, having squeezed out the second, will claim its title. It would be absurd if we preserved the gap. All who now travel third will take a step in the social scale and become second- class passengers. The name will change, although the thing will be the same. We are recognising on the railways not only the power but the upward tendency o! democracy. It may even be that by the time we are ready for the alteration of name the first class itself will be tottering to decay. We can see the process at work on the underground railways of London, and in twenty years peer and clerk may go in the same carriage the country over, just as they now make their daily journeys together to the City in the Tube.</p>
        <p>General J. S. Cowans has vacated the office of Director General of the Territorial Force. He is succeeded by, Major-General E. G. Bethune, an officer who has made himself very popular in Lancashire while command- ing the West Lancashire Territorial Division and is well-known to Warrington Territorials This is the first appointment that General Bethune has had at Army Headquarters. He is possessed of a genius all his own, and no officer holding command in South Africa so endeared himself to Colonial auxiliaries as did this remarkable man. Such Colonials (says a correspondent of the "Manchester Guardian ") as came under his ,command would do anything for him or go anywhere with him. He has a great opportunity in his new appointment as chief administrator of the Territorial Force. The Force itself is at the present moment passing through a crucial stage. Much wilhdepend upon the manner in which it is handled in the next two years as to whether it will fulfil the high hopes that Lord Haldane has built upon it; or whether it will succumb to the campaign that so many Unionist agencies have directed against it.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Personal. Mr. A. F. Corney, has this week taken the Degree of M.A. at Oxford.</p>
        <p>Junior Liberal Association. A meeting of this association will be held on Wednesday evening. Business important.</p>
        <p>The Rey. John Yonge. At an open meeting of Wycliffe P.S.A. Society to-morrow afternoon, a presentation will be made to the Rev. John Yonge on his retirement from the presidency.</p>
        <p>Open-Air Polities. Mr. T. H. Garside, of Manchester' will be the principal speaker at the Progressive Labour League's open-air meeting at Bridge Foot. on Monday evening. THE B.V.C. VACUUM CLEANER is the easiest to work and gives the best results. Price, 375. 6d. and 848.— Sole agent : J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger 51 to 55, Bridge Street, Warriogton.—Advt. Death of Mrs John Appleton. The death has occured at 4, Lucia Road, Egremont, Cheshire, of Mrs Appleton, widow of the late Mr. John Appleton, a former well-known builder in Warrington. The deceased lady, who was 68 years of age, was highly respected by'a large circle of Warringtonians. Death on the Station. A tragic incident occurred at Bank Quay Station on Monday afternoon when a soldier of the Special Reserve died on the platform. He was on his way with others to camp for training. Bakery Fire.</p>
        <p>A fire broke out in the bakehouse of Messrs. Jamas Strange and Sons, Knutsford-road, shortly before four o'clock on Tuesday afternoon. It originated ueflr a gas engine, owing to a leakage in the main gas pipe. . The escaping gas caught fire and threatened to destroy the buildings. The fire brigade were sum- moned and were quickly on the scene under the com- mand of Superintendent Turner. In about half an hoar the flames were subdued. The damage, which is considersble, is covered by insurance. " PREMIER " CYCLRS.—Special lines at £5 10s. or with 3 speed gear, £6 153.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. " A Dangerous Spot." Christ Church Magazine says :—Our sincere sympathy goes out to the parents and relations of the two little girls—Bessie Speed and Gertrude Hackett— who were drowned in the Ship Canal on Monday, ' May 13th. They were school and play mates in life and "in their death they were not divided." The particular spot on the bank of the Canal at which these children lost their lives has for many years bad a peculiar attraction to the boys and girls of the neighbourhood. There are here what are euphemis- tically called " caves "—really indentations in the red sandstone the result, we suppose, of the wash caused by passing steamers. It is an innocent looking place, but is in reality a dangerous spot. We are glad to see that the Ship Canal Company have promptly acted upon the recommendation of the jury at the recent Inquest to place a fence round this particular point of the canal bank.</p>
        <p>Palace and Hippodrome. An exceptionally exceptionally strong programme will be mitted next week. The bill is headed by Edward D'a Ford, who will introduce his latest chorus crazes, Charles Mathews, the champion jumper of the werl.o Edwin Lawrence will no doubt highly amuse151 I "Scraggy, the scout," and the Five Vernons will selections on the Xylophone. Ford and IValtoo wlr appear as vocalists and dancers and a host a ethe attractions will also be presented. Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd. The annual outing promoted by theofficestag etf the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., t,°°„ - place on Saturday, hnd took the form cf a most 01°J able drive to Delamere Forest by way of Frodshael; Hatchmere and Sandicvay. Mr. Slack (director secretary) accompanied the party, Mr. Rae&amp;mto (managing director) being unable to attend, owing, indisposition. A stay was made at the Blue le Hotel, Sandiway, where a bowling main was held, party afterwards sitting down to an excellent 10' and fork tea, after which billiards was indulged ie• The Picturedr3me. The usual standard of excellence in the Picturedrome entertainments has been well maintained througbetl, the week and the pictures "The girl and her trot and the great spectacular production Cardinal Wolsey " have each proved highly popular. The eve; enterprising management have secured one of the fe films of the great Derby for next week and the pictury will prove acceptable not only to the sportisgt fraternity but to the wider public who take an Intel' in the classic event. The chief film on Monday el. be "His Terrible Lesson" and for Thursday and remainder of the week "A. Mexican courtahip °ll occupy the premier position. LAWN MOWERS ground by special machinery. Se „ your machines now before the rush.—.J. S. CiAttLAN' 51, Bridge at. Warrington.—Advt. The Territorials.</p>
        <p>The recently formed drum and fife band of the -It' Battalion P. W. V. (South Lancs. Regt.) will paLacill at 8-45 p.m. on Friday next in front of the Town where the " First Post" will be played. Tbey then march along the following route: Crosfiei Street, Tanner's Lane, Orford Lane, Battersby Church Street, Mersey Street, Bridge Street. Sallied Street, finishing in front of the Town Hall and sOOl3l, ing the "Last Post." Lieut.-Colonel B. Fairclouq, the commanding officer, has presented the band another guards' pattern side drum, and Mr. Roger.' Parr has given a sergeant-drummer's sash, which 15.1 most handsome piece of work. Another valuable is the leopard skin presented by Capt. G. R. CrosEe" Music in the Park. The Borough Prize Brass Band will play the folloo ing programme of music in the Park on Sands', conductor Mr T Griffin :—Hymn" Clifford," G Fras.e march "Nonpareil," C Anderson ; selectl "Rigoletto," Verdi; selection "Faust," Goup°,A fantasia "Operatic Melodies," J A Greenwr selection "William Tell" (Rossini), air by Greenwood; "God save the King." •pol The band will also play the following in the on Thursday, June lath :—March "The Favorite, F Carter; selection "Bouquet of Ballads," E New,,,tl valse "Queen of Erin," S Douglas; selection r,v songs of Old England," E Newton; lancers "o'3l' Hours," E Walton; "God save the King."</p>
        <p>Boy Scouts. A meeting of the Local Association was held at Old Academy during the past -week for the pla of discussing the question of re-organization. -3' B. Starkey presided. An Executive Committee,: elected, and consists of Messrs. J. Fulton Smit', :Leer, J W Welsby, A Range, T Richardsa, Scoutmaster Garner, together with the Hon. Treaelli and Hen. Secretary A Committee of Scoutrosek with Scoutmaster Bennett as Secretary, b°9 formed. Mr C J Newton, of the Athletic Market Place, was appointed Hon Secretary °I.. Association. Various schemes for bringing the obi` of the movement before the public were discussed. combined parade of local Scouts has been tarsi') for Saturday 15th June, A " Rally" of Scoute,';', surrounding towns is 'being organized, and will place towards the end of July. Special Amhulfe Signalling and other competitions will take place' Lancashire Fire Brigades. The fifty-seventh annual meeting of the Lanc,a_, Fire Brigades' Friendly Society was held at SoutbE on Saturday, and was attended by effieera and 41 the number of about 1,000, representing 47 brig,9,43 from Accrington. Bolton, Bootle, Burnley, Chorley, Darwen, Manchester, Rochdale, StalYbri;, Stockport, Warrington, and elsewhere. The W tot., Fire Brigade was not represented, but a deta, ment from Messrs Crosfield's Brigade with Secc Officer Flo,d. in charge attended. Shortly noon the majority of the men paraded in uniforeat the Promenade Recreation ground, and were loge% by the Mayor of Southport ',Mr F G Reynolds)• quently they marched in procession to the Buildings, where the business meeting was The hon. secretary, Mr. Joseph Watson, stated there were now 160 brigades in the society, 156 last year, and 2,521 members, as compared 2,519. New members in 1911 totalled `-)79, and ebtril3 the first five months of the pre.,ent year 126; svrf 224 left the society last year and 114 this yesr. 5 funds at the end of 1911 showed a balance cf being an increase of £565 on the year.</p>
        <p>MOTOR CYCLES.—Agent for " Douglas" it "Prern-00 New Models now in stock. Trials and all inforratiO free.-J.S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warringtoa." Warrington Co-operative Society. • gto' The 206th quarterly meeting of the Warrif3itedr' Equitable Industrial Co-operative Society 1,433,beti: was held in the Co-operative Ball on MondaY, the. about 200 members were present. The report 1°4,3" quarter ending April 23rd, showed sales °'-rlotb'' increase of .£5,069 over the corresponding re,"tioo ' The President, in a brief speech, moved the aa.°rprita,, of the report including the payment of an Ir3—rieo dividend of 3s, 21. in the which was carve '• unanimously. Voting for three members of Cornvit'irea in place of three who are compulsorily' le of according to rule resulted in the electilir. s:r Mrs Mary Roberts, Mr. Sidney Packer and GeV Hasler; there being eight candidates for three Paiod. rhe proposed Benevolent Fund was then disciloso of , and a few questions replied to, and the suggeej 001 the committee was finally carried unanimousi/.0t9 the addition of a clause that all applications for 8' Tjte from the fund must be sent to the Secretary. tivee members committeeers thenof this proceeded isf tuondel,ecatnd m threerr.etnreibebe:rtt:bgtor; on th Rushton and Mr. Seed were appointed, Mr. and Mr. Hibbert being two of the just retired nantee; of the committee and Mr. Seed also an ex-cotn°3 ees IoY man. It was resolved that the grant to the en* i,eine picnic should be increased to 4s. per head, this equal to about £5O as against 4'30 usually goa, of Under the head of any other business the ac.j.' the committee in increasing the grant to the eci ton Horse Parade from 10s. 6d. to 21s. was c°l2 Former Postal Official's Death. oso/ Mr. William Dannett, a former prominent P big official who was highly respected alikpetibibistl; wereresi residence, g hRt oisne colleagues in service and by those of the contact t Gwith pi Latcovett on the 30th ult, at the age of 77 yea