      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE. A WEEK OF OPEN-AIR MEETINGS. LIVELY SCENES AT TOWN'S END.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE. A WEEK OF OPEN-AIR MEETINGS. LIVELY SCENES AT TOWN'S END.</p>
        <p>The local --- advocates of Women's Suffrage have been kking a Special effort to popularise their cause this 1 series of open-air meetings having been held .4_ various parts of the town under the auspices of the 'l'liffartrington branch of the National Union of Women's .bee,..age Societies. On the whole the meetings .have 55.4 successful, the only serious attempt at opposition forthcoing at Town's End on Tuesday evening the speakers were continually interrupted. They t,"'"er, stuck pluckily to their task but were unable - 444 much impression on the crowd. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>OPENING MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE OPENING MEETING.</p>
        <p>The A meeting of the week was held on Monday --io7t7ring at the corner of Thelwall-lane, Latchford, and eocieet.„'ting speeches explaining the projects of rthe Dreaid',.,wore made by prominent members. Mr J Ball Pori e" and was supported by Miss Eskrigg, Miss , At a,nd Mrs Pemberton. were t,o,e outset the CHA.IRMAN said the question they , 1455 mere to the speak upon was whether women were to Thu Parliamentary vote on the same terms ,as men. , f, Particular question was by no means a new one for Presr,,baok as forty years ago it had been promoted and 4 4418‘4 forward by so well-known a writer and so able John Stuart Mill. Since that time there bad the fe steady growth of opinion in favour of granting clueq,Pmehise to women and on several occasions the " 1-:0rae;" had been put to the vote in the House of ,lad ~-°lls, and on two or three occasions the proposal reajo',"esed the second reading. Although they had a tin ta,.43' of votes on those occasions in favour of the cle., uere had not been the driving power of the tietternent behind it which would have enabled the 'e,tpee°, he placed on the Statute Book. They hoped and Ole It`e.d that when the Government brought forward tieB erorm Bill on men's franchise that fair opporuuni- Copa,„`°°ald be given to members of the House of Poeid'h,ens to move an amendment which would make it 'lt Ili.'" for women to have the vote on the same terms !ekt ea' (Hear, hear). His own support of the question Ilibl „Med largely Ob one siinplepoint. It seemed to ea,.,t the claim women made to have the vote was NBOy ' Parcel of the emancipation of women from the l'etni.elisabilities they had been troubled with for many kelp th There was an old idea in the minds of many Iltte,, 4,,t women belonged more or less to an inferior theili-4,1 it had taken a great many years to persuade tieete,, that they must look on the two 4401-"8 of the human race equally before the _, 'uao„ erkt give the women votes to obtain elsoliee of those laws whic