poorer sisters of the community tn'oe wr wanted a voice in the legislation of Iltit'l'trY. They could never hope to have a healthy it th -°ePerous nation unless the home conditions were tg°e4best __ it was because they wanted to see the r'llette - Lions and the home life of the people Igete:,,tnat they were asking for the vote. They had '3ostice on their side and that always won in ulause). Gt. pointed out that they in that society least believe in resorting to violence to were wanting—the vote. Those who had ows and benaved in a very militant en courageous and set an example of a cause but they in that society did not were the right lines upon which to hear and applause.) In political affairs to all parties because they had the sup- cvatives, Liberals and -Labour men, and gaining more support both in the House of • and all ever the country from all political • tien "hat they wanted to see wiped /00 out were the :484 b 8 which existed at the present time ; that a lii.`,o4,.eeanse she happened to be a woman was denied The reason why the Conciliation Bill was . NI tied w fe,.. as that the Irish Nationalists had previously 'N Bill - 114 . I.t, but having regard to the Home Rips l Imo\ the, "4 t 6 'netructed not to vote for the Conciliation hi,... occasion. However, they had a chance in ` t rlici °L an m dment to the Reform Bill to be to th%l sofidh,fore taheen House in the autum, and they could '4 440 ,c,°o the Labour Party to do their best for loo,ssaah They wanted to get as many other members "T'S in favour of granting the franchise to tltlf 0(...t was only by getting a majority of people in tb_Diaian; their measure that they could hope for it to ent"„ through the House of Commons. If the vote 7,ted to women it would not entail any leak ' unties, and it v:as simply because there were ,',.tters in which the voice of women ought to ,r 7. , i Lugo. 43 ,4(ir, y 6 the wer e asking for the vote. Seeing Ivo so"t were so many laws directly affecting the rtieri was only a rig