        <p>_';vo en did how necessary to fight °t;),,thairtirni, , and their forefathers in the past o gh loi,loglin„ --erti ea• It as because they d been f I,' • w I that they ig..1.1/1g so long 41411'erty......14,,en who were fighting. for the same degree symhapathised Ilrktpley ,iXsvote—at the present time. On every Nt ea% aii great deal of labour .9 rest and men i tha ('ataq w. the great industries of unrest country clis- tat.tlttuthith their wages, their hours of live. Those haonsde ll),4 R•eril..9toces under which they :t.'a °.u5.11 thae% t-a_til: g by means of ttheeh'hiteaurcleietaomentary vote of Commons il4siktVattattbto,: "cPose,l ietni toast: ve h Ih's''Bt3' eq.° 48 the Men's conditions were, they improved—(hear,in hear their testa 13aath worse if they had tha gaardeci n cost of fditiolsns ) would hear)—but u %tie, hicrewesrien, hinewethveer,Hwoeursee. The far worse. the tit tn4tl' w,,,„ Ilg numbers the industries 4ad t.tll latj,PaSSing to a great extent out of The hands It L"oot ito toe hands of women and girls. lir ,F)vv'a's biti't that :'as tries were two cases in point and It INtatillcm ln the boot industry d been out ~the ,„41aora w,harehY girls and women could turn e machinery ba Nv-"aa :° tit ban men at less thaa half the w_age. I'4 Vvrtitia Zl.v, ed. Woman labour was a very real and "iltti,,ttrih;;"• She did not know what the back streets nr°ll were like (A voice : " They're did not know what the cottage</p>
        <p>fit 11N.</p>
        <p>'4</p>
        <p>She'</p>
        <p>homes were like (" beautiful ") when she considered the enormously high rate of infantile mortality in the town. Some of the houses and homes in Warrington must be of a very low standard indeed (evoke : "What do you know about them ? ") and they wondered what was the reason for such a high rate. She did not think and could not understand how any women who was a mother of children could allow the slaughter of babies which went on and be content with her own conditions. Many and many a big fortune was being built up on the labour of women, girls and young boys. At this juncture a number of boys led off with comic songs which were at once taken up by their elders and the hub-hub continued until the speaker had to sit down practically exhausted. Miss BnoADBENT said she would remind the Conser- vatives that the best and greatest Conservative leLders were in favour of womens suffrage. (Jeers.) Women claimed their right to have a voice in the government of the country. They were fighting for a great cause— were going to win in spite of disappointments, failure and delay, and they appealed to the publio to help in the great struggle for freedom. They were all members of a great Empire. of which they were justly proud. In their great dominions of Australia ana New Zealand the women had that for which Englishwomen were now asking. It was not merely a political Question. They disliked being classed as criminals and lunatics on the Statute Book simply because they had no vote, and she thought that was a disgrace to their sex. It was said that woman's place was in the home, but the law had come into their homes. They were told by law how they were to educate and feed their children, and they ought to have a voice in saying how those laws should be framed. Their society was a law- abiding one and was worked by constitutional methods, but she did not think the action of the militant organization was as bad as that of inciting soldiers to disobey their orders. Behind Tom Mann, who was sentenced to six months imprisonment, was the Labour Party and the sentence had been reduced to two months whilst the women who had no political party at their backs, were sentenced to nine mouths imprisonment. The great difficult ques- tions of the day needed women to help in their solvency and they were asking that the people should help them to gain the franchise and set them free to give their services to the coun.ry. (Applause). Mrs. Robinson was called upon to speak again by Miss Eskrigg and bravely tried to make herself heard over the tumult but was not successful.</p>
        <p>In concluding the meeting the Chairman said he thought they had had a lively time. (Hear. hear). He believed they would have heard and read sufficient— pamphlets have been distributedithroughout the week— to make them go home and study that great question thoroughly. (Applause).</p>
