      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>GOOD MEETING AT ORFORD.</head>
        <p>GOOD MEETING AT ORFORD.</p>
        <p>Miss Crosfield and the Movement.</p>
        <p>There was a large and attentive gathering at the corner of Longford-street, Orford-lane, on Wednesday evening when many striking speeches were made for the cause.</p>
        <p>Mrs PEMBERTON presided and she was supported by Mrs ltobiuson (Manchester) and Miss Trude Crostield. After having introduced the speakers the chairman called on Miss Crosfield to address the gathering. Miss CROSFIELD said she had been speaking on the subject of Women's Suffrage for the last year or two and had met with a great deal of prejudice and ignorance on the matter. They, the objectors, had said that woman's place was the home and that the women were wanting to take work from the men and give them- selves an undue preference. Those were absolute mis- takes. (Hear, hear.) Woman's place was the home, and the home of all our homes was England. The welfare of the counts) meant just as much to the women as to the men. (Hear, hear.) The women and men were co-partners in the welfare of the whole country andithe housing and sanitary questions as well as the progress of the nation, were just as much women's concerns as the men's. In fact a woman ought to know more about the housing conditions than the man because she was the one who was in the house most. Many of the homes in England to-day were an absolute disgrace to the country. Who could say that slums ought to exist in this, the 20th century ? They ought all, men and women, to put their hands to the wneel and help to do away with the horrid conoitions which existed at the present time. Why was the Labour Party in Parlia- ment absolutely send for the suffrage movement? They were the best friends the women had got. They had realised that if women were sweated to within an inch of their lives and got starvation wages it hurt the men just as much as the women. The most expensive part of the production in a factory was toe wages bill. Eighty-five per cent of the cost of the production of a manufactured article went towards the wages and if a manufacturer saw that be must cheapen the cost of production the wages must feel the reduction first. He could not get the members of the trade unions to do it for less, but if women were starving he could get them to do it at a lower rate. In 1860 there were perhaps 10 per cent of the employees in certain indus- tries and factories who were women, whereas now there were 75 per cent. It was a terrible thing for the women to have stepped in and ousted thousands and thousands of men. The right to work was the right of men and women alike and if the women bad to work to earn their bread no one could blame them for going in, but they could blame the system which allowed women to be absolutely sweated and earn starvation wages, If the women had got the same wages as the men it would have been all well and good, for the same wages would have been going to the home, but bucla, however, was not the case. The average rate of wages for married women in industries a ,c 1 iactories was only 7s 9d per week. What man would look at such a wage? The average rate of wages for girls was Gs 6d per week and the highest wages women got was an average of 14s per week and that was in textile work in Lancashire. They got that because the Trade Unions in the textile works in Lancashire allowed women in their organisa- tions and it was the only Trade Union where women could come in on the same terms as men, and they had got the higher rate of wages. What were the sweating wages ? 'They would work for fourteen hours a day for six days per week—a relative of hers was trying to get the 12-hour shift done away with and an eight hours' shift instituted—and the highest wages they got was Ca per week. Probably the worst case of sweating in the British Isles was in Ireland where the cheap linen came from. It was little wonder it was so cheap. Those women had to make 912 dots, and put them on the handkerchiefs for 31d. There was only one remedy, and that was that they must give to women the same Parliamentary and political protection the men had got. In some respests we were awfully behind the times in England. Norway, Australia, New Zealand and several other countries were coming to the front, for they had granted the franchise to women. In those places the wages of the women had risen to something like that of the men. Talking of chivalry, we were amongst the best nations in the world for chivalry when we understood the case but there was a great deal of ignorance about things. They could not talk of chivalry, as Mr Ellis Griffiths had said,when there were 5,000,000 women earning their own livings when they had not got the vote. They had to compete in the labour market with the men and yet they had no political protection like the men had. If the cry of chivalry was perfectly true the women ought not tcshave to pay taxation. The cry bad been for a long time against taxation without representation but yet the women had to pay just as much as the men. Parliament did not impede men in the faintest degree from taking up any work. All work was open to men but that was not the case with women. (Hear, hear.) Those who were trying to prevent women's suffrage were indirectly responsible for the driving of women to degradation and misery because they were preventing things which might improve the conditions under which the women had to work and live. Many of the greatest thinkers, politicians and writers of the day were in favour of the movement. No one would benefit more than the men themselves if women got the vote, and when the frightful stigma of sweating was removod for ever. The Bible said "Am Imy brother's keeper,'' and it applied with equal force as : "Am Imy sister's keeper ?" (Applause). Mrs. ROBINSON commenced an interesting speech on the question by saying that the member of Parliament for Warrington was not a supporter of the women's cause. Mr. Harold Smith did not think the time was as yet expedient for. the enfranchisement of women. They who believed in it implicitly, knew that Mr. Harold Smith like .many other members of Parliament would change his views on the women's question when his constituents desired him to do so. She asked them to pass a resolution asking Mr. Smith to give his sup- port and do his level best to get the enfranchisement of women included in the Reform Bill, and also " that this meeting calls upon. the Government to enfranchise women during the lifetime of the present Parliament. Any member of the audience was invited to second and Mr. H. Wardman answered to the invitation, and said he had great pleasure in seconding the resolution simply because the women there were demanding the universal vote for women. If it had been a limited franchise he would not have done it, it was only because they were demanding equal vote with the men that he was in sympathy with them. The emancipation of women would never come until the women had got the vote in order that they might work out their own salvation. (Applause.) 'The resolution was heartily carried there only being a few dissentiets. THE CLOSING MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Meetings were conducted on Thursday evening and also last night. At Dalton Bank on Thursday there was a good attendance presided over by Mrs Ransome, and the chief speakers were Mrs Robinson and Miss Cros- field. Last night there was a large crowd near the Co-operative Stores, Sankey Green, when Mrs W H Stansfield presided and Mrs Robinson and Miss L Broadbent delivered able addresses on the subject. To-night the last meeting of the week will be con- ducted at Bridge Foot, N'airclough's Mill, and the Chairman will be Mr W Whiteley and the speakers will be Mrs Robinson and Mr W Baguley.</p>
        <p>At St. Paul's Church, Knightsbridge, London. OR Monday, Major Godfrey Dalrymple White, M.P, for Southport, late Grenadier Guards, son of the late General Sir Henry Dalrymple White, K.C.8., was married to Miss Catherine Cary, eldest daughter of Viscount Falkland.</p>
        <p>Photo Burrell.</p>
