      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>WARRINGTON PHOTOGRAPHIC SOCIETY AT DYSERTH ON WHIT-MONDAY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PHOTOGRAPHIC SOCIETY AT DYSERTH ON WHIT-MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dalton brings up the stragglers.</p>
        <p>the bottom of the ditch. They were told by the Board of Trade that out of every .:(.:,100 spent in drink only fis was paid in wages. In the boot trade out of every :c:.100 spent, over went in wages. If the public houses were removed from the land the whole problem of unemploy- ment would be, a long way on the road to solution. The workingman who spent any appreciable amount of money in the public-house did so by robbing those at home. It needed all the money they had got to make the home comfortable. Money spent in the public-house was worse than wasted. The labour used in the drink trade was also worse than wasted for it produced a great deal of halm for those who indulged in its product. He did not suppose the drink evil was greater in Warrington than elsewhere but it ' needed watching and they wanted the people to help in the watching and bring to their notice cases where they believed the law f had been broken. The enewy was rich and prosperdus and they could not fight it with riches, but they would fight its organisation. by their organisation. He believed there were:people all over the country who would willingly lend a help- band in theing wiping out of such a great curse to civilisation. (Applause). Questions were then invited and answered and a hearty vote of thanks to the speakers closed the meeting. The meetings will be held every Thursday evening throughout the summer until September, with the exception of the Thursday before Walking Day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>"A Sentimental Feeling."</p>
        <p>An interesting point affecting the assestment of the Manchester •••liip Canal Co.'s uncle:taking was raised at the meeting of the Warrington Union Assessment Committee )esterday. Mr. Kershaw (Messrs. Grundy, Kershaw, Samson and Co., solicitor, Manchester), appealed on behalf of the Ship Canal Comuany,against the following assessments : Walton Lock and adjoin- ing proper ties, £327 gross, £255 rateable • bridge houses at St-g Bridge, Wdderspool and fatch'ford,d.:l2 gross and X. 9 10s. rateable each. lie stated that the company did not wsh to be unreasonable, but they contended hardships would be inflicted up in them if the present assessioent were isisted upon. The company made no leek at all from Walton Lock and, as was well-known in Warriegtou,the lock itself was a very onerous ob,igation upon them. It was constructed to allow Wart ington train to pees into the river Mersey, and the compai y would be glad if it could be done away with, as it was worked at a dead loss to them There was no toll for the use of the lock ' • shy tolls that were charged were for the use of the Shirt Canal. The committee were probably aware that the true principle of rating a canal comp,ny was that the company shoult contribute to the relief of the poor in each parish through which the undertaking passed, in proportion to the profits derived from the use of their land in that parish. Triere was no other lock on the Ship Canal that was rated separately, in fact he did not think there was another case in which a lock was so rated. In every other palish in which they had assessments, canal companies were rated according to mileage. With regard to the houses, he contended that they should not be assessed as they were part of swing bridges which were not rated. The Clerk (Mr. A. Bottornley) p dated out that the whole of the assessment of the canal in the township of Wallington was fired on a very nominal basis indeed.</p>
        <p>After the committee had deliberated, the chairman (Ur. A. Brooks) informed Mr. Kershaw that their business instinct told them that the figures ought to remain as they were. They thought that with the numerous facilities the company got in Warrington, for which they paid nothing —or the asiessments in question were all they prid they ought not to object to the assessments. However, there was a little sentimental feeling, the committee wishing rot only to keep on good terms with but t r help a deserving company, and they 11,rd decided to reduce the assess- ment of Walton Lock and adjoining properties to £230 gross, £2OO rateable. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>AN UNCOMMON TREE.</head>
        <p>AN UNCOMMON TREE.</p>
        <p>Photo, Burrell. AN ALOE TREE IN THE RECTORY GARDEN.</p>
        <p>The above is a photograph of an American aloe tree which is at present flowering in the Rectory garden, It is estimated that the tree is about one hundred years old. It was in the Rectory gardens when the present Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) came to Warrington twenty-five years ago, and has never been seen to flower during that period. Such trees live for many years ten to seventy, according to treatment—before flowering, and when this takes place a tall flowering stem springs from the centrelof the massive tuft of fleshy leaves and grows very rapidly until it reaches a height of 15, 20 or even 40ft., bear- ing towards the end a. large number of flowers. The species was introduced, into Europe in 1561, and is now extensively grown in the warmer parts of this continent, as well as in Asia (India in particular).</p>
