      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WORKINGMEN AND TEMPERANCE.</head>
        <p>WORKINGMEN AND TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>A united summer temperance campaign conducted by the Warrington Temperance League, the Bend of Hope Union, and the United Kingdom Alliance was com- menced at Bridge Foot on Thursday evening. Mr. J. Griffiths pre,ided over a fair attendance and he was supported by Mr E Boreland (Liverpool) and Mr T I) Leslie,th new U.K.A. agent in Warrington. Mr Boreland said that strong drink was a temptation to some people and the public house was a place of temptation to many. People who took strong drink, whether temperately or intemperately, shared to some extent the responsibility for the evils which came through indulging in intoxicants. (Applause). Mr Leslie said the temperance question was absolutely a w,rkingman's question. They were the class the temperance reformers were wanting to get bold of for he did not think they would get many millionaires. (Laughter.) Workingmen to-day were to think more of what were the causes of beginning veinrntyagnd unemployment and what remedy could be ' applied. So long as the drink traffic was in the way, they had a barrier which prevented the wiping out of poverty and unemployment. The working class people spent over .Y.. 100,000,000 annually on . intoxicating liquors but if they would only realize their power they would sweep away for ever the drink .traffic in the country. (Applause.) Some people might say that if the drink traffic was done away with and public houses were closed, it would be causing an increase of unemployment. Of all trades, the drink trade was the worst employment-giving one. It was down at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Curnock—Beckett.</p>
        <p>A wedding which aroused great interest, particularly among the local Wesleyan Methodist community, took place at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Thursday when Mr. George Leslie Curnock, L.D S (Eng.), son of the late Mr George Dennis Curnock and Mrs Curnock, of Epping, Essex, was married to Miss Lilian Irene Beckett, daughter of the Rev. W. A. Beckett (superintendent of the Warrington Wesleyan circuit) and Mrs Beckett, of Bewsey Manse. During her short stay in Warrington the bride has gained the esteem of a large circle and the church was filled with well-wishers. The officiating ministers were :—The Revs. IW. A. Beckett (father of the bride), Eric S 7Visterhouse, M.A., 8.D., of London, (cousin of the bridegroom), Ebenezer Morgan, of Padgate, and Walter Trevor, of Sale. The serv;ce was choral and included the hymns " The Voice that breathed o'er Eden," " 0 perfect Love " and, "Lad us, Heavenly Father, lead us." Mr. Henry Riding, F.R C. 0., was at the organ and played festal music, including Mendelssohn's Wedding March. The bride was given away by her brother—the Rev, W. J. Beckett, of Richmond College, London. She was beautifully attired in ivory silk cashmere, with silk lace and fringe, and wore a veil surmounted with orange blossom. Her bouquet consisted of white roses, lilies of the valley, white carnations and orange blossom. Miss Margaret Ellen Beckett, B A., was in attendance on her bister as bridesmaid. She wore a charming Wedgwood blue dress, with black picture hat, and carried a bouquet of pink and white carna- tions which, together with a gold, beryl and pearl b acelet, was the gift of the lot idegroom. Mr. D. Reginald Curnock (brother of the bridegroom) dis- charged the duties of best man. After the ceremony a reception was held in the Church parlour. Later the happy couple departed for their honeymoon tour in the Lake D:strict, the bride travelling in a grey costume, with pile mauve blouse trimmed with Honiton lace (the gift of the bridegroom), and cream straw bat, trimmedg with mauve flowers. Many handsome presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Shaw—Gorton.</head>
        <p>Shaw—Gorton.</p>
        <p>A. fashionable wedding was that which took place ea Wednesday at Cavendish-street Congregational C nycb, Manchester, when the union was solemnized o Miss Gladys Gorton, elder daughter of Mr and Mrs. W E Gorton, formerly of Grappenhall Lodge, and Mr William Shaw, son of Mr and Mrs George Shaw, Pennington Hall, Leigh. A large number of guests atsembled, including many from Warrington and district. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. James Cregan, and the service included the anthem "0 Perfect Love " by members of the Halle choir,and the hymns" Love Divine " and "0 Father all creating." Mr. E. Bennett North was the organist. The bride was given away by her father and wore a charming gown of ivory satin, trimmed with Brussels applique, the gift of her mother. The full court train was of broch6, trimmed with the same lace and orange blossom; the bride's lovely veil of Brussells applique was also lent by her mother. Her only ornament was a diamond drop pendant, the gift of the bri _legroom, and she carried a sheaf of lilies and white heather. There were five bridesmaids: Miss Elsie Gorton (sister of the bride), Miss Sylvia Watts, Miss Dora Powell, Miss Hilda Craven (cousins of the bride) and Miss Llewellyn-Jones. Their dresses were of blue shot satin, with short cream lace tunics ornamented with pale pink, and blue chiffon roses, hats of cream tagal lined with black chiffon and trimmed with blue and ' pink forget-me-nots and shell-pink camelias. They carried sheaves of pale pink roses, tied with blue ribbon, and wore as'giftsif rom the bridegroom brooches of sapphires and pearls. The best man was Mr Frank Shaw (brother of the bridegroom). The other grooms- men were Mr. R. W. Gorton (brother of the bride), Mr. Eric Shaw and Mr. Bertie Cain (cousins of the bridegroom), and Mr. Leslie Smith. „After the wedding ceremony a reception was heldiXt the Man- chester Assembly Rooms, followed by a dance at the Midland Hotel. The newly-married pair left for London and the South Coast during the day, Mrs. Shaw travelling in fawn cashmere de soie, trimmed with heavy lace to match. Her hat was trimmed wit° two large Lancer feathers, and she had also a Shantung and lace travelling coat. Mr and Mrs Shaw received a great many beautiful presents, amongst which were : —Bride to bridegroom, gold cigarette case ; bridegroom to bride, diamond drop pendant and pearl neck band : Mrs W E Gorton, household linen and lace ; Mr W E Gorton, cheque ; Mrs Shaw, sapphire and diamond neckless ; Mr Shaw, cheque and all furniture.</p>
        <p>Lake.—Bonnell. The wedding took place on Thursday, at St. Paul's Church, of Miss Dora Bonnell, fourth daughter of Mr. F. M. Bonnell, Cattle Market Inn, to Mr. '‘Vm. John Lake, Blue Lion Hotel, son of Mr. P. E. Lake. The Rev. W. Bracecamp officiated. The bridesmaids were Misses Minnie and Beatrice Bonnell and Edie Lake, and Mr. G. Marr acted as best man.</p>
        <p>New Football League. With the object of forming a Warrington and District Association Foltball league a good number of enthusiasts and representatives of local teams met together in the Lion Hotel on Thursday evening, under the presidency of Mr Charles Wood, who was assisted by Mr R G Etrandreth. After some discussion it was decided that the meeting be postponed until the 20th inst. In the meantime Mr Brandreth was requested to write to the various club secretaries in the town on the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ENTERPRISE. THE PROFITS ON WARRINGTON'S UNDERTAKINGS. 'BIG SURPLUS FROM THE GAS DEPARTMENTS.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ENTERPRISE. THE PROFITS ON WARRINGTON'S UNDERTAKINGS. 'BIG SURPLUS FROM THE GAS DEPARTMENTS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Town Council spent much of its time at the monthly meeting on 1 uesday evening in discuss- ing the returns of the working or the various municipal undertakings during the financial ytar just concluded. Special interest was centred in the announcement of the Chairman of the Gas Committee that the net profit from that department reached the handsome sum of £16,307—an increase of over £5,000 on the previous twelve months. Had it been known earlier in the year that such a nest-egg was accumulating, it would not have been necessary to increase the rates—in fact, a reduction might have been possible. There was the inevitable discussion as to the wisdom or otherwise of the policy of building up large reserves in the various concerns, Councillor Pemberton maintaining that a heavier propottion of the profits should be devoted to the relief of rates. °mum whole, however, the returns were toceived with satisfaction, Alderman Bennett find- ing in them a complete vindication of the soundness of municipal trading.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>HUGE GAS PROFITS.</head>
        <p>HUGE GAS PROFITS.</p>
        <p>Alderman JOHN EVANS, in moving the adoption of the minutes of he Gas Committee, announced that the profits arising from the manufacture of gas in Warring- ton during the past year had reached the record BUM of £16,367. The Alderman called attention to a resolution of the Committee on the question of management and administration of the gas undertaking. Hitherto the management had been divided, the offices and the works having been kept separate,hut the committee thought it would be more satibfactory for the gas engineer to be appointed general manager with complete control of the department, and that he be held responsible for the proper working thereof. Alderman TINNION seconded.</p>
        <p>, Councillor lIA \ VTIIOhN said that some time ago he raised the question of amalgamating the gas offices with the Borough treasurer's department and received a promise that when any change in he management took place the point would be taken into consideration. He did not wish to labour the question at that stage but it was patent to many of them that a considerable saving might be effected by an amalgamation. Alderman BENNETT congratulated the Gas Committee upon the splendid result of the year's working and alluded to the profits made by the other concerns. He was always very glad when the undertakings turned out satisfactorily. He mas a great believer in municipal trading, which had been subjected to a great deal of ignorant and unnecessary criticism. The time had arrived when municipal trading would be extended in Idirections previously undreamed of, and that would be one of the lines of solution of some of the problems which affected us ti-day. The objection always urged against that policy was that municipalities were not sole to manage the concerns as successfully as if they were in private hands, but the Gas Committee had shown conclusive proof to •the contrary. They were making enormous profits and at the same time steadily reducing the price of their product, but yet giving admirable service. The same applied in a minar degree to the other departments. The total amount paid to the relief of the rates by the Gas Department was £128,000. It was almost unbelievable, and provided abundant justification of the enterprise of the com- mittee. The water undertaking showed a gross profit on the capital outlay of 3 per cent.; the electricity department a. profit of 7 per cent; the tramways a profit of 8 per cent., and the gas undertaking a profit of 11 per cent. Those figures were highly satisfactory, especially when the undert‘kings readered good and cheap service to the community. Councillor PEMBERTON said there were two opinions as to the success of municipal trading enterprises. The MAYOR,: The question of gas profits is not on the minutes. Councillor PEMBERTON : The chairman has reported that he has made £16,0R0. _ _</p>
        <p>Aldermack J011:1 Ev'ass : I did not ssy I had made it. (Laughter.) The MAYOR : It was a verbal announcement. It is not on the minutes.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION : I submit the Chairman was out of order in making that statement.</p>
        <p>•Councillor PEAL-BERTON : Ti 3"; he ought to have been stopped from making it, or we ought to be allowed to criticise it. It is hardly fair that these statements should go forth to the public in this nicely garnished way.</p>
        <p>'I be MAYott It will come on at the next meeting and then you can make a nice, prepared sneech. Councillor PEMBERTON : 1. don't make prepared speeches. They are apt to go wrong. (Laughter.) The MAYOR : It might be more palatable if pre- pared beforehand. Alderman TmszioN : It could not be worse. (Laughter.) COUBoillOr PEMBERTON : It would be a great mistake to make it more palatable. You want to make it as unpleasant as you can, to keep people up to the mark. Alderman JOHN EVANS said a sub-committee had been appointed to go into the question of the future administration of the work of the department but up to now be did not think anything had occurred to justify them in recommending an alteration in the management, as far as the collecting of accounts was concerned.</p>
        <p>Councillor HAWTHORN : I referred absolutely to the collection of accounts and not to the management. The minutes were approved. THE WATER UNDERTAKING.</p>
        <p>A report of the Borough Treasurer was submitted on the accounts of the Water Department for the year ended 31st March :—The gross income during the year was £29,653, and the expenditure £8,733, leaving the gross profit at £20,920 ; net profit £1,584. The net profit has been transferred to the reserve fund, which now amounts to £5,781, and the appropriation account has a working balance of £6,859 standing to its credit The sum of £57,351 has been paid oil (including £5,482 during the current year), leaving the debt outstanding at £403,840. ELECTRICITY. .</p>
        <p>The accounts of the electricity undertakings showed that the income during the year was £19,245 and the expenditure £11,530, leaving a gross profit of .£7,709. The contribution to the sinking fund and interest on loans, less bank interest. &amp;c., amounted to £5,963, leaving a net profit of £1,740. Alderman SMETHURST, in moving the confirmation of the minutes of the Electricity and Tramway a Committee, said the electricity department had not made as much profit as last • year, there having been rather heavy maintenance expenses chiefly in connection with machinery due to the complete overhauling of three of the engines. Then again, during the last two years, all new meters had been purchased out of revenue The income for the year was about 140 less than last year, there being a slight decrease both in respect of power and lighting supplies. The Reserve Fund amounted to £9,358 2s. 5d., and the working balance to £3,078 33. 4d., which, however, was not sufficient for the undertaking, and would have to be increased. There was no doubt that the exorbitant prices which the Corporation had to pay for fuel during March affected the year's profit to some extent, but he thought the Council would agree that it was most advisable to keep the undertaking going, and he was pleased to say that in no case had the committee to curtail the supply</p>
        <p>TRAMWAYS. - - - The Borough Treasurer reported that for the same period in connection with the tramways under taking the receipts from passengers were £21,338; other receipts amounted to £335, leaving a total income of £21,678. The expenditure was £l3,432,leaving the gross profit at £8,241. The amount charged in the net revenue account for loan charges, less bank interest, was £5,785. The sum of £1,500 has been kept in hand for the relief of the rates and a further sum of £338 has been carried forward. The balance of the profit amounting to £6lB has been transferred to the reserve fund, which has also been credited with £3lB for interest, increasing it to £lO,OOO. Alderman SMETHURST said the year's working of the tramways bad resulted in a net profit of £2,445 18s. 2d., which was £lll 145. Gd. more than last year, and wae to be disposed of by transferring £1,500 to aid the borough rate, £617 17s. 10d. to the reserve fund, and bringing forward £338 Os. 4d. The traffic receipts,which worked out at 113 d. per car mile, had increased £O7O over last year, and that fact was pleasing not only from the tramways point of view, as it was an index to the</p>
        <p>Gi:NCRAL PROSPERITY OF THR TOWN. The expenses had alsolincreased,mainly due to the extra cost of repairing the track, which was in a very much better condition than it was last year. That was an item which would naturally have a tendency to increase rather than decrease. During the year 5,375,572 passengers were carried, or 271,565 more than last year, and the car mileage was 14,676 more than the previous year. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Warrington was to be congratulated upon the sound financial position of their tramways undertaking, which had been able to meet the repairs and renewals out of profits, to build up a reserve, and to hand over a considerable sum in relief of rates. In conclusion, the Alderman said :—Mr. Mayor, to-day is my 73rd birthday—(applause)—my 21st year in the Council. I have been chairman of these committees for nine years. The concern is now a large one, the combined capital being £208,041. We have paid off £52,801 and given out of profits in relief of rates £5,368. May I quote from the lines of the " Village Blacksmith ' : " Something attempted, something done." We have added £lO,OOO to each reserve fund. (Applause). Councillor WILKINSON seconded the confirmation of the minutes._</p>
        <p>--Alderman BENNETT congratulated Alderman Smethurst not only upon his birthday but upon the very successful results of the working of the two departments under his chairmanship. They hoped he would live many years to see similarly satisfactory results. Councillor PEMBERTON said it was entirely I wrong that the rates should have been increased as they were in the recent Budget, while they had profits accumulat- ing in the various concerns. Alderman Bennett had long been looking forward to the day when they would be without rates altogether, bat they kept on increasing.</p>
        <p>One reason was that when the Corporation made profits they did not use them. They had paid off the greater part of the loans and yet they continued to build up a reserve fund. They ought not to do that. As soon as they had paid off the different loans they could re-borrow. They were now punishing the present-day ratepayers for the benefit of future ratepayers, That was totally wrong. The future should pay for itself. It was as much as the ratepayers could do to pay' their little corner." What was being done was unjust and improper. A reserve fund of £5,000 would be ample for both the electricity and the tramways undertakings. It was absolutely wicked to raise the rates 2d. in the £ when they had such funds in hand, and if he were a ratepayer in the town he would throw every one of them out of the Council. (Laughter.) lie would not leave any one of them in if they put an increase _if 2cl. on him. Councillor WtreeecsoN .There would be some " rum-'uns" put in. (Laughter). Councillor PEMBERTON It would not be difficult to get better ones than some of the present mecubers,from a financial point of viev. Councillor BELLIAN said that Councillor Pemberton made a great mistake in saying that the Corporation could re-borrow. Tley could not until they had paid back the loan. (Hear, hear.) They had twenty-severe years in which to repay the £70,000 they originally borrowed. When the tramways were re-laid the cost would be £26,0f 0, and he would like Councillor Pemberton to remember that fact. (Hear' hear.) Thee full life of the tramways was 15 years. What woula the ratepayers think in nine years from now when the question would have to be faced, if the Council accepted Councillor Pemberton's policy and did not build up an adequate reserve ? Councillor Pemberton bad not con- sidered that point at all. Councillor PEMBERTON : Yes r have. Councillor HAWTHORN said Alderman Smethurst had presented two very satisfactory reports. For nine years he had set before himself the ideal of creating a reserve fund of £lO,OOO for each department and now he saw the realisation of his dream. That fact should encour- age the department to go forward. Some parts of the town were very badly served with tramways and pnblie conveyances,notably Orford-lane and IThitecross Ward, and he hoped the needs of those localities would receive early attention. There could be no doubt among sound financiers that the Corporation was following the right policy in creating reserve fonds. They must keep up-to-date—not be like the Fire Engine Committee, trudging along with the old methods—(laughter)—and that was why they wanted a good reserve. Councillor WHeuNsoe said that the building up of adequate reserve funds Was the one sole protection for the ratepayers in case of emergency. It was the somndest policy to create surpluses. If the cost of education had not shown such an advance, there would have been no need to increase the rates this year—in fact he should have advised the Finance Committee to make a reduction of 2d. The general administration of the Corporation work was not responsible for the increase. To save the rates was no reason why they should filch the profits of their undertakings and thereby bring those concerns into financial jeopardy. ' The reason the electricity department did not make a large profit was because they kept their charges so low. The manufacturers who they were told were creating the profits were having their pound of flesh. Councillor PEMBERTON : And they deserve it. Councillor WILKINSON : Certainly; but don't ma're such a sound about the profit they make. They will not give you any price out of which to make a profit. Councillor di PEMBERTON : They would be soft if they</p>
        <p>- Councillor NVILKINSON said it would he illegal to take money out of the sin King fund for the alleviation of rates.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : You can stop putting it in though. Councillor WILKINSON retorted that it was evident the councillor knew not the difference between reserve fund and sinking fund. With regard to the progress of the electricity and tramways, Alderman Smethurst and he took over the concern with fear and trembling and a large deflait faced them. They soon cleared it off and in nine years, after paying all their charges on sinking fund and interest and spending £1,600 a year during the last few years on the track, they had realized their hopes and built up a good- reserve. Councillor HENSIIALL thought that the truth lay between the optimism of Alderman Smethurst and Councillor Wilkinson, and the pessimism of Conncillor Pemberton. The point that Councillor Pemberton had raised was, however, a proper one. When a certain individual was asked to provide for posterity,he wanted to know what posterity had done for him. (Laughter.) Now that they had built up a reserve fund of £20,000 on the electricity and tramways departments bethought they had made reasonable provision. It was the duty of the masters of multiplying millions to think of the needs of the present generation, and in future he trusted that the profits, which he hoped would increase, would go to the relief of the rates. The MAYOR : The interest on the reserve fund goes to the relief of the rates.</p>
        <p>Councillor HENSIIALI7: lam sure we should be glad to get some of the principal as well. The MAYOR : The interest on £IO,OOO is considerable. Councillor WIUKIKSON : The interest on the two is equal to three farthings in the £. Alderman SMETHURST said Councillor Pemberton's "sermon " was an annual one and he did int think it was worth taking any notice of. (Laughter.) The Mayor filled the chair with great ability and tact but it would take even a greater man than be to keep Coun- cillor Pemberton in order. (Laughter.) The Councillor should remember that he ought not to shout in the Council Chamber as though he were at a football match (Laughter.) They had it on high authority that every town ought to put V2OO per mile to a reserve fund for its trams. Showing how imperative it was to have adequate funds in hand, the Alderman said the cost of repairing the tram track at the corner of Winmarleigh- street and Palmyra-square was £5OO, while similar improvements near Mr. it, Johnson's shop,Bridge-street cost £7OO or £BOO. Although the trams were said to be in such a bad condition that nobody wished to ride in them,the number of passengers had doubled in the last ten years. The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>CRICKET CLUB SUES FOR</head>
        <p>CRICKET CLUB SUES FOR</p>
        <p>SUBSCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before Dr. Sellars, sitting as deputy judge, the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club sued G. W. Shakeshaft, 9, Chester-road, Lower /Calton, for £1 is., duo as his sub- scription to the club for last year. Mr. C. H.Unsworth, who appeared for the club, said he did not think there would be any dispute as to the facts, but he would formally prove the posting of the card of membership, on which the rule was printed. The Deputy-Judge : What is your defence? Why don't you pay ? Defendant : I resigned two months before the season commenced. The Deputy-Judge : In writing ? Defendant: Yes. __</p>
        <p>_ - - Mr. Unsworth : He sent a letter of resignation on February 23rd. The rule provides that it should be sent before December 31st. This has been brought before the committee and we have had a lot of correspondence with him about it. There is a special notice sent out with the membership 'card calling attention to the fact that members wishing to resign must give written notice to the secretary on or before December 31st. Defendant admitted that he knew of the existence of the rule.</p>
        <p>Mr. Unsworth : I don't know what defence be has, except that there is a notion abroad that, this being a voluntary and unregistered club, we cannot sue for subscriptions. That is quite an erroneous idea. It is clearly laid down that where a body of persons associate together, each has right of action against the other in order to maintain the club. It is not. fair to the other members that one should agree to the rules and then break them.</p>
        <p>The Deputy Judge: I think there is no defence, Mr. Shakeshaft. 1 suggest that you should consent to judgment. Mr. Unsworth : I offered to let him off with half-a- guinea a long time ago, but he would not pay it. Judgment for the plaintiffs was then entered by consent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MARKET PRICES.</head>
        <p>MARKET PRICES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON RIDAY) n". 3 FRESH EGGS, II for Is. SMALL SOLES, 10d FRESH BUTTER. 1/2 SALMON TROUT, Is Sd CHEESE, (Cheshire) 6d to LEMON SOLES, 10cl 9d HALIBUT, 10d RABBITS, (English) 10d to 7/2 COD, Gd FOWLS 53 couple HAKE, Gd NEW POTATOES, 1/id per lb. PLAICE, 6d and Sd BEEF, Gd to lid LOBSTER, Is Gd to 2s lb MUTTON, Gd to lid per lb. SALItos,25 to 2s Gd per lb. PORK, I Od per lb. EYEMOUTH EIADDocK,IOd VEAL, 7d to lid per lb. BRILL, 6d to ls LARGE SOLES, is CRABS, 10d to Is each * PIGEONS. IS each SPRING CHICKENS,IS3dIh ENGLISH DUCKLINGS, 3/0 to 4s.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. T. B. Allen. Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death which took place at an early hour on Monday morning of Mrs. ltosie Allen, wife of Mr. T. B. Allen, and eldest daughter of the late Councillor Rogers, of the Feathers Hotel, Bridge-street. The deceased lady, prior to her removal to Thurlstone, near Penistone, about five years ago, was well known in St. Paul's parish for her diligent work amongst the poor. Deep sympathy is felt with the husband, who is left with two boys_ one three weeks old—in his bereavement. The funeral o ok place at the Warrington Cemetery on Thursday..</p>
