      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Following in Her Mother's Sufferings</head>
        <p>Following in Her Mother's Sufferings</p>
        <p>Debility and Nervous Disorders cured in Mother and Daughter by Dr. Williams' Pink Pi 11.%</p>
        <p>To the woman of forty as well as to the girl of seventeen, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have given a helping hand in countless instances. The urgent need of most of the weaker sex is for more strength, and better blood in their veins to nourish their weary bodies, for this is the only way to banish Neuralgia, Neurasthenia, or other painful, Nervous Disorders, also Anxmia, Headaches, Palpitation, Dyspepsia and Debility. These Disorders are being cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills every day, among the thousands of grateful women being Mrs. and Miss Jordan, of 33 Hall Street, Blakenhall, Wolverhampton, whose cures are given below The Mother's Praise. The Daughter's Thanks.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Jordan states: "I am the mother of nine children. A few years ago my health seemed to go all wrong. I felt so dizzy that at times I hardly knew what to do. Also violent Neuralgia seized me. Then month in and out a dreadful pain at my hack kept me miserable day and night. I tned treatments to cure the Indigestion that distressed me, but the after-effects of the treatments made me worse.</p>
        <p>Miss S. E. Jordan, states: "Curiously ennugh, though lam only seventeen years of age I suffered much the same as mother. For many months I never knew a day's peace from suffering. Dreadful headaches made my bead so tender that the weight of my hair seemed more than I could bear. Food began to disagree with me, and fearful pains flew across my chest and shoulders after taking meals. "Frequently my heart u.ed to flutter at an alarming speed, ascl the Indigestion caused sink- ing sensations which made me tremble. I became so exhausted that I could hardly even drag myself about. At time I felt as though there was not a drop of blood in my body. . .</p>
        <p>"About a year ago I endured a grief that left me prostrate, and the strain brought on Nervous Exhaustion. A few mouthfuls of food set up severe pains in my chest, and I suffered intensely from flatulence.</p>
        <p>"One friend advised me to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. I did, and after a few boxes of the Pills the Neuralgia cleared away. Then I got better rest at nights, my nerves were steady and Iny appetite improved. "1 never missed taking Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and as time went on was able to take substantial meals regularly. So day by day I got better, my strength improved, the indigestion vanished and I have nothing to trouble me now."</p>
        <p>"Alter trying many things in vain mother got me some of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. I took them and before long my nerves were steadily toned up, and my strength improved until I could get about without distress. "In time the head pains left me and I had no discomfort after meals. The New Blood, which Dr. Williams' Pink Pills gave me, threw off all Indigestion and mide me quite well."</p>
        <p>You cannot afford to delay.—lf your Blood is impure cr your Nerves are weak— follow the example of those who have been cured already bv Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, for no other remedy so surely tones up the vital organs, fortifies the Nervous System and enriches the Blood. Dr. Williams' I'ink Pills are obtainable of most dealers, but ta,,e great care that no useless substitute is imposed upon you. Your safeguard is the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, which is on every genuine package. If in doubt send 2s. M. for one box, or 13s. OJ. for six, to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co, 46 Holborn Viaduct, London. Nothing will do but</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills</head>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills</p>
        <p>F" Plain Talks to Women" is the title of a helpful book, post free from above address.'</p>
        <p>A meeting with old frifnds, a few drinks, a ride in a railway train, and disparaging remarks about a lady's hat, led to the appearance of a well-know retired Cheshire farmer at the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday morning before Councillor Roberts (in the chair), Councillor C Jackson Holmes. Colonel Edelsten and Messrs J Rymer Young and T Locker. John Davies (77), a retired farmer, of 12 Duffield- road Irlam-o'-th-lleights, M anchester,was summoned for interfering with the comfort of passengers by disorderly behaviour in a railway carriage from Chester to War- rington on April 27th.—Mr Tate, of London, appeared for the London and North-Western Railway Company, and Mr T S Steel defended.</p>
        <p>Mr Tate said that if the defendant had not made the dangerous experiment of criticising a lady's hat he would probably not have been there. On the date mentioned a Mr. and Mis. Smith, of Earl-street, Loser Broughton, Manchester, were passengers by the G2G p.m. train from Colwyn Bay to Manchester. At Chester the defendant and several other passengers entered the same compartment. Soon after the train left Chester the defendant commenced to pass remarks about Mrs. Smith's hat. He led off by saying "Excuse me, how ridiculous," and when asked by Mr Smith what was ridiculous be replied "The lady's hat ; it looks like an old-fashioned tea tray." The passengers took no notice of the remark, as the defendant appeared to have had some drink. A gentleman in the carriage whispered something to Mr. Smith, and the defendant wanted to know what had been raid. The gentleman said it was to be hoped the lady had more sense under her hat than he the defendant had under his. Davies then used bad language and said he would fight the gentleman. Mr. Smith told defendant to moderate his language, as there was a lady in the compartment. Davies immediately apologized to Mrs Smith, but again commenced to use bad language. A gentleman in the compartment told him that he was not a gentleman, whereupon the defend- ant jumped up and raised his stick, and in doing so he knocked a picture off the rack, which fell on the head of Mrs. Smith, causing a severe headache. At Warrington a complaint was made, and the passengers, with the exception of the defendant, were accommodated in another carriage. Mr. and Mrs Smith bore out the statement in evidence and Mrs Smith said she was very much frightened by , the defendants conduct. Mr Steel : Frightened or amused ?—I was amused at first but annoyed afterwards. Mr Steel : Who was it called him a cranky old fellow? I don't remember that. Mr Steel : Do you remember him retaliating with , " You are an undersized little fellow" ?—I remember ' that. Was that not the commencement ?—I don't remember. Mr Davies was then called into the witness box and stated that he was returning from Cheater where he had met some friends and bad a few drinks with them. Shortly after the train left Chester the other passengers in the carriage indulged in loud conversation, they were contending with each other just like one met with footballers in a train. He remonstrated with them and then one word led to another. He might have made some remark about the lady's bat and he thought he was the cause of the picture coming off the rack, it was done accidentally. Mr. Steel said that was one of the cases that the magistrates could deal with by dismissel on payment of costs. He also laid stress on the defendant's age. There appeared to have been a certain amount of amuse- ment in the carriage and no doubt the old geeelen3an got irritated. He had been a churchwarden for some years and was a well-known Cheshire farmer and for seventeen years was a member of the Chester Guardians The magistrates imposed a fine of 325. including costs. Borough Pollee Court.</p>
        <p>SATCRDAY.—Before Air J W Hayward (in the chair) Alcb rruan Tinnion and Messrs A J Jolley, P. T Fair- dough and T Pember ton. A HOLLOW FRAl7ll3.—James Finn, of no fixed address, was charged with stealing 6d. by means of a trick trom Fred Lathom, 77, Lythgoes-lane, on April 25th. The Chief Constable said the prisoner endea- TO ire,' to sell to Latham El MllO cards which he repre- rented to be transparent picture cards. Latham refused to purchase at first but the prisoner pressed and</p>
        <p>Latham bought two for sixpence. The cards were sub- sequently found to be ordinary correspondence cards and tte po ice were informed of the matter. Prisoner woo was sent to gaol for 14 days was described by the Chief Constable as " a borderer between a loafer and a worker."</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr S Wright (presiding) Aldermen Smethurst, Bolton, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs G Pilling, T Looker, W RoFerts and H Thornton.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED WOUNDIN.G.—John Forster, 11, Portland- street, was charged with having inflicted grievous bodily harm on William Davies, Nap,er-screet. It was stated by the Chief Constable that at 10-13 on Saturday night Davies and Forster were having an altercation in the Golden Ball public house. Immediately after having been separated by the landlord the prisoner, it was alleged, threw a pint pot in the face of Davies inflicting severe injuries which necessitated his removal to the Infirmary. It was necessary to apply for a remand.—Mr. Steel, who appeared for the prisoner, had no objection to the remand. He stated that what happened was quite an accident but the chief constable said that was not'so. Mr. Steel applied for bail. The Chief Constable strongly objected.—Tbe magistrates refused to allow bail aud the prisoner was remanded for seven days. CONSTABLE'S STRUGGLE WITH ARMED MAN.—The struggle of a constable with an armed man on the roof °lithe Feathers Hotel was sensationally outlined when James Mackin, lodging at 17, Dial-street was charged with having been drunk and disorderly and also with having been on enclosed premises for an unlawful purpose. The Chief Constable stated that at 540 on Saturday afternoon the prisoner was found under the influence of drink on tha roof of the Feather's Hotel. Constable Wood was called and when he arrived Mackin was most violent and took out a knife. A rather severe struggle ensued. Eventually the officer gained possession of the weapon and took the man into custody. On the way to the police station the prisoner was very rough and civilian and police assistance had to be obtained. Why the prisoner should climb on to the roof one could hardly imagine. The man went into the hotel but did not have any drink there. He went through the house in the direction of the lavatory. He (the Chief Constable) applied for a remand. Prisoner said he went to the lavatory and was not on the premises with the intention of committing a felony. He tried to leave by the back door but as it was locked he climbad up the wall to get over. Mr. Harry Rogers denied that the door was fastened, and said that even if it was there was nothing to prevent the man leaving by the way he entered.— 'Mackin was remanded for a week.</p>
        <p>TIIURSDAY.—Before Mr John Fairclough (in the chair) and other magistrates.</p>
        <p>POLICEMAN ANDPHOToGE,6IIS.—Joseph Rottenber g photographers' collector, 123, Rosebery-street, Liver- pool, was charged with being disorderly in Monks- street, 012 the 25th ult. P.C. Nicolls stated that about three weeks ago a man called at his lodgings at 9, Monks-street and obtained two photographs from his landlady, Mrs Barber, on the understanding that enlargements were to be presented free. Two days later another collector called and demanded 255. He was told the enlargements were not required and that he would have to return the originals. On the 22nd another man called and took the enlarg,ments away and on the 25th May the defendant called with the same two enlargements and was told that they would he retained until the originals were returned. He became very abusive and the whole neighbourhood was disturbed. On the way to the police ststion he tried to pass a coin into his (the officer's) hand, and said " am very sorry, take this and let me go."— Prisoner was bound over for six months and Nras ordered to pay the costs of the case.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair) and other magistrates. FRIENDLY SOCIETY.'3 MONEY WITIVITELD.—James Gillooley, 12, Earle-street, was summoned for "'law- fully withholding the BM of £23 ss. 10d. belonging to the Friend in Need Lodge of the Warrington Branch of the Loyal Ancient Order of Shepherds.—Mr. W. 11. Jenkins, who prusecuted, said an arrangement had been arrived at whereby the defendant was to pay 4s. per week in order to refund the money and he did not ask for a fine. The magistratal. granted the order. HABITUAL DRUNKARD.—George Ashbrook, brick setters' labourer. 13, Admiral-street, was summoned by his wife Ellen for being an habitual drunkard and she asked for a separation order. Complainant said her husnand came home speechlessly drunk almost every I night in the week and he was drunk every week-end. I Mr Firth gave evidence as to the defendant's drunken I habits and said that the women had suffered terribly thrangli his conduct. Toe woman was a clean thrifty parson and the man was one of the worst men in Warrington.—A separation order of 12s. per week was granted.</p>
        <p>As a train from Ilkeley to Bolton Abbey was nearing Addinghana Station in Wharfedale, on Monday, a well- dressed woman was seen to rush down the embank- nisnt and jump in front of tte train. The body w.is C ut in two.</p>
        <p>En the OU "ITS !_lp4</p>
        <p>A LADY'S HAT CRITICISED. RETIRED FARMER'S OBJECTIONABLE CONDUCT IN A RAILWAY CARRIAGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Fxaminer, Saturday, June, 8, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Fxaminer, Saturday, June, 8, 1912.</p>
        <p>MR. A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>SPEECHES AT LIBERAL BOWLING COMPETITIONS.</p>
        <p>MILITARY TRAINING AND INDUSTRIAL PROBLEMS.</p>
        <p>The ancient game of bowls has no more enthusiastic admirer or supporter than Mr. A. H. Crosfield, whose interest in the local clubs is proverbial. The recent striking development of the pastime among the Liberals of Warrington has given him much satisfac- tion. and on Saturday afternoon he took the oppor- tunity of visiting greens where Liberal trundlers were engaging in friendly rivalry. First Mr. Crosfield put in an appearance at the competition f ,r the Peter Peacock Challenge Cup at the Bowling Green, Bank Quay, and had a most enthusiastic greeting from the workers of the various wards who comprised the com- peting teams. There was a large gathering of rarty leaders, including Mr. C. E. Parker (president of the Liberal Association), Aldermen Tinnion and James Evans, Councillors Wilkinson (chairman of the bowling competition) end Jackson Holmes, the Rev. W. Cooper, Messrs P. Peacock (chairman of the Liberal Executive and donor of the cup), E. Wynne Jones, S. Wright, P. O'Brien, John Dodds, P. Delaney, J. Willett, H. New and E. A Sanders. Councillor WILKINSON, who presided, extended a hea:ty welcome to Mr. Crosfield. Opponents, he said, had spoken of Mr. Crosileld as a visitor from Cannes, but they had in him a Warringtonian born and bred. (Applause.) He supposed Mr. Harold Smith was at present viewing the sights of Germany—(lauchter)— and he had sent borne a glowing testimony to the pros- perity and pleasure to he seen in that country. No doubt the places Mr. Smith visited would be proseerous and there would be a great deal of pleasure. (A \Totem "What about the coffin ? " and laughter.) He did not think their opponents would have much to say about I the coffin now. (Laughter.) Councillor Wilkinson then announced the receipt of letters of apology for non- attendance from Councillor Hawthorn and Mr T Dale (chairman of Whitecross Ward Committee) Continu- ing, be said they had in Mr. Crosfield a man who from his early youth had devoted a great deal of his time to sport—cricket, football end bowls, to say nothing about golf. (Laughter.) He had always been a great enthusiast at howls, as was evidenced by his close con- nection, extending over many years. with the Cemetery Club. He was, in fact, in his element when he was watching or enjoying a good game of bowls. (Applause). Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, who was received with cheers, said it was perfectly true, as Councillor Wilkinson had remarked, that he had taken a great interest in golf, as, well as in the good old game of bowls, for many years, and he had yet to learn that interest in such sports in the slightest degree disqualified a man from taking a deep and active interest in the political and industrial questions of the day. (Hear, hear)."l have been consider- ably amused "continued Mr. Lrossfield "at the childish criticisms levelled against me on that vomit especially when I consider the quarter from which they come,</p>
        <p>military training, because it won't wash. (Applause.) While we Liberals are by no means oblivious of the graver problems of our time and are determined to meet them and to see a broader measure of justice spread through the country, we can still rejoice that in our lighter moments we can turn to these forms of outdoor recreation which not only help to preserve health but to keep us cheerful in face of the serious business of life." (Applause.) Finally Mr. Crosfield congratulated Mr. Peacock on the happy idea which had prompted the inauguration of such a splendid competition. (Applause.) He was glad to see that grand rally of Liberal workers and he was sure they could he relied upon to back up those pro- ' gressive causes which made for the welfare of the masses of the people. (Loud applause). Alderman TINNION, in proposing a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Crostleld, took occasion to rater to Tory criticisms on the subje3t of Mr. Crostleld's occasional absence from the country. Mr. Crosfield, he said, was not the only man who went abroad, for when he (the speaker) was in France last week he was presenting his luggage for examination when a gentleman who was pretty well-known to them in Warrington lugged " up his portmanteau. It was Mr. Harold Smith. (Laughter.) Be (Alderman Tinnion) said "Hello! It's every man his own porter in this country " and Mr Smith replied " Yes. It's the best way of getting along." (A VOICE: "Had he any pamphlets in the hag"?) What there was at the bottom of the portmanteau he (Alderman Tinnion) did not know—only a very slight search was made—(laughter) —but Mr Smith seemed happy and contented to get away from his Parliamentary duties and enjoy himself on the Continent. And yet they quarrelled with Mr Crosfield for doing the same thing. No man in Warrington had done as much as Mr Crosfield for bowls, shich was undoubtedly the most popular summer game in Warrington. (Applause.) He was one who laid the foundations of its success when Warrington had only one or two teams competing for county trophies and he had supported every organisa- tion which sought to further that glorious recreation. (Applause.) In Mr. Crosfield they had the best candidate they could possibly have and he felt sure that with the help of the workers who were present on the green,they would achieve victory whenever the next oppor- tunity came. (Applause.) They were greatly indebted to Mr. Peacock for promoting that competition which was bringing all sections of the Liberal forces together and enabling them to get to know each other ins way that no previous event had done. (Applause.) Mr. C. E. PARKER seconded. He said they were proud that Mr. Crosfield, along with Sir Oliver Lodge and several other important Englishmen, had been elected members of the international conference on labour questions. He (Mr. Parker) was glad that the member for Warrington bad</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>GONE TO GERMANY</head>
        <p>GONE TO GERMANY</p>
        <p>(Laughter). The reports that brought back, however, very much depended upon something Lam, was well known to bowlers—whether they went with any bias or not. (Laughter). He hoped Mr. Smith on his return would tell them a great deal about Germany because although we had much to learn from that country in somm directions. he for one did not fear comparison with regard to those questions which were affected by our ditir.-nces ut tiscpt policy, for in every respect England would come out the Oen. (Applause) Mr. P. PEACOCK supported, and the vote was passed with acclamation.</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSPIELD, in returning thanks, said he was anxious to have the privilege uf meeting as often as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>because I fail to see why people who are unierstood to take a little interest in horses and dogs and even to spend a considerable amount of time in following them across the country, should quarrel with a man if he pursues a little ball rood a golf links occasionally. (Laughter and applause). I think :the latter occupation is the better of the two. Certainly it is the more innocent, in as much as it does not cause pain to any living creature." (Hear, hear.) He looked back upon nothing with more unmixed eatisfaction,pro- ceeded Mr. Crosfield, than upon the small share he had had in helping to encourage those forms of healthy outdoor recreation which—it was a platitude, but a good sound one to repeat—had bad such a great deal to do with building up the physique of old England and maintaining the character of John Bull. (Applause.) England had led all the nations of the world in the development of outdoor sports like bowls, golf, cricket and football, but other lands were now following in our track. Whereas for r• any years in Germany they had been accustomed to a six days working week, they were beginning to think it was time a half holiday each week should be included and that time should he given for the enjoyment of games. They heard a great deal in Warrington about</p>
        <p>MILITARY TRAINING —and an excellent thing it was on voluntary lines, in fact no-one was more anxious than he to see the services well supported. But did they suppose the fact that the German had bad so much military train- ing, without that development in the Fatherland of those outdoor forms of recreation which had attained such remarkable popularity in our country, had bred a better man on the average in Germany than we had in England ? ("No.") He had had a good deal of evidence on that point brought under his notice in the course of the last few years, because during the time he was in the House ot Commons he was very much impressed with certain practical work which bad been carried out for the improvemenb of conditions of labour in this and other countries Mr. Crosfleld then referred to the successful movement promoted by the International Association for Labour Legislation, to get rid of that abominable disease of " phosay-jaw " in the match- making industry. One country after another was induced to abolish by law the use of white phosphorous and as that meant a certain increase in the cost of manufacture, it was precisely one of those CBSO3 where, in common fairness to the manufacturer and to the workingman, action should be taken simultaneously, so that one country should not be penalised by loss of trade. What one Cr two countries had done on their own account was therefore extended all through Europe, with the result that thousands of cases of "phossy-jaw" had been prevented. The association bad also brought about the abolition by law of night work for women in all the countries of Europe, and he claimed that both those reforms were the work of internationalism of the sanest and wisest kind. In England at the present time a great advance was taking place throughout the iron and steel trade. It had been initiated by Englishmen themselves, aud the eight hours' day movement, which had swept the sheet trade and the tin plate trade of South Wales, bad proved the greatest possible bless- ing to marktpra end men. It had produced untold good From the standpoints of physique and health ; it had enabled men to handle effectively the most up-to-date machinery ; and it had actually strengthened the hands of employers in competing with rivals on the continent where far longer hours of labour were in vogue, and this wise movement bid fair to extend itself betore many years were over right through the iron and steel trade of Britain. (Applause). In America also strong pressure was being put upon employers in the iron and steel industry, though in that country the demand was at present limited to a rather inadequate stipulation for weekly rest. Germany was waking upon this question • and in about ten days time a conference would meet in 'London to discuss this far- reaching reform with the object of facilitating and accelerating it if possible by international co-operation. (Applause).What had impressed him in dealing with those industrial questions was the actual saving, from an employer's point of view, which would be effected by working shorter shifts, for the better physical condi- tion of the workers would increase their capacity for dealing with up-to-date machinery more effectively than by the old system. (Hear, hear). But international movementstrought about</p>
        <p>INTERNATIONAL CO 1 PARISONS and for many years past he had heard British managers say : " Germany is all very well. The German work- man is a good, conscientious, steady workman, but still be will almost break the heart of an English manager accustomed to the greater efficiency and initiative of the Britisher." It was most striking when they looked into the question and found how the games of England and perhaps a little bit more British beef and mutton than they could afford to consume in Continental Protected countries—had told and how the the unit of effective production per man in England was a long way ahead of that of the German, with all his military training. (Applause). " After all added Mr. Crosfield" we had better not hear too much about physical degeneracy due to lack of compulsory</p>
        <p>LIBERAL LEADERS AT BANK QUAY BOWLING GREEN. From the left: Mr. J. Willett, Mr. G. T. Rushton, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Mr. C. E. Parker, and Councillor John Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>possible those who had been so liind to him ever since tie took an active part in politics in Warrington. Some of his Tory critics who were always on the look out for opportunities of finding fault, had made a little mistake if they wanted to criticise his attention to the constitu- ency curing the time he represented Warrington in Parliament. At that time his journeys between Euston and Warrington averaged about forty a year and, without boasting, he thought that compared more than favourably with tire record of previous members for %Carrington. (Applause.) The Tories were fond of spreading pretty little fairy tales about hirn—(laughter)—and they apparently wished people to believe ths.t when he went abroad he was concerned with nothing but golf. He supposed it did not dawn upon them that the work of the Test,- national Association for Labour Legislation, which had t atazin him abroad not very long since and which would necessitate another visit to the Continent next September, covered an enormous amount of detail. Of course, he rejoiced in playing a game of golf whenever he could, hut if they thought all his time when he was abroad was given up to golf, it was only another of the mare's nests which the Tories were so clever at discovering. (Laughter).</p>
