      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>AT THE LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>AT THE LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>-- Later Mr. Croefield visited the bowling green at the Liberal Club, whare an interesting League match between the Liberal Club and Cemetery teams was in progress. At the close Mr. Crosfield distributed the prizes gained in the vice-presidents' main the previous Saturday. The winners were :—Messrs A Gleave, W Hewitt, W Patrick, G Simpson, F Walker, E A Sanders, J Lawless, R W Wright, H Bennion, JCollins, R Gallop, J Lea, W Daintith, Councillor Halliwell, H McMahon and B Webb.</p>
        <p>Councillor WiticiNsolq, who presided over a large assembly, said that if there was one man in Warrington who had done more than any other to encourage the game of bowls it was Mr. Cruzfield. (Hear. hear.) He was a thorough sportsman—(mar, hear,)—both in bowls, foot- ball in his younger days, and now in the great game of golf. Bowling had always been very dear to his heart and he took a deep interest in the game. (Hear, hear mil applause.) Mr. CROSPIELD, who was cordially greeted, offered 'may congratulations to the Liberal and Cemetery llltel bowling teams on their performances in the League, the Liberal team having won that day's match while the Cemetery Hotel team had triumphed on the aggregate of points. He thought he ought to convert himself into a sort of Jekyl and Hyde, for he had for very many years enjoyed the privilege of being cc ntmtell with both clubs and was there in a double cepaclty. lii, interest in the game of bowls, how- ever, went back much further. His father used to</p>
        <p>employ him to carry the " footer," and he would never forget when a man of splendid build and enormous proportions, hailing from America, was introduced on the old bowling green at Lyrnm and invited by courtesy to lead off after the jack bad been sent down. He did so, putting all his beef into it—(laughter)—with the result that the wood cleared the lawn, ran up a bank,and finally landed in the kitchen garden. (Laughter.) Con- tinuing, Mr. Crostield said he rejoiced to see the progress bowls had made in Warrington. As education developed be had no fear of men getting too one-sided in their views of sport, and he believed they would keep their sense of proportion and realise that all games were a means to an end and that the serious problems of life had also to be tackled. (Hear, hear.) One of the great advantages of our games was that men of all ages and ranks met together in a friendly spirit and played them in a truly sportsmanlike way. During the time he had the honour of representing Warrington in, Parliament— I (applause)—he was much impressea by the way in which men of all shades of opinion—in spite of the severe political contention that went on in the House—could meet together on the links and engage in friendly rivalry. rhat helped to promote that good feeling which was such a barmy characteristic of English public file. In a brief allusion to the</p>
        <p>INDUSTRIAL UNREST of the last two years, Mr. Crosfleld said that he at any rate could claim that he was not being wise after the event,because he had urged long ago that some levelling up in the matter of wages in this country was needed, and was absolutely justified on the figures. (Applaus ) He had urged that on platforms and in private long before the recent troubles —in fact there was no question on which he felt more str..mgly. He, however, deprecated comparisons between the relative strength and fighting power of capital and labour. The great Carlyle used to thunder against the time when cash payment would be the only binding link between employers and managers on the one hand and skilled operatives and labourers on the other in our great commercial community. (Loud appplause). Mr. Crosfield proceeded to say that he attached great importance to the work of the International Association for Labour Legislation and he was hoping for the day when, by international comparisons, the searchlight would be turned on those industries which did not deal fairly with their employees and such conduct would be held up to the odium of public opinion. (Hear, hear.) One country could help another in that respect and thus they could gradually raise throughout Europe and America not merely the wages but the whole environ- ment of the workers. (Applause.) The International Association for Labour Legislation had already done practical woalc of the highest value which had proved an infinite blessing to vast numbers of operatives both men and women. (Applause.) He was hopeful that much more of the same good work would be done through its initiative and exertions. Again touching upon recent unrest. Mr. Crosfleld said he deeply regretted that the levelling up in some branches of industry, although justifiable and necessary on the figures, was not voluntarily conceded but had to be forced, as it were, at the point of the bayonet. Great employers of labour, great directors and magnates of industry in this country must remember the good old adage about British fair play. He would have said just as much if a mistake had been made by the employees in the way of excessive demands and unwise claims, but in recent years he was bound frankly to admit that he bad felt it was the capitalist class which on the whole had made the mistake. He preferred to speak of capitalists as a whole, and he was not singling out individual businesses because there were exceptions to every rule and here and there one met with a firm struggling along and fulfilling a useful purpose where if demands were made by labour which could not possibly be met, and such a business was closed in consequence, the only result might be permanent loss of employment to a certain number of men or women as the case might be. But that was by the way, and such exceptional cases were no contradiction of the general rule and the particular fact he had stated. (Hear, bear.) He hoped the difficulties which con- fronted the industrial world would be adjusted in a literal spirit and that, whatever was the outcome, lovers of bowls would still be able to meet on the green and be as good friends as ever they were. (Applause.) Mr. H. NEW proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Crosfleld and the donors of prizes. He expressed the hope that in the near future they would have the pleasure of again returning Mr Crostleld to Westminster. ' (Applause and a voice : "We shall.") It was a privilege to be able to come down to the green on a Saturday afternoon and see the men playing the fine old game ot bowls and striving-for the victory. It was the same in life—we were all striving for victory. The very fact of their future member being amongst them, and knowing the great friend he was to theta on the labour question, gave' them much encouragement. (Applause). Mr. COOK, as captain of the bowling team for the second season in succession, seconded, and the vote was heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>, ..„ a 1 ri i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ALLEGED BURGLARY.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED BURGLARY.</p>
        <p>Violent Man in a Bedroom.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court yesterday, before the Mayor in the chair, and other magistrates, Michael McGrath of no settled address was charged with burglarionsly breaking into the premises of James Sexton, 13, Orford-street, with the intent to steal.— The Chief Constable stated that at 11 o'clock the previous night, Mr. Sexton and the remainder of the household retired to bed and shortly after midnight Mr and thei Sexton was awakened by the screaming of his children, and on going into the room occupied by them he saw the prisoner and at once closed with him. A keen strugglesextaa ensued e andbrother aa theyd sa a botheae fteol hiso n assistancethe ground.l‘'r . two seized the man whilst Sexton went for the police. lon the arrival of the officer the prisoner was still struggling to get away but the two youths courageously stuck to him. After the officer had taken McGrath into I custody and was proceeding downstairs the prisoner I tripped him up and he fell to the bottom of the stairs. Another officer was called and the prisoner was safely lodged in the cells. Continuing the Chief Constable said that an adjournment for a week would be necessary iniorder that the case might he committed for trial. When the officers returned to the house they found that an entrance had been affected through one of the windows, the catch of which was loose. He had ransacked the drawers and had apparently gone in the children's bedroom with the same intentions. Prisoner said he had been working and had had a drop of drink. P.C. Rowles : lie had had some drink but knew what he was doing. No burglars tools were found in his possession. Prisoner said the money found in his poseession was part of the wages he had earned the previous day. The aplication for a remand was granted,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>OZONAIR AND HEALTH.</head>
        <p>OZONAIR AND HEALTH.</p>
        <p>That stimulating and refreshing effect in the air experienced after the passing of a heavy thunderstorm, is now enjoyed each time one enters the Central London Tube. The " Ozonair " system recently installed on this railway is responsible for what is in effect a con- tinuous thunderstorm in each station without the attendant disadvantages. Air drawn from the street is electrically treated and entirely freed from impuri- ties, a vastly different thing to the mere cloaking of them as with many disinfectants. This most effective system may be applied with the utmost facility to any home or building, and it has entirely overcome the trouble attending smells from cooking that reached the guests at such ereat hotels as the Piccadilly and Savoy, London, and Ritz, Paris.</p>
        <p>Captain Rostron, the commander of the Cunard liner Carpathia, was presented on Monday in New York, with a purse or £2,ooo,which bad been raised by Mr. Hearst's New York American," by popular subscription. The presentation was made the occasion of a little cere- mony at which a number of prominent people were present.</p>
        <p>BAKE with the assurance of SUCCESS by using BORWICK'S '- BAKING POWDER. '</p>
