coraiide_the daughter of John La I'rot slis° flortaable repute lived here, who too! N; W„ waeir situation. The original of we \Mae," noe°,,a Maiione, Lord of Stansfield, who -or - rrona Normandy- • Wiliam the a. At) lam • with i ft,„; te4' J;tt vitio_lll,,all likelihood a follower of Earl Stt IN 4 h th for '4 tbiti lordship was bestowed." it aq e Nil I as eh vantage-ground from which Mr. 41 ItiletOtt4 thefor the present to delivefir khies NI le ,Rlviejo'°rlrler somevvhat weak and c 1 • tkpi 14 "arfare a' Novi let us glance at the weapons Dlal°/eil tns3r 'tk•lelreing the reader to deckle! thltAOiti anti kulok warranted by the methods of Inrs• ,t.ue 1111 a. course followed in the slight t 4 h. ,la ing that has taken place tlfirhlllnre,,,,”,`elr or No. Following the example fe,ra ~,'' Rail 'ttlt': Mr. Barker, the new tenant of not the church at Cross Stone "11--tot 'flahiP. Ltit the Patirli Church of leas% so one may surely conclude</p>
        <p>k 114 !Irltlred hty ')/14 lah tty eo4.lea h Th:lo4 is_44 bar 3' detitti the 4Ntt ellOkl that 44Y044 Ait)tiN Itt</p>
        <p>from the coincidence that he has courteously com- plied with an invitation to read the Sunday morning lesson there, just as the gentleman whom he has succeeded in the tenancy of the Hall has been wont to do for years. Already Mr. Townroe has given characteristic addresses at party gatherings in and about Too morden. Quite candidly and with no kind of restraint he avows his object in thus going to live among them. He realises that a candidate for the suffreges of an indus- trial commuiiity "should endeavour to understand the needs of the district," consequently he has " gene there to meet the voters on all sides." Already he has confessedly had "a leng and instructive talk with the secretary of the IL P." in that yell. y, and announces his further inten- tion to get through as many of the trills and work- shops in the division as possible. Surely, in vain is the net spread in the eight of any bird; aud I imagine that tl e Progressive birds of Middleton are mostly too old to be caught eith Tory chaff—that the time has for ever gone by when the,e were to be found thereabout any number of supposed Liberal canaries whose tail- feathers were EC' deceptive in hue as to seem to invite capture by the simple process of sprinkli g Unionist salt thereupon. But to retu n to Mr. Townroe himself. 'What of the political faith that now-a-days is in him, and cif the gospel of sccial regeneration which this latest Tory crusader in Middleton has set forth assiduously to preach to the masses of cotton operatives and other workers for who e assist- ance at the polls he will ultimately apneal ? War- ringtons,least of all the Liberal sections of the borough, stand in no need of instruction on this point and I can add little if anything to the information which they p ,ssess respecting it ; the most I cm do is to iidicate the line which Mr. Townroe is taking in the presenta- ti.n of his doctrines to the multitude wh m he seeks to convert or pervert, eccording to the °pit ions and prejudices of those who do me the honour to plane° over this contribution. Although he Middleton di% ision swarms with convinced and sturdy Nonconfot mists, he has already vigorously denounced the Welsh Church Disestabli-h- -, went. Declaring diet, as a hueirress map, te is supremely aexie us to get down to viral issues -issues which aff et work and wages, and which he sums up as " the questi n of imperial int-iests and the improve- ment of social conditions,' and inviting workingmen to "think imperially," he suggests a system t Colonial farms as a panacea for the admittedly widespread evil that is robbing our agricultural villages of their thew and sinew and aggravating the pet plexing problem of the city slum. In other wi rds he would depi ive the old c• untry of, and at her expense et rich the new world with, the pick of the home labour market ! For tl is he tru-t$ to tl e co-operation of the trade unions, looting fora time when these will be linked up .throughout the whole empire ; but methinlie the organised workers of the Middleton division will in due course show unto him a more excellent way. He and they are alike dis- satisfied with present-day conditions in this country. Ile agrees that they must have better housing, and insists that something must be done to stop the terrible ],infantile mortslity in Lancashire towns. He claims that the Unionist party have Iced these questions in the past, and bears this out by reference to factory legislation effected long prior to the appearance of that party as now constituted ! He states that forty years ago, the Liberal party bring apathetic and even hostile, "it was the Unionists who came forward and siid 'We must have factory legis- ' lation ; we must I ok into housing and try to improve , secial conditions.'" And as it was in the beginning, it is now, and ever shall be, so far as the working classes and their Unionist well-wishers are concerned. To this effect does Mr. Townroe hold forth in his new sphere. In a word, it is the old, old fairy tale; Cedliog's the friend, not Short. So, presumably in expectation of a success which at the last election was denied Professor Hewing himself, does this adventuous young man from Warrington trim his sails to the Mihdleton trade winds, and go angling for gudgeon in what will presently prove for him a sea of troubles. On the present, or what may termed the Stansfield Hall venture, one can well understand he is out for a good time amid a social ' environment calculated to deceive the very eh ct. Flattered, as I fancy his being, by the well-intentioned Primrose dames and other sincere admirers, the pros- pect may just now appear to his vision exceedingly rosy; but after a while his eyes will be opened to the fact that such appearances are quite deceptive. When he enters fairly 1111011 the serious part of his undertaking and finds himself face to face with local Liberalism in grim determination, Mr. Townrc'e will be put on his mettle as he has never yet been, and will be tried and tested with a severity which perhaps he now scarcely suspects. Anyhow, he will find :that the progressive forces, Liberal and Labour combined, within the extensive and populous area of the threatened conflict, are of more than average intelligence and in deadly earnest, and that he has before him there a tremendously uphill struggle—that, in a word, hems destined so far as Middleton is concerned to labour in vain and spend his strength (and incidentally a good round sum of money) for naught but confusion of face</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>IS YOUR NAME HERE? Susan is Hebrew, a Lily. Alma is Latin, the Kindly. Guy is French, the Leader. Margaret is Greek, a Pearl. Job is Hebrew, the Mourner. Rachel is Hebrew, the Lamb. Paul is Latin, the Small One. Edwin is Saxon, a Conqueror. Lionel, Latin, is a Little Lion. Clara is Latin, the Bright One. Hugh is Dutch, the Lofty Man. Martin is Latin, the Martial One. Gilbert is Saxon, Bright as God. Lucius is Latin, the shining One. Ernest is Greek, the Serious One. Eunice is Greek, the Fair Victoiy. Florence is Latin, the Blossoming. Arthur is Saxon and means Noble. Leonard, German name, is Lionlike. Ruth is Hebrew, and means Beauty. Sophia is Greek, and means Wisdom. Arabella is Latin, the Beautiful Altar. Swab, Hebrew name, means I'rincess. Rosamond is Saxon,the Rose of Peace. Cesar, Latin name, means Hairy Man. Agatha is a Greek lame, the Good One. 1-aac, a Hebrew name, means Laughter. Oliver is of Roman origin, an Olive Tree. Lucy is the feminine of the Latin Lucius. Edith and Editha are Saxon, for Happiness. D .niel is Hebrew meaning God is judge. Jamee is of Hebrew origin, the Beguiler. Meredith is Celtic, the Roaring of the Sea. Agnes is of German origin, the Chaste One. Moses, a Hebrevv name, means Drawn One. Matthew, a Jesish name, bignifics a Gift. Harold, the Champion, is of Saxon origin. Huldah, from the Hebrew, means a Weasel. Eugenia and Eugenie are French, Well Born. Constantine is Latin, signifying the Resolute. Catharine, a Greek name, means the Pure.</p>
        <p>BRAVERY. Bravery is not fearlessness ; Bravery is not fearlessness When a wrong is done; Bravery's standing up to fear; Bravery's holding back the tear When the pain's begun Bravery is not lack of feeling For the pain that has no healing Or the breaking heart: Bravery is the calm endurance Of the pain with firm assurance Of a better part. Bravery needs no battle bluster, Nor a field where passions muster ; Calm, and strong, and still, It may play its part in silence, Or may effer wrong defiance With a patient will : 'Tis the true soul's vital forces Fed from Virtue's rich resources.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>THIS WEEK'S STORY.</head>
        <p>THIS WEEK'S STORY.</p>
        <p>The motorist emerged from beneath the car, and struggled for breath. His helpful friend, holding the oil-can, beamed upon him. "I've just given the cylinder a thorough oil- ing, Dick, old man," said the helpful friend. " Cylinder !" said the motorist, heatedly : " that wasn't the cylinder. It was my ear!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, June, 8, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June, 8, 1912.</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>"THE STRENGTH OF INSECTS." This was the subjrct of " Uccle Caleb's " notes in last week's " Children's Corner," and he suggesttd a test with a large teethe. Striking illustration in the form of the tiny ant has since come my way. It was at Hardcastle Crags. I took train to Hebden Bridge, and walked forward to the crags, and in following the path through the woods I came across any number of ants. They were on their daily foraging expedition, and it was exceedingly interesting to note ho', when one charced upon a morsel of food or other desirable object, it would prounce upon ft, seize it nith its mandibles, and, although the plize migl.t be bigger and ever so much heavier, drag it along to the ant- hill. The ant is a m'cld of perseverance and determination.</p>
        <p>ATTACKING TIIE SALVATION ARMY. Returs ing after tea, I found I had a little time to spare, and pi sauntei ing about I noticed a young DUD addiessing a considerable crowd. I knitted that be was an ex-officer of the Salvation Army, who, having becn dismis ed the rot vice, is now going .ab ut tin country mercilessly " exposing " and very vigorously denouncing General Booth and all his works. I havf since heard him in another town, and I should sa that, whatever may b 3 the result of the c Ilection which is invariably taken, lie injures his on cause b 3 inaccuracy as to details aid by over statement generally, lie paints in too vivid colours, and hi assertions are of the most sensational and reckless charac'er.</p>
        <p>the -same advantage as thir more fortunate little brothers and sisters, but gradually they become wan and lift less, due doubtlens to their unhealthy c ndi- tiens of lite. happily Warrington is being roused to a sense of the foolish waste which wculd be entailed by continued neglect of valuable child-life, as well as of the grms injustice to the ionocent and helpicEs subjects of the heavy and undesetved handicap, and there are Letter days in store even for the denizers of the slum. SMOKE AND THE WHIRLWIND.</p>
        <p>With all due respect to the Mayor and members of the Health Committee, and with the natural diffidence of a mere layman, I hasten to endorse and supplement the " Whillwiticl's " protest against the abounding, harmful, extravagant and largely avoidable smrke nuisance. As Canon Masterman remarked tie other week, our descendants will wonder why we tolerated our ugly, smoke-begrimed, squalid cities, just as we wonder at the insanitary cities of our forefathers. Let me in a cord recall how, in his Warrington lecture on smoke prevention, Mr. W. Nicholson, chief inspector under the She flield County Cemncil, stated that at least 80 per cent I smoke was unnecessary, and manufac- tu ers weie beginning to see that am ke prevention was profitable. The time had COCIT when tbe L-cal Government Board should create a smoke dep rtrnent to supervise local authorities. In reminding interested readers of this authoritative utter- ance in their midst, I may just refer those who are irclined to dispute the el tirn tht f eto.y sm ke is unavoidable to one of thtir own el use who has a deed the problem. I mean Mr. Gordon liar icy, the advanced Lberal Member f ' arliament f ar Rochdale, who, in partnership with his brother, has two big mills in the Littleborough district ha never pollute the atmcsphere at all. As for him</p>
        <p>th it will not when he my, thole must be more drastic application of the coercive lash. Time was when the ruling powers at Middleton thought thf re was no alternative to the p.d:cy now appal., mly in favour with the municipal authorities here, but the late Mrs Hopwood set them a memorable example and shamed them out of their inacti la. Being a wealthy lady, she engaged special em ke inspectors, and devoted her leisire to the personal prosccution of provid and perstst.nt offenders. These proceedings by an able and relentless foe of grin() and dit t, whom none could trifle with, and wh ) was only to be conciliated or mollified by strict complance e ith the law's demand's, had the dl ct of practically abolishing the smoke nuisance thtoughout the area of the lady's operations. If Warrington much longer retains the naemcn able distir cants of "one of the smokiest towns in the country for its size," the sc-called health authorities will be without excuse. As for the chief offenders in this rr spect, they must either mend theit ways, or stand convicted of indifference and careless- ness alike to public responsibility and material loss, or they must be convicted in another sense calculated to nuse them to action in the town's as well as their own ptivate interest. EPITAPHS.</p>
        <p>In intreducing a geed example which a member of the' Examiner staff hal copied, it was observed a week ago that well-authenticated tombstone epitaphs are always interesting. lam afraid the following will badly be found acceptable under the bead :</p>
        <p>"Boy Gun ' Joy Fun. "Gun Bust Boy . PuEt." Nor this, which is like unto it :</p>
        <p>" Little boy, Box o' paints; Sucked the brush. Joincd the Saints."</p>
        <p>I have Fever al of the other sort, but I must reserve them for a more convenient season ; they wculd require this cclumn to themselves, and that is quite out of the question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>COSTERS" DERBY.</head>
        <p>THE COSTERS" DERBY.</p>
        <p>" SPICY " BITS.</p>
        <p>He knows how to tickle the ears of the groundhogs, and the multitudes aPracted by his matter and style are evidently impressed by his wholesale charges of extortion, corruption and jobbery. Although he tells his hearers that he could go on night after night dealing with various phases of the subject and not repeat himself in atty particular, he cannot deny himself the luxury of again and again returning to one specially prepared titbit. It runs something like this : " William Booth (I never refer to him as ' the amoral '—he's not entitled to it) would seek to demon- stra'e by means of a walnut the immense suleriority of his own t rganisati-n and the utter wortlilt ssness and the instgniticance of all other religi us denominations. Taking in his hand a nut just as it bad left the tree, he wr.uld point to the outer covering of all as illustrating the Church of England ; thsn stripping off that, he would show the shell as repre- senting the Noncor for mist Churches ; and finally he would crack the nut and exhibit the kernal as typify- ing the Salvation Army. Once when I was watching him do this, he exclaimed triumphantly : Now, I cpnae to the test, and I will leave you t draw your our own inference as t ) which is the true and genuine religion.' He thereupon broke the shell and the out proved to be absolutely rotten r' SLIPS BY THE WAY.</p>
        <p>That is the case of this remarkable adventurer, and he backs it by some rather gross if cot blasphemous statements I hold no brief for the Salvation Army, and am not concerned to disprove any of the sericus allegations made by this person against its venerable chief and his trusted subordinates; but I do suggest that if his slipshod mode cf treatment of facts veil wi hin my knowledge be any indication, he is not very ieliable. The s cond time I heard him he began by describing General Booth as originally a Wesleyan minister at Salem Chapel, Halifax. Of course, the General never was a Wesleyan minister. As I stated in this column when last he visited Warrington, Salem Chapel was at that period the head of the Methodist New Connexion Ci cuit including Brighouse, where the Rev. William Booth was then stationed. The speaker further dis played copies of the " Daily Mirror," with pictures of the unfoitunate Boxed evictions. According to what he said, the pivots bore a Monday's date and illus- trated scenes occurring on the previous day. That could not posibly have been the rase. Such wai rants are not executed on Sundays, eh? SUCCESSFUL LOCAL SINGERS.</p>
        <p>My hearty congratulations to the Warrington Male Choral Union, whe se highly creditable achievements at the Eisteddfod was recorded in the last issue of this paper. Hundreds of years ago, one of the greatest of the old comrsers of unaccompanied choral music declared —" There is no music of instruments what- soever compared with that which is made of the voices of men, where the same are well sorted and ordered." The present century has witnes,ed a groat revival in part singing, and male voice choirs especially are increasing on every hand. One is naturally pi oud to think that Warrington is likely to maintain pride of place in competition with worthy rivals in the con- tes's now multiplying more raridly than ever. LABOUR M.P.'S VIEW OF THE CHURCH. Mr. Ramsey Macdonald's Edinburgh speech on the Church at.cl Labour, which was the subject of an editorial note in last Saturday's "Examber,” reminded me of a former statement of his that the reason why working men do not go to church is that they have ceased to take an interest in going to church. The Church, he complains, has lost touch with modern life and the blies of rindern life. The individual still has his thirst and hunger after righteousness; but Society, with its materialist and debasing pursuits after wealth and folly, and the weariness it imposes upon the people engaged in its tasks of industrial production and com- petition, makes the spiritual life difficult. He urges that the function of the Church is not to become the world, but to inspire the woild ; the duty of the preacher is not to propound a scientific sociology, but to infuse the right spirit cf eternal goodness in all s ciologies. In enlarging upon his congenial theme Mr. Macdonald made a remarkable disclaimer and some admissions w hich I should like to reproduce here, but it must suffice simply to state that they were timely arid just, if not exactly relished by the strictly secular section of the body whose brain Mr. Macdenald undoubtely is.</p>
        <p>- - SLUM CIIILDREN, What with heal h week, the annual report of the Borough Medical Officer, and appropriate and repeated editorial comments, Warrington ought ere long to be able to show a cleaner bill. Greater attention is being paid to the poor children now-a-days, and assuredly this is destined very soon to justify itself. Miss Eva 11. Jones, chief woman Health Inspector qf Bradford, writing in the " Chris'ian Commonwealth," a while ago, observed that child-life seetna to swarm in the p:orer quarters of our industrial cities, the araimic, impoverished little faces of the children showing the result of their unhealthy aud unnatural life ; she had seen numberless babies born, and as a rule they are perfectly normal abd wonderfully bright and healthy- looking, so that they begin life usually equipped with</p>
        <p>M ntlay was a gala day in the East E d. Under the mil ic..)a of Our Dumb Lergue, nearly 200 donkeys were sh wn at the People's Palace in ills MUe End-ro id, to comp( te for the testers blue ril and - a cup given annually by Queen Alexandra for the best kept donkey. The owners appeared in gorgeous costumes, th usands of pearl buttons, arrangel in fanta,tie desjgns, decorating them from head to foot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>FACE, BODY AND HEAD BROKEN OUT.</head>
        <p>FACE, BODY AND HEAD BROKEN OUT.</p>
        <p>PiNll Cottage, Great Oak, Bryngwyn, Nr. Raglan, Mon., Wales.—" Two years ago I caught a chill and my face and body and head all broke out in a mass of sc tbs—thick hatd scabs. 1 could not shave, nor get much sleep because of the itching, and it was raw and painful. At times I was very depressed and low, and wcrk was a misery to me. I spent pounds on different ointments and pills ai.d at last I decided to try Cuticura OintruPnt. Before I finished the first box I got relit f. he scabs began to peel off my head and Lice, and now after using a couple of boxes of both Cuticura Ointment and Pills I am entirely well." (Signed) Ernest Gardner, August 21st, 1911. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A ,sample cf each with 32-p. book poEt free from nearest depot: Newbery, 27, Chatterhcuse Sq., London; R. Towns &amp; Co., Sydney, N.S W.; Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co., Calcutta and Bombay ; "Cuticura," Boston, U.S.A. Tender- faced men snould shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.</p>
        <p>MEMORIALS OF AN ENGINEER AND PHILANTHROPIST. At Crewe on Saturday two large bronze' busts and a life-size oil painting of the late Mr. F. W. Webb, for many years the chief mechanical engineer to the London and North-Western Railway Campany, a ere unveiled. Mr Webb was not only a great engineer, but a great philanthropist, leaving the major portion of his wealth of about a quarter of a million to the Webb Railway Orphanage and other charities. He was also one of the founders of the Crewe Cottage Hospital. A bust was placed in each of these institutions to his memory. ,The model was made by Sir H. 13. Robertson, of Corwen, who was a personal friend, and who gave it to the eom- ruittee in addition to defraying the cost of casting one of the busts. The other was subscribed for by the employees in the Crewe worts. The oil painting was also subscribed for by the railwaymen, and has been placed in the erpharage.</p>
        <p>A fire broke out on Sunday evening on the Cunard liner Carmania, s she was lying in the Huskisson Dock Liverpool. Before the fire was got under the saloon quarters were almost destroyed, but the firs was confined to the superstructure.</p>
        <p>WOUND. The secret of happiness. -I- It is health. So bear in mind that Steedman's Powders correct the slight stomach troubles which make children no fretful, and their parents depressed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL:</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL:</p>
        <p>ALLEGED BOYCOTTING IN THE POLICE FORCE.</p>
        <p>HOW THE BRIDGE WORKS ARE PROGRESSING.</p>
        <p>Although the agenda for the monthly meeting of the Warringt,.n Town Council on Tuesdt y evening appeared, it first sight, to con'arn little contentious business, there were were several long discussiens. The Mayor (Councillor Dr J,.seph) presided, I,nd there were also pr. seat : —Aldermen A B.rnett, T Burton. J. hn EVEDS, J +rues Evans, W Pierpoint, J Srnethurstand D Tinnicn ; Councillors (1 Archer, T H Ashcroft, W II Hurtles, J Broadhurst, I Chantler, E Cupoingten, Dickenson, J Halliwell, J Hawthorn, R tleiisball, C Jackson Holmes, W II J Jenkins, F I Lashford, F Lindley, J P mberton, H Robe,t,,J Saukei, JSt e—a d, T S St el, .1 Wilkinson and W J Wilson ; Messrs J L Whittle (town clerk`, A I Hal'away (deputy town der), T H Spinks (essistant town clerk). J Fairhurst (borough treasurer), F V L Mathias (eb o• ecal ei &amp;near). .1 M Murray (director of education), Dr J C Hibbert (medical officer), ml J Gray (water engineer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>LATE MR. CHARLES WHITE.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. CHARLES WHITE.</p>
        <p>Before the ordinary business was entered upon the Mayor proposed a resolution of condolence with the family of the late Dr. White, who, he raid, was a man thysically and mentally of a very high type. Ile beg,n ha professional career under the old school of medicine and surgery, when the sciences were not so extended and elaborate as they were now. Ile kept abreatw ith the times to the very last, ar at any rate until he retired from prt ctioe. Ile (the Mayor) bad known him for 38 years as a highly intelligent medical p- actiticncr. To the last he enjoyed his life, Pid it n-ret be sorre consolation to the relatives to know that he passed away quietly and painlessly. (Hear, bear). Alderman BFRNETT, in seconding, said he bad known Dr. White all his life and had the highest remit-4e ouinion of his integrity. Ha was not only a sery all, to hr but a and&amp; forwani and cultured Englishman. His dew h was a public lo si to the town, and he was sure they all s'ncerely joined in the vote of cendolence which had ben moved by the Mayor. During the last onneersltion he had with him, which was at Market C,'a'e about th 3 time the B Irbauld tablet was unveiled, rho doctor mentioned The fact that he received his t-ainine, in chemistry, he (Alderman Bernett) believed, f ern Arthur Aikin in one of the London hospitals. Mr. Aikin was a brother of Mrs. Barbauld, and son of Dr. John Aikin, a dist ingui.,hed man of leltera, who lived in War ington towards the close of the 18th century. He was sure the Council would be interested in that little rorniniscerce, Is it affording a pleasant link with one ef the most interesting periods in his history of the borough. Ceueciller JENKINS, in pa3ing a tribute of respect and admintion, mentioned that for several years Dr. Wbite freely rave his services in the medical examina- tion of C.e ht nrtr4 ds of children who had been sent to the camp at Prestat ye. Alderman BURTON spoke of Dr. White's interest in the Infirmary and of the high regard in which he was held by the Workingmen's Committee. Councillor SKELLAND commented upon the doctor's kindness to the men white he was engaged as medical officer f a en iron works with which be the (the speaker) wcs connected many 3 ears ago. Councillor ROBERTS said that of Dr. White as a citizen there was no room for any opinion but one. His memory was as fragrant of good deeds and of honourable, just and upright life as that of any man he (the speaker) ever knew. The resolution was carried in silence, all standing. THE LATE MRS. PICKMERE.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR next proposed a vote of condolence with the family of the late Mrs. Pickmere, who, he said, was the wife of ore of the most popular, if not the most popular. Mayms the borough had ever had ; at any rate judging from the long time he was in (ffice. He remembered Major Pickmere being in the chair at a meeting connected with the Infirmary, when he (the Mayor) was elected house surgeon. It %as during the Maj .r's first Mayoralty, Furl he remembered the kind way in uhieh he addressed him as a 3 oung man. He also remembered Major and Mrs. Pit kmere as probably the most stately end handsome couple one could hive met anyv here. They were kind and benevolent in their actions and words. (War, bear).. .</p>
        <p>Alderman SmErrt:f nsT seconded the motion. He had known the Major and Mrs. Pickmere ever since be came to Warrington, which w s a long time since, and a kinder or more amiable lady and gentleman he had never met. He remembered their periods of (ince as Mayor and Mayores., and no couple had been so popular. (Hear, hear). The resolution was carried in silence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>LATE COUNCILLOR ROGERS.</head>
        <p>THE LATE COUNCILLOR ROGERS.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR read a letter he had received from Mrs [lc-Ten', thanking the Council for the kind vote of sympathy passed at the previous meeting. It had been a solice tl herself and family in thcir beretvement and was sincerely appreciated. ALLEGED BOYCOT CENG IN rift?, POLICE FORCE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>IS THERE DISSATISFACTION ?</head>
        <p>IS THERE DISSATISFACTION ?</p>
        <p>When the minutes of the Watch Committee were sub- mitted for approval, Councillor Broadhu.st asked if there was any truth in the rumor that twelve members of the police force hail been boycotted on account of not signirg the declaration in connection with the wages and pension qu-stions. Was it a fact that Mr. Stephen Walsh was to ask the Home Secretary whether these twelve men had been boycotted withott having had the privilege of giving the matter a second th ught as to whether they would sign the m'cessary form or not ?</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: We know nothing about it. It is mere rumour._ . . .</p>
        <p>Councillor If A \ TIMM.; said that at the last meeting he raised a question with reference t ) the wages ol eight or nice constables ho did not come under the new wages scheme, and it was stated that at the next meeting of the Watch Committee the questions would be raised. Had that been done? . -</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: You are right ; it was promised. I am afraid it is a promise that his been forgotten. (Laughter). Councillor HAWTHORN: I will overlook it if you will inquire into the matter. The Mayor : Yes. Councillor BROADHURST said he hail been informed that several men who would like the privilege of sign- ing the new regulations had, unfortunately, not the option now. The MAYOR : There will be no difficulties placed in the way of their doing so. Co, ncillor BROADHURST : lam informed there has been a difficulty. The MAYOR: I can only promise you that we sball inquire into the matter. Alderman SatrritUßST : The matter has b;..en thrashed out at the ince ing of the committee twice, and the Chief Constable stated &amp;finitely that every person in the force had been asked whether he would sign or not. he MAYOR : But the situation mentioned by Coun- cillor Broadhurst has not been discussed. Alderman SMETHURST : We have had no complaint. The MAYOR : No ; I grant that. The TOWN CLERK : We have not beard of it before. Coutcillor WILKINSON : It is a mystery. Alderman TINNION : There is no mystery aboutit It is unfair to say to. Ccuncillcr WILKINSON : Someone explain it, then. Councillor P.EMBERTON : There is no doubt there is dissatisfaction _</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : I have not heard of it and I ought to be it: touch with the police. Cot voillor PEMBERTON : I did not think you would have heard. The MAYOR : Well I ought to have done, even before you. Councillor PEMBERTON : You are not likely to. There has to be great discipline in the police force, and it i 3 just, perhaps, that tittle sympathy which would bring these people together. The MAYOR : Might I ask what is your authority ? Councillor Putt:Lamb: : You may ask it, but I shall not give it. The MAYOR: Then you cannot complain if we don't accept I he statement. Councillor PEMBERTON : That is quite true. You can please yourself v‘hether you believe it. cr not, but I make the statement that there is dissatisfaction. The MAYOR : Of: course I; shall inquire into the matter, I can say that. Councillor PEMBERTON: I will give you my word as a public man that dis3atisfaction does exist. The MAYOR: And I will give you my word as a public man that I have not heard anything about it. Councillor PEMBERTON : lam not saying, for one moment, that 3 oa la ave. The MAYOR : I have as much right to say that as you have. C.uocillor PEMBERTON : I agree. I don't think you do know. The MAYOR : Rut you talk in a way as much to say that if 3ou say a thing re-one must contradict it. That is irritating to any intelligent being. Councillor PEMBERTON : I don't mePn to do that. The MAYOR : You do it regularly. It does not help debate at all. Councillor PEMBERTON : If I did, I should only be followiug another celebrated man, " What I have said, I hive said." (Laighter.) The minutes were agreed to. . TOWN PLANNING.</p>
        <p>_ The Street Improvement Committee submitted the following resolution for confirmation :—" That application be made to the Local Government Board under Part 11. of the Housing, Town Planning, &amp;c.,Act 1909' and the Town Phu-ming Procedure Regulations (England ard Wake, 1910) for authority to pr, pare a town planning scheme.and that the Town Clerk forward a certified copy of this resolution to the Hoard together vc Rh such mops, statements and other particulars</p>
        <p>required by the said Act or Regulations or as may be asked for by the said Board, and as may be approved by the Street Improvement Committee, and to take all other necessary steps in relation to such application and for giving notice thereof." This resolution was confirmed by the Council. THE PUBLIC HEALTH.</p>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE AND THE ASSISTANT MEDICAL OFFICER. NO VOICE IN THE APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>Alderman JAMES EVANS, in moving the adoption of e minutes of the Health Commitee, asked the Council to defer nny discussion upon the Medical Officer's annual report to especial Council meeting. The Health Committee had some important matters of an urgent character to br:ng forward. One had reference to the provision of sanatoria under the Insurance Act, and the other dealing with tuberculosis generally. The com- mittee would have two alterr ative reports to consider and their recommendations would be submitted at an early date. A deputation consisting of the Mayor, the Medical Officer, and himself had attenaed three meet- ings r t St. Helens to consider dealing with thesanatoria etion on joint lines. They had also considered the question fron. their own ttandpoint, and in all pro- bability theMenlth Committee would fret it imperative to seek the sanction of the Lcc d Government Board to a scheme under the Act. Alderman BURTON seconded.</p>
        <p>COW:Tint t ROBERTS drew attention to the proposed .ppointment of an assistant medical officer to succeed Dr. idutt, who had resigned in order to take up an appointment at Brighton. Ile said that the new doctor's salary had be, n apportioned one-eixth to the Health Commit.eo and five-siatbs to the Education Committee. On the principle of those who paid the piper having the right to call the time, the proportions should be reversed. He claimed on behalf of the Fducali, n Com- mittee a voice in the appointment. lithe Health Com- mittee would not agree to mmsultation on the question they should leave the Education Committee to appoint their own medical officer, for the time had arrived when the full services of a medical man were required for the important work of medical inspntion in the schools. Councillor PEMBURTON Said it bad already been decided by the Council that the Health Committee should have control of the appointment. There had been a big fight on the question, but the Town Clerk had repeatedly ruled that the Thwn Council was the authority with regard to the spending of money. The arrangement as to payment was not inequitable. Counci:lor BROA DU URST Said the discussion WS S "much ado about nothing." He did not see why the Education Committee should wish to trespass upoa the functions of the Health Committee.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said that with ' regard to the financial question, it was a case of robbing Peter to pay Paul. As to the principle, it seemed more convenient and equitable th‘it health matters should be kept entirely in the hands of the Health Committee. In reply to Alderman Bennett, the Mavoß protnised that he would convene a special Council meeting to discuss the Medical Offioer's report. The minutes were then approved. THE LAYING OUT OF' NEW STREETS. At a meeting of the Paving and Sewerage Committee it was resolved that the following setts be purchased : —2,000 tons from Messrs Clare and Ridgway, Ltd., from Ratho Quarry ; 1,000 tins from Messrs D H atd J Newell, Dalbeattie ; 500 tons from Messrs Clare and Ridgway, Ltd., Gourock Quarry.—A resolution from the Health Committee with refererce to the emission of black smoke from the chimney at the compressor station, Mersey-street, stag read and insttuctions given thereon.—Consent v‘as reLeed to the erection of a telegraph pole on the footpath in Museum-street.—It was resolved that the attention of the Street Improve- ment C xn-t ittee be drawn to their resolution of 17th December, 1908, svith regard to the laying out of new streets, and that they be strongly urged to see that the provisiot s of Section 500! the Warrington Corpora- tion Act, 1809, are, in all cases, strictly complied with. Aldern an TINNioN, in moving the adoption of the minutes, drew special attention to the request to the Street Improvement. Committee, lie said the position they were liable t .) be placed in was a very unfortunate one a times. The Street Improvement Committee had the , approval of the plans submitted ty builders for the erection of property and the formation of streets. The duty of the raving rod Sew etage Committee was to pave those streets as they found them. It had COMP to their Inowledge that some plans which had teen passed by the Street improvement Com- , mittee were cot satisfactory. Difficulties of a grave character had arisen end in order to obviate them in the future it was absolutely necessary that the resolution with reference to the laying out of new streets should be rigidly adhered to. He believed that net on one single occasion, or only on very few, had the Street Improvement Committee carried out that resolution, lie hoped that in future the committee would not make fish of one and flesh of enotber but would insist on the resolution being adhered to in its entirety.</p>
        <p>SCAVENGERS' WAGES. Alderman TIN!,TION called attention to the fact that the wages of eight scavengers had been increased from 21s to 225. They were not, strictly spet.king, able- bodied men, but they did their work exceedingly well and were entitled to some consideration. Councillor PEMBERTON seconded. Councillor DICKENSON: Have all scavengers who have not been raistd to 245. been raised to 22s ? Alderman TINNION replied in the negative. " May I say " he added " in the interests of those whose wages have not bona increased, that I hope the matter will not be pressed. They are getting 215." Tbe minutes were passed. THE NEW BOWLING GREENS. On the minutes of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committ,e, Councillor Broadhurst asked if there wars any possibility of the bowling green on the Sankey Bridges recreation ground being opened for play this summer ? The people living in that district were grateful f,r what the committee had done but they ' would like to be able to make use of the green as soon as possible. Councillor BIRILES said a deputation had been appointed to visit the new bowling greens and As the dates of opening. He did not think the Liverpool. road green was sufficiently " set " to be ready for play- ing purposes. Councillor DICKENSON soil bowling experts had con. damned the idea of opening the green for some time,aa the result might be to ruin it for years to come. GAS STOVES OR GRILLS? -</p>
        <p>----- • On the minutes of the Gas Committee being submitted Conceitlor LAbIIFORD asked whether it was true that the gas officials were removing stoves and putting in grill] without notifying the tenants of the houses. He produced a copy of a letter sent to the chairman, asking the department not to remove a certain stove. The MAYOR : You bhould have given notice of this question. Councillor LASTIFORD: I did not know. I only got the letter this morning. Alderman JOHN EVANS : If Councillor Lashford had applied to me before the Council meeting I should have been glad to give him some explanation. lam not prepared now. I don't think it is fair that a question like this should be sprung upon me.</p>
        <p>fHE BRIDGES. HOW THE WORK IS PROGRESSING.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST, in moving the adoption of the minutes of the Bridges Committee, referred to the progress of the work on the new Warrington Bridge. the contractors had new completed the training wall on the Cheshire side with the exception of the ooping stone, and the wall on the Lancashire side was expected to be finished in about a fortnight. They were also driving the piles for the main bridge structure. He hoped to see one half the bridge opened this year and one of tha engineers had assured him that was not impossible. With regard to Howley Bridge there had been a long delay and some fighting about the lend required and other questions. The had now got the laud, the foundations had been prepared and the standards were being built. He hoped the bridge would be ready for opening on the occasion of the great fair in Victoria Park in July. The MaYou seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pembertm said the greatest progress at Warrington Bridge seetsed to be made in e training walls which were only light in structure. He would like to see something done tossards getting the bridge itself across the water.</p>
      </div>
