        <p>GOOD TAILORING AT CHEAPER PRICES</p>
        <p>Clothes that Fit, cut to th joint of distinction, in style that only London produces. Such i the Tailetir g that is to Le obtained at 'SUITS LTD," Jubiie, Buildings, Horse.narket St,</p>
        <p>Warrington. Why not be well dreEsed ? SUITS LTD." have made it possible for you to enjoy the wearing of good Clothes at reduced pr'crs. Have your new suit London cut! It will cost you less, and you will appear co much more no-to-date in it. Save money, and also lead the fashion in your locality. SUITS and RAINPROOF OVERCOATS, 20/- aid upwards; TROUSERS from 5/1 1 . Call for Patterns. SUITS LTDI, Jubilee Buildings Borsemarket Street, Warrington</p>
        <p>The Grey Blcuse.—Tte blouse was the pioneer of the re idy-to-n ear stiles, that every seasrn become more extensive in range, more highly finished and more u^e- fol. Tho prevalence of every shade of gni is very marked in the in the West end shops, and stores of London and Paris, more espscially on those blouses cut on shirt lines. A very pleasing model that would lend itself to home confection was in a slft plain material of a pale shade. It had no yoke, but three wide tucks that reached the armhole on either side of the front end four tucks behind of the same width. lbese tucks wet° each stitched three tinn aat the t dge, with a washing silk of a deeper shade of grey, and the double frill of the material was Land embroidered in the same colour. Anther pally pr tt y model had narrower tinstitched tucks, with a simple design embroidered in a deep shade between each, the collar nnd cuffs being treated in the same way. Quaint Dresses for Childree.—The dresses of quite little children always re fl-)ct the fashions of grownups. It is not surprising therefore that with so many revivals of old-word models, the little ones are dressed in styles that 11 ,unshed in the times of their great great grand- mothers. Very quaint irdmd are the small girls' dresses for this season, but tl ey are serncw hat too befrilled and too fus y, also in some instances they are made of trateti‘ls that are itoo rich for children. Simplicity should be the keynote of the dress of the yoen%. Serge and soft silk, simply made, being suit- able for the nurse y and sch3olraom. About Bullies —The popularity of the ruffle still waxes, but it shoal,' not he wora hy women who have short necks, or by those who are high-shouldered. Its effect is greatly enhanced when it matches, or harmonises with the hat. A runt) of blue tulle for instance goes well with a yellow Dunstablo 'trim bat, so does one of white r .inproof tulle, very fi mly pleated, emhellidied with tiny black velvet or satin li)wer3, worn with a white hat trimmed with black.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June, 8, 1912.</p>
