      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Duchess of Sutherland find Social Unrelt.—The Duchess of Sutherland, in her presidential raft:tress to the Association f Teachers of Domestic Subjects, at Batt, raea Polytechnic, referred to the prevalent condi- tions of social unrest. "We are living in a most difficult age," she said. "Everybody wanted something they had not got. She wanted a cottage, others wanted a castle." The Duchers's idea of a cottage is probably much the same as her daugher's, who once said to her, " I suppose, mother, if ever the Socialists did seize Stafford House, we sloculd have to live in one of those cottagey houses in Grosvenor-spare." Many of the ." cottagey " houses referred to cauld not be bought for 1:10,000, but ro doubt they appear insignificant, as viewed from Stafford House, in St. James's, with its gilded talons, and marble halls end galleries, which are not excelled even in Buckingham Palace. The Duchess attributes the general state of unrest and discontent to the eclu:ationsl movement of late Victorian times, when she said, " we passed through a time of foolishness in the education hue and ill fed, ill developed families, and many faded and disappointed spinsters were left to tell the sad t‘le of incompetence and ignorance." The Duchess recalls th e time when girls were taught the domestic arts, and to weave, spin, and embroider, but if social salvation depends upon returning to the more primitive conditions of life, in olden times, we may be sure we she; ll never find it. Alexandra Day.— VVet have already several floral fet3 days, and it is prop-sed to aid to the number by observing June 2fit.h, as Alexin Ira Day. It is close upon ,fifty years since the youthful Danish Princess came to this country, to be the bride of the future King Edward, and had his Majesty lived, he and Queen Alexandra would next March have reached that rare event in Royal—at well as in ordinary lives—their golden wedding. That unhappily, was not to be, but it is proposed to commemorate in s nue appropriate way, the long residence of Queen Alexandra in this country and the close and deep attachment that, from the first, Las always exittad between her, and the nation. An artificial wild rose, which is to be sold in the streets of London on June I:fith, is to he the emblem of Alexandra Day. By permission of the Commissioners of Police 10,000 ladies, attired in %bite and wearing hats trimmed with the II wcr of the day, will be engaged in selling the roses, wh:ch ere heing made at Groom's Cripplesge, and a ill thus hem fit an excellent charity. The main object of the festival is to help philanthropic and charitable institutions, and many well-known women, including the Countess of Milton, Mrs. Lowther, wife of the speaker of the House of Commons, and Miss Marie Corelli, are taking an active interest in the movenunt. The whole of the prelimin- ary organization has been carried out by women, and although it will be something of an experiment on this occasion, it is hoped to make Alexandra Day an annual celebration throughout the British Isles. The Breakfast Table Newspaper.—The amenities of the family breakfast table are often a little difficult. It is not a meal to encourage cordi el social relations, and it is often urged that it would save a good deal of friction and temper, if the formal breakfast was abolished. We all know what usually happen,. The masculine head of the household takes the newspaper, and props it up against the cruet, lie then proceeds to collect within easy reach all that, he is likely to require for the meal, and lapses into stlence. If he is Token to he replies in monosyllables, which become more or less inarticulate, if the interruptions of his resding the newspaper are anything more than few and far between That in very many households comprises his contribu- tion to the family stock of cmversation, and it is apt, to become a little oppressive, not to say aggravating at times. The only remedy seems to be to provide each member of the family with a newspaper. As a rule, mater-families caunot rut up her own breakfast table barrier, because tber • is no second newspaper. If women cannot have votes, they can at least have newspapers, which at times might be useful as an aid to harmony, in breakfasting in company. Blouses and Illazers.—For tennis, golf, and sports generally, the lot se plain blouse is in the best taste, and is the most comfortable. In ivory China silk, or some soft washing material, simply cut, a suitable embellishment where this is desired, is found in French knots, that nays r go out of fashion. The blazer has formed a part of the eportieg wardrobe for some time, and it certainly is the smartest of span coats. Last year it was almost goner Illy adopter', with the result that manufacturers have paid special attention to the increasing demand—and this years goods are quite fascinating, and a great improvement in make and style. In Scotch homespues in grey and green, and in heather mixtures, they are very attractivewhilst the more pronounced stripes in black and white, green and. gold, red and purple, cannot fail to please the most fastidious sportswoman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>HINT TO LADIES.</head>
        <p>A HINT TO LADIES.</p>
        <p>..</p>
        <p>In order that sulfuring women, of any age, may be able to obtain the remedy which has given relief to thousands, Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound t is now paced at the disposal of all chemists, who either keep in stock or obtain it for enquirers in a day cr so, at 2s. 01 or a very large bottle for 43. GI It has La years been known in Mrs. Pinkham's own neighbourhood that every description of ailments peculiar to women are scoled and healed by this harmless herbal mixture ; but owing to the impossibility of communicating with all sufferers directly, Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Com- pound has now bona made an article ot regular sale, and can be had post paid at the above price f,oin The Lady Manager, c/o Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 12a, Hampstead Road, London, N.W. Mrs. Pinkham's admirable book of private knotvledgs for women free cf any charge whatever 03 application to the same addrees.</p>
        <p>BROTHERHOOD AND SOCIAL WRONG. MR. Alt THUR HENDERSON AT SOUTHPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur liendersor, M.P., speaking at Southport, on Monday on " Brotherhood and Social Obligations," said that nothing more wholesome could take place in the tratiAn's life than the Christianising of the democracy and tin democratising of Christianity. The tragedy of social wrong continur d to cry aloud to God for a remely. What was to be the attitude 'of the great Brotherhood movement ? Many even in the Brotherhood movement were more ready to insist upon their rights Owl to accept their responsibilities. Ile believed that the present state of affair 3 was due to the acceptance of the theory that the present social extremes wsre inevitable and indispensable That doctrine might do for an indifferent man of the world, but it was not comistent with the doctrine of Christian brotherhood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>L 035 OF THE "TITANIC."</head>
        <p>THE L 035 OF THE "TITANIC."</p>
        <p>The great ship sailed across the tranquil sea Ablaze with light, And reared her sumptuous decks deflattly Uuto the night. "f wo thousand souls were in her keeping—these gl.t sleep at core ; The stars sailed over them through heavenly seas Serene and pure. The winds were drowsing, and no ripple stirred' The ccoan's breast ; Men laughed and feasted ; Music's voice was heard, Its spell confessed. But from the North with stealthy feet there crept A phantom bark, To meet the shining ship that doomward swept, Amid the dark. No sign or sound it made ; all unaware, The night stole on : Until the look-set saw it looming there, An iceberg lone. Swift sped the message to the men below ; The great ship swerved ; But deep that calm and treacherous sea below, Its chill feet curved, And like some hidden demon of the sea Her bosom tore ; And through the rent the waters silently Bsgan to pour. What mattered ? for she scam ly felt the shook So strong was she ; " Unsinkable," this ship was built to muck The envious sea. But soon the icy deluge in the ho'd Drove mirth away From deck to deck there crept a whisper cold, And then dismay Succeeded jesting, and the boats wera lowered— The woman first, The children after them—and still there poured That stream accursed Down to the roaring furnaces where some Their task still plied, Their brave limbs drenched with the encroaching foam, While far and wide Across the shuddering night dim word was sent Of peril dire, And great ships heard it, tuned their helms, and went With hearts on fire, Through the ice-haunted sea, and sudden speed, That danger spurned, To help their drowning brothers in their need, While rockets burned Like I relent meteors in the heedless sky, But burned in vain ; The pale stars answered not, and no reply Flashed o'er the main. And new the imminent horror to men's eyes, With chilling stare, Came creeping slowly, but no coward cries Disturbed the air. The life-belts bad been given out one by one; For the last leap Men girded up their loins; the boats were gone; And still the deep Crawled nearer like some dark and cruel dream, And still men stood Unmoved, and watched the mocking starlight gleam, For in their blood There rang the captain's cry, "Be British !" while Sweet music stole Over the waste of waters to beguile The affrighted soul With visions of Heaven's nearness, and to soothe Their fears away, And make the night-black waters strangely smooth With beams of day. The great ship dipped, and, in the moonless air, Plunged darkly down As up to God a low wail of despair Was faintly blown Across the corpse-strewn distance to the boats That shuddering heard, And felt Death clutching at their very throats, And scarcely stirred. Into that Dead Sea, as the morning neared, At last there came A vessel through the icy darkness, steered ' By men whose fame Shall evermore be Inked with those who faced Death unafraid, And on their brows such laurels shall be placed As shall not fade. As long as England rules the impetuous sea The captain's word Shall be remembered, and the melody The sad boats heard As the great ship plunged doomwards, and the twain Who, to the end, Their cull for succour cut across the main Still strove to send. Our over-confidence we mutely mourn ; Our wealth and pride Were less than nothing on that awful morn To those who died. But those who live such moving things may learn From deeds like these A 3 make t'-es hearts of men within them burn For centuries. mintutt BENNETT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</head>
        <p>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</p>
        <p>GOVERNMENT'S PROGRESSIVE WORK.</p>
        <p>REPLIES TO TARIFF REFORM ARGUMENTS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Progressive Labour League held another highly successful open-air meeting near Arplf y Cannons on Monday evening. There was a large attendance and the speeches were received with I enthusiasm. Mr. J. Elwell presided and the principal speaker was Council!. .r J. Kendall, of Manchester. The CHAIRMAN said the members of the League were convinced that the policy for which they stood was in the best interests of the workers and of the country generally. At the meetings to be held weekly during the summer months they would not only have old truths to present but they would review the record of the present Government which be claimed surpassed that of any modern Government, so far as labour legislation was concerned. The speaker then turned his attention to the current, isstm of the monthly organ of the Women's Unionist and Tariff Reform League and said he had been rruch amused at some of its contents. Speaking stout trade returnn, writers in that j-urnal said it was not right that in estimat- ing exports, values and nct guar tit'es shculd he taken into account, bit when they were referring to imports thy adopted the opposite I rgtiment. They said that the raw material imported into this country last sear was £13,000,000 less in ame mat ti an in 1910. "lbat was in value., but the tariff reformers did not tell us that more than one half of that amount was due to the itflated price of robber alone. Win n they came to look at otter articles, they fount that the decrease in imports of raw ma'erials bad been very little, even in values as well as in quantities. Last sear was a vcord for imports of raw material, will, the exception of the year 1910, so lie did n thiuk there was rutmh to complain about on that tcore, rsvcially when tht y consider( d how the strikes that, occurred last year would 51T3rt both imports and exports. Ile noticed another artitle in the journal I y " wonit MAN JIM."</p>
        <p>(Laughter.) The enter was referring to mien)! 1 yment and the peculiar thing about it was that i n the year 101 ','o prove that un oyrnent •xi he bad t go hart% to luuB Bit he did not give the relative tipires with rejar 1 to ot ier countries Ia 1903. In America unemplo3 non! was over 31 per cent, and the prudtic ion inoreputicu- lar trade went down te o-fifths oilhe total, the pig ir produced in America being 15,000,000 tons instead ot 25,000,(0C tens. '1 hey t It or that in 1000 . Demi 1 liy- ment in this country vas2', per cint. hut that in 1903 it had risen to It p r cent. But v hat a difference there was betweNt It ant 31 par ce t ! In Am, ric‘, where they had Protection and all its bleisings,tinsmplo3 maw in 1508 was :31 per cent, a• d only last wi ek he taw that while trade ryes booming in this coultry and there w. re comparatively few unemplayert, it was reported that vv.!. 31 per ceut. weir) uncmployed in America. (Applause). Councill-r KENDA I L said Conservative literature, Ike Conservative policy, was very barren. Never in the !lister, of that great part) had thera b:ien a time when its policy was so thin and poor as at the present momei t. Someti C 3 when be found some of his Liberal friends getting a bit nervous about the speed at which the Government was travelling, he asked them : Suppose you quarrelled with the Liberal and progres- sive pity, whe e would you go to ? " Most of them said "We should have to chin k ' p it cs altogstler." It would be impossible for them to go over to the Conserve lye party and be content to do nothing. On the other hand there were people who wanted to accelerate the speed at which the Liberal party etas travelling, but between ths two ha thought they would continue to maintain in power a Govern tient which had shown true statesmanship and had procee led with a definite pol:cy by steady steps, from position to pus' - don. helping forward all the time the interests of the great mos es of the people in this country. (App'ause ) Referring to</p>
        <p>THE LAB DR TROUBLES, Conon:110r Kendall s tid that never in the history of the world was there a period of such groat unrest as when the Founder of the Christian re ligion was on earth. The reason for the unrest was th,- t on te onehand they bad Immense wealth and on the other interre poverty, the rich man referred to in the parable, and the poor man lying at his gate. That was the problem which set the greatest Social Reformer the world had ever known,not only with His tonguo but with His hands to denoui,c2 the unfair accumulation of wealth and the unfair con- ditions of the misses of the people. To-day there vies a danger of those conditions being intensified, and it was a mat' er of intens i gratification that the masses were striking for br t'er conditions not only for themselves but for the generations that were to follow them ( Ipp'ause . For generations 40,000,000 of the E-glish people had been allow lug a small section of 5,000,000 practically to say wbat their conditions of life should be. If those 5,000,000 had said " Your or nditions shall be as good as ours," there would have been no unrest to-day. It was because the 4(1,000,000 bad at last realized the power that there was behind that accumulated force and vote that they were rising is protest end trying to make their conditions bitter. fie h2lieved that uprising would be well guided and directed ; he had faith in the British workingmen if they would use their power well, that they would eventually secwe a fairer share of this worles good fur the work they were doing. (Applause.) They ought to ray their tribute to the Government for the efforts they were making to bring together the opposite forces. Sometimes their Socialist friends got impatient because the GJvernment were not doing more, but it was hard work to begin. Mr. Lloyd George had been doing a bit. (Applause.) Hai they seen what certain Lancashire parson had been saying about him ? (Laughter.) A Christian minister, forsooth, preaching the gospel of hatred, when he would find in St. Luke's gospel that he was told to love his enemies. He said " I bate the devil but I hate Lloyd George more." (Laughte-.) When a Government applied itself to social reform, as this Government had done since 1006, they w toted all the support and help they could get (rim the masses of the people for whom they were fighting. (Appian' o ) They had opposed to them the</p>
        <p>ORGANISED FORCES OF WEALTII AND PRIVILEGE</p>
        <p>and it was neceesary that those Who sympthised with their progressive pclicy should give them active and whole-hearted support. Ile hoped the Governmett would be able to establish in the interesis of th. workers, POMO better machinery than the crude and out- of-date method of strikes and lock-outs. (Hear, hear.) Referring to Home Rule, the tpeaker said their opponents said it would be " the end of all things," but their predictions with regard to other progressive measures had been so completely falsified, that their present warnings could not be taken seriously. A few months ago the Tory party said they were going to organize a great ants-H ,rno Rule campaign. They were going to bring over some of the liberal-minded Ulster men who were PO anxious to commit suicide— (laughter)—and send them all over the country in order to rouse up the Nonconformist and Christian element. What had actually happened ? fbey had switched hick on to the insurance Act. (Laughter.) Home Rule was merely a kite flyer, and when they got it up they found the air did not suit, and they bad to haul it in again. (Laughter.) He Nal proud to think that he as a Liberal would have the pleasure of saying that • a Government which he bad supported, had dune</p>
        <p>JUSTICE TO IRELAND, justice which, if it had been done years ago, would bare saved many an exile, the breaking uo of many homes and the breaking of many a heart in that Island. We had trusted every other part of our Empire. They bad separate governments, ell loyal to the linglis`t Throne and Crown, and Ireland was the only place which had not. been allowed to apply the principle of trusting the people. From the record a Irishmen in ether covntriss, where they bad proved themselves statesmen of the flrit rank, he believed that, with all the love and loyalty which they possessed, they would make a brilliant success of the g3vernment of their own lmd. The speaker next touched upon</p>
        <p>WELSH DISESTABLISIIMENT, He said that wherever they had frrei religion from the trammels of State control, they had strengthened religion. The Church in Vales, if it was wcrth anything, would be stronger when Disestablishment had hero carried—as carricd it word I be, in spite of the narrow- minded bigotry which had been shown. They must not be frightened by hysterical m ople who went about threatening the downfall of England if they tampered with old-time Establishments. Personally he hopsd they would come to the same thing in England before long, and that religion would have a free chance, that those who professed religion should, without any privilege, Et at en level and fair terms. When the history of Nonconformity was read, those who were religiously-minded need have no fear that religion would not make headway when it was freed from State control. We were also to have "ONE MAN, ONE VOTE." That was promised absolutely and the Government had not broken any promises so far—in fact they bad not only kept their own promises hut other people's as well (Laughter). At last the workingman was going to be put on a level, as far ea voting power was concerned, with the wealthiest man in the land. Toe Government would no doubt also include provisions to purify elections. Reform of the Poor Law was also coming. some manufacturers were kicking up a noise about the Insurance Act because it was g ring to c)st them some- thing. Ile wanted to euggest, however, that when the Act was in full operation and when the Poor Law had becn reformed—as it would be on the basis of the report which was under considera- tion when the I stirrup° Act was brought in—there was going to be a saving to the manufacturers which would more than counterbalance the cost of the Insur - ence Act. One-third of the poverty with which the Guardians bad to deal was the result of sickness among the workers, and that would be lifted off their hands. He hoped the time would come when there would not be a workhouse left ;Applause ) Surely one could manage to invent a system whereby old people and those who broke down should have some bettt r provision than the Workhouse gave. By granting old age peusions and passing the Insurance Act, which provided egainst sick- ness from sixteen years of age upwards, and also iaaugurated insurance against unemployment,the Govern</p>
        <p>ment had begun a great rnoveme t:rt the workers of the country out of the poverty wuwu had existed under P. or Law administration for generations p st. When that reform came about thero would be many millions more to devote, without any sacrifice, to still further benfltting the people. Finally the speaker said the time would come when no man a ha called himself a working- min would be rtmh a fool as to ally h:mself with the interests which had always stood in the way of his pro- gress. (Applause). Mr M LLEWELLYN proposed a vote of thanks to Councillor Kendall and the Chairman. lb o expressed satisfaction that the norkingraan was beginning to throw off his apaihy and to realise his own worth and power, lie was not going to be dominated any longer by those who held great wealth. (Applause). Mr. VOSE seconded and the vote was heartily passed. Councillor KENDALL, in responding, said a large number of people who possessed excessive wealth realized that it carried great respensibilities. There were at the present time supporting the Government some of the rte.-rest men in the country—men who were devoting their eolith t ) bettering the conditions ot the people and were supporting legislation which was going to cost them something, but taking it aa a privilege that they wore able to contribute in that direction, lie hoped that by voluntary genial legisla- tion we would be able to p-event what Lapp. ned 100 years ago in France when ihe masses f the p-ople, , deprived of their rights, rose up and tocurrd them by force. We were securing those rights in this c untry by peaceful means and steady progress along the la ell- ordered path Gf reform. (Applause).</p>
        <p>THE TR.\ NSTI</p>
        <p>Remarkable seencs e!e itr essed outside 1 Leadenhall-street this week, when, in answer to tn at the decks, huudreds besieged the building an considertble distance down the road.</p>
