        <head>FIRE BRIGADE CRITICS.</head>
        <p>FIRE BRIGADE CRITICS.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER COUNCIL DEBATE.</p>
        <p>ARE THE APPLIANCES UP-TO-DATE.</p>
        <p>The cpiest'on of the up-to-dateness and efficiency of the Corporation Fire Brigade's equipment eas again under discu sihn at the meeting of ,the Warrire.ton Town Commit on Tuesday evening. It was reported that at a meeting of the Fire Engine Committee the following resolution, unanimously passed by the War- rington Chamber of Commerce was read : That in view of the recent disastrous fire at the premises of Messrs. Cartons Limited, the War.ington Chamber of Commerce respectfully iriggesta to the Fire Engine Committee the desirability of obtaining a n otcr engine and of taking any other necessary steps to bring the whole equipment absolutely up-tc-date." It was resolved that the Borough Surveyor be instrected to obtain information, and report fully on the matto r. Alderman 13trwroe, in moving the adoption of the minutes, referred to the suggestion of the ihamber of Commerce as to the desirability of obtaining a motor fire engine. The question had been under the considera- tion of the Committee for some time rind was still under consideration. The brigade had at preezent two very good engines and they were kept in good repair. One of them threw 500 odious of water per minute and there were few engines which could throw a larger volume of water. Tne engine was a good sad useful one, while all the appliances of the brigade were I opt ie money. They were fitted throughout with iostantant ous gheosotd brae, npda-iwr oa ev eduu. pa-to de -bd ti te. e r cTohuel d hn) ()set- it e eh owuegrbet tfohe r crecrauephleindetsh, esosctheantn mr), ft me wfiersel.lvinthhernegatbrde btorigathdee I personnel of the brigade, it was acknowledged through- out 1/lncashir3 that they wore a very smart end capable sat. As a prof of that, they had had many letters of commendation from per ph) who had had occasion to use their services and who spoke highly of the efficient way in which the fires had been dealt with. There was no C 8115,0 for lear on the part of the Chamber of Commerce or anybody else. At the last meeting of the Council,Councillor l'etnberten said he had known from twelve to fifteen minutes to elapse before the horses arrived at the fire station. Ile (the speaker) was not in a position to refute the asser- tion, hut he bad been connected with the brigade for fourteen sears and no such delay had yet come under his notice or under ti at of the committee. Perhaps Councillor Pemberton's observations were rather ancient and referred to the time when the boll AVJ I hung in the market place and the superintendent bad to run there to ascertain where the fire ruan, before any- thing could be done. The F.re Station ass now con- nected with the telephone, with a single wire to the 6t tbles, so that the moment there was an alarm tbe brigade could get to work. With regard to Garton's fire the call was received at 6.10 by the superintendent, and at 6.13 the fl at engine and equipment had left, the station. Ile was pleased that the Water Committee was making great eff o ti to improve tho supply of water for fire purposes, tanks having been 'placed in various parts of the town. A practice was recently held in the neighbourdood of School-brow and a very ample supply of water was forthcoming. Councillor littoAnn uttsT seconded. He said that unfortunately he WIIS not present alien Councillor Pemberton made his n ruarks at the last meeting. Alderman TINNWN : Very fortunate for him. (Laughter). Councillor lirtoAnucesT : Ile would have heard the length and breacrth of my tonguN (Latighter). The Mavort : That is not ou the minutes. (Laughter). Councillor 11110.tuittritsT : It corms) s a wrong impression to the eub'ic. Continuing, he said be made a special visit to the fire station that afternoon when an alarm was givut, and the engine and horses were on the way to the outbreak in less than four minutes. The Warriugtr n Fire Brigade compared very favourably with any voluntary brigade in the country. (Dear, hear). The resolution of the Chamber of Commerce would appear to imply that their appliances were out of date, whereas the only thing that was not up-to-date was the old reel cart which had now been smashed up. There was cnly one instance he could recall of the brig ide not turning out promptly and that was when a false alarm ens given. (Laughter). Alderman BENNETT said the resolution of the Chamber of Commerce had been sorneehat misunder- stood. It was not meant in any sense to be taken as a vote of censure on the Eire Engine Committee. They all recognized th It the committee did admirable work, that the men were plucky end got through their work quickly and well, and that on the whole they dealt with fires in a satisfac'ory manner. But the Chamber of Commerce, of all bodies in the town, had a right to speak on the q lration. There was scarcely a firm of importance that was not connected wi h it, and it represented al enormous amount of property. The fact as it appeared to that organization vt as : Here was a new works, presumably up-to.date in every particu- lar,but in spite of the magnificent and ellicitnt services of the br.gade, the fi e got out of bounds in a few minutes and th 3 whole pace was absolutely gritted. VI hat happened to Garten's might happen to any works in the town. Alderman TtestoN : There is not another works built like it. Alderman Beeeel r, continuing sail the Chamber of Commerce felt that if, in spite of the brigade's appli- ances, it was possible for a works of that inipmtanoe to be so cemphtely gutted in so short a time, it behoved them and every intelligent citizen to see that there was n p rash le leopholein the equipment and that evers thing which wihd )rn, ingenuity and fcresight could devise should be done to prevent the recurrence of such a ccntlagration. Ihe rcr.,olution was not in any s,:mse t so o wouldnn A rub, e u t o s T h : v a y eou thew meant to be a reflec.ion on the brigade, ,he lad sometimes thought that in a town of the size of War r:ngton it would be better to base a