      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>As will be observed Ir om our advertisement columns on Sunday next (to-morrow), Dedication Festival Services will be held at St. John's Presbyterian Church, Wilderspool Causeway. The preacher, morning and evening, will be the Rev J Pringle, S.D., of St. Helens. In the afternoon at 2.45, the choir is giving a rendering of the sacred cantata "Day and night," by H Ernest Nichol. The choir will be under the leadership of Mr A G Lewsey, and the soloists are Mrs G C Miller, soprano, and Mr J Mc'Arthur, bass.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sunday presided over by Mr. Farrar. Mr. Eli James gave the address upon "God's servants and God's care for them." He spoke of the joy of services, the need for loyal and constant efforts for God and man. Whether our talents were few or many, we could put them to use for the Master. There was honour and blessings upon the smallest service Tendered when offered in the Master's name. Mr. McEachern read the lesson. Mr. A. Healey led the prayer. The Chairman remaiuded the meeting of the open meeting next Sunday and thefpresentation to the Rev. J. Yonge, the President on his retirement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Primitive Methodist Veteran's Retirement.</head>
        <p>Primitive Methodist Veteran's Retirement.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. H. Hunt, pastor of the Primitive Methodist Church at Runcorn, retired on Wednesday, aged seventy years, and will live at Southport. He was born at Hatton, Cheshire, in 1842, joined the Primitive Methodists when fifteen years old, became a local preacher as a boy, and entered the ministry fifty years ago. He spent five years in Oldham,eight years in Man- chester, eight years in Leeds, seven years in Liverpool, four years in Bolton.and four years in Runcorn. He has held practically all the important Connexional appoint- ments. Fcfr five years he was general Sunday school secretary, ten year general temperance secretary, and twenty-five years editor of the " Primitive Methodist World," In 1907 he was the Morse lecturer, delivering the lecture on Sunday school reform in connection with the triennial conference. Mr. Hunt was secretary of the conference in 1892, and president in 1903. He was joint secretary of the Primitive Methodist Centen- ary Evangelistic Co,^mittee, and had charge of the great centenary demonstration on Mow Cop. He has written voluminously on the temperance question, and beaded a deputation to the Prime Minister on the Licensing Hill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Honour for a Local Minister.</head>
        <p>Honour for a Local Minister.</p>
        <p>The Rev J J Glover, Minister of Risley Presbyterian Church, end formerley of Blackburn, has just been elected Moderator of the Manchester Presbytery, the district of which includes Risley and Warrington. Mr. Glover, in his speech from the chair, acknowledging his election by the Presbytery, said he in no way coveted the honour, but he accepted it gladly for the sake of the old Risley Church, which was this year celebrating its 250th. anniversary. Mr. Glover paid a high tribute to the loyalty of the Presbyterians at Risley, and to their interest in the work of the church shown in tangible form. He spoke of the importance of the country churches, claiming that they deserved well of the Church as a whole, as they were constantly recruiting the town and city congregations. Mr. Glover, who expressed the hope that curing his year of office as Moderator he would show he was not unworthy of the confidence the Presbytery had reposed in him, is the first minister of the Risley congregation to be called to the Moderator's chair by the Presbytery of Manchester. Mr. Glover is very well-known in Warrington, and is excedingly popular with the Presbyterian congregation at St. John's where he has often preached. He rendered yeoman service to the Presbyterian cause locally when the same was at a very low ebb, and during the days the small remnant of the congregation worshipped in Cairo Street School Mr. Glover did much to Inspire hope and confidence in the future. Subsequent events have more than justified his then hopeful outlook. Needless to say Mr. Glover's many Werrington friends have heard with much pleasue of the honour conferred upon him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Social Idealism. Sermon by the Rev. Dr. Mellor.</head>
        <p>Social Idealism. Sermon by the Rev. Dr. Mellor.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. Stanley A. Mellor preached at Cairo- street Chapel on Sunday evening on " Religion and the Social Ideal.' He sato that in these days it became more and more difficult for any man,no matter what his private circumstances might be, not to be alive and awake to the demands and the needs of the wider social whole. The power with which we had to reckon in the world to-day was the power of the social ideal. What men everywhere desired to do was to make society better, more endurable, more fit for good, strong, honest, upright, decent men and women to live in. There was little place in the world at the moment for the man or woman who was not awake and alive to that fact ; ifs man was not, in some way or other, engaged in the effort to break down the oid and build society anew, then, as far as the real life of the world w a concerned, that man was of no account. The men who really were of account in the world at the moment were those revolutionary and discontented spirits who were so passionately in love with the ideal that they could not bear the sight of anything that stood in the way of tbat ideal and would infinitely rather see the universe in ruins than submit to a continuance of things as they are. There was no department of human activity and no institution of human society that could escape contact with that new power of social idealism. With- out the sanctity of religion, that power was doomed to 1 failure. Unless the social ideal found a home in the hearts of men who were, personally and individually, fit to represent and care for it, what force or value could there be in it? It was a longing for righteous- ness, for justice, for good, for happiness, for joy—and,it must go nomelets in the world unless it found a home in hearts for whom those things had a vivid, personal, religious meaning. In a careless, dissolute man, dis- content had no value, idealism was but shamed. Revolt was useless unless it be carried out by men who, in themselves and of themselves, were fit for revolutionit was the personal element in the end that was of account, and in the warfare of the world at the moment religion alone could inspire and fortify that depth of personality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>Special Interest was centred in the Sunday school anniversary services held at,this church on Sunday. the preacher being the Rev. H. G. Roberts, of Sheffield, formerly of Warrington. Many old friends of the rev. gentleman were pleased to have the opportunity of renewing their acquaintance with him, and there were large congregations to listen to his eloquent sermons. In the morning his subject was " Grace" which, he said, was better than knowledge or righteousness, for righteousness without grace had no mtbic in it, no delight, no lustre. Thnre was urgent need to-day to cultivate the gracious disposition. It was disappoint- ing that there should be in the Church of Christ those whose society was anything but a charm, although they were teen of strong principles. He knew men who scrupulously observed the minutest details, but who had the foolish idea that to be upright in character they must be downright rude in speech— men who by their general behaviour on committees and I in other walks in life never reminded one of the charms and the beauties of Jesus Christ, and he had come to the conclusion that the beat of men were outside the Churches. He knew men who were exceedingly con- scientious, but in their presence he felt be was at the assizes. lie urged his hearers to endeavour to grow in grace, to have a wider sympathy, to be more gentle, more ready to forgive, more 1 )vable, more charming. At the evening service the rev. gentleman preached on "The supreme importance of religion" and said the worst thing that could come to their Church was not poverty or heresy, but inertia. What they wanted to-day was leaders who would lead, not men who lived upon the gifts and sacrifices of their fathers. There was special music throughout the day and the scholars joined in singing hymns. At the morning service the choir sang very effectively the anthem (stainer). In the afternoon " Sing a song of praise " a flower service was held, when Mr. James Gauld gave " an address. NH.. T. Burke sang "Lend me your aid (Gounod) and Mrs. Booth was heard to great advantage in "Come ye children " (Sullivan). Miss Cottam gave a recitation. At the evening service the choir gave a fine interpret,ttion of " The heavens are telling the glory of God" (" The creation "). Mr. Burke's solo " Thou shalt break them (Handel) was also much appreciated. Mr. Frank Hickman plesided at the organ</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>DERBY, 1912.</head>
        <p>THE DERBY, 1912.</p>
        <p>This classic event resulted as follows :—Tagalie 1, Jaeger 2, Tracey 3. Tinie as taken by Benson's Chronograph, 2 mins. 334-5 sees. 'lime last year 2 mina. 36 4-5 stcs.</p>
        <p>Nine hnndred and eighty-seven thousand, two hundred end twenty-seven pounds was taken by the Glasgow tramways during the financial year which ended on .‘.'aturday. This was easily a record, and £41,200 in excess of the previous year. Over 270 million pas- r engers were carried, this being 38 millions in exce.s of the number carried last year—chiefly as the result of a reduction in fares.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>Mi</p>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASE.—At the Lymm Police Court on Tuesday morning, before Messrs W G Groves and S Davies on the bench, Catherine Peers, of Millington was charged with having used had language at Agden, on Monday evening. After P.C. Needham bad proved the case the defendant was fined 5s and Costs. MAY QUEEN RESULTS.—It affords us much pleasure to announce that despite the loss of the afternoon gate owing to the inclemency of the weather, the May Queen Committee have every cause to hope that the large audience and generous support given at the evening performance of the festival on last Thursday but one will enat le them to meet all the expanses entailed by this year's celebrations. A DANGEROUS HORSE —On Saturday night last a Mr Baker of Warrington, who was staying for the week- end with some friends at Oughtrington, was walking along the bridle path when he observed a horse grazing at the foot of the bank at one side of the path. As the animal was between Mr Baker and his destination he endeavoured to quietly pass it on the opposite side of thelpath. Unfortunately the horse must have taken a sudden fright for lashing out with its hind feet it struck Mr Baker in the side forcing him through the hedge, causing painful and it was feared internal injury. For a time Mr Baker lay stunned but eventually he managed to reach his friendS,Mr George Owen's, house. Dr Thorpe arrived on Sunday morning and found on examination that Mr Baker bad sustained a fractured rib. It appears that the horse, which had found its way out of the field into which it had been put, belonged to one of the fairs which were in Lymm during Whit-week, and had on the very same night kicked at and hurt the shoulder of another pedestrian. Mr. Thomas Wilson, who was also passing along the Bridle Path.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the Urban District Council was held last Friday evening in the Council Offices. Mr W G Groves presided, and there were also present :—Messrs rhomes H Warburton (vice-chairman), Samuel Davies, lt H Gotobed, and William Gould, with Mr W Mullard (clerk and collecter), Dr de Jong (medical officer), Mr W L Donaldson (gas manager), and Mr David Mort (surveyor, &amp;c.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>SEWERAGE SCHEME APPROVED.</head>
        <p>THE SEWERAGE SCHEME APPROVED.</p>
        <p>On the minutes of the General Purposes Committee it was stated that the Order of the Local Government Board bad been received giving sanction to the Council to the borrowing of the following monies for the Lymni sewerage scheme, viz, £l,OOO for the purchase of the land for 60 years, £7,995 for works of easements for 30 years, £337 for roads works for 20 years, and £360 for machinery for 15 years. or a total of £9,692. It was decided that Mr W W Beckett, of Manchester, be engaged as engineer, and that advertisements be issued for a clerk of the works at a salary of three guineas per week ; and that the Clerk obtain a loan of £9,692 from the Public Works Loan Commissioners in accord- ance with the sanction of the Local Government Board. The minutes were approved. TEAM-OWNERS AND RATE OF PAY. At a meeting of the Roads Committee a petition was presented by the Team-Owners' Association asking for an increase of pay, and after full consideration of the subject it was resolved—" That it be a recommendation to the Council to pay Is. per day more for horse, cart and man at and from the first Friday in June, no increase to be granted for contract works. The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>DUCHESS OF CONNAUGHT.</head>
        <p>THE DUCHESS OF CONNAUGHT.</p>
        <p>A recent portrait of the Duchess of Con- naught, who is lying seriously ill in Quebec with %appendicitis. A special train was despatched from Montreal with Dr. Garrow, the eminent surgeon, and nurses.</p>
        <p>• • . " • . ie. • • '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Great Sankey</head>
        <p>Great Sankey</p>
        <p>THE DUST NUISANCE.—At a largely attended meet- ing of the ratepayers of Great Sankey held in the schools on Monday evening. strong protests were made against the lethargy of the County Council in dealihg with the intolerable dust nuisance. Mr. George Clarke, headmaster of the school, said there had been an increase of catarrh among the scholars, owing to germs in the dust. It was eventually unanimously resolved to send the following protest to the Lancashire County Council :—" We, the parishioners of this parish, are not at all satisfied with the slow manner in which the matter is being dealt with, and that their reply to the wishes of the parishioners in April last for tar- spraying the road is not at all satisfactory ; other districts with considerably less inhabitants and less traffic on the roads having been attended to a month ago."</p>
        <p>Grappenhall. PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night the usual monthly meeting of the Parish Council was held in the Council schools. Mr Lister-Kaye presided and there were a:so present:— Messrs W Lomas, J Bradford, F Ellison, Dr Hutt, and Mr Charles H Holt (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>DANGEROUS CORNER.</head>
        <p>DANGEROUS CORNER.</p>
        <p>The Chairman atated that as one of the deputation appointed to meet the County Surveyor he was able to report on the dangerous corner at the Dog and Dart Inn. It was decided that if the necessary land was given the corner would be widened. At the request of Lord Barrymore's agent, Mr Richmond, a plan of the suggested alteration had been forwarded. They were now waiting the decision of the landowner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>DR. HUTT RESIGNS.</head>
        <p>DR. HUTT RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>The doctor handed his' resignation in to the Council. He stated that owing to a recent appointment at Brighton he had been compelled to take this course. The Chairman said that he was sure he as voicing the opinion of all members of the Council in saying that in Dr. Hutt they were losing a vauable member. He (Mr. Kaye) was looking forward to very valuable assistance from Dr. Hutt in connection with the sewerage scheme before them. (Hear, hear.) A YORKSHIRE COMEDIAN'S MOTOR SMASH.— Whilst on his way to an engagement in Blackpool on Monday last, the popular Yorkshire comic (Jack Lane) met with an accident to his motor car on Knutstord- road, Granpenhall. It so happened that when opposite to Legh Villa's at the corner of Victoria avenue, the motorists were passing a flour cart belonging to Mesirs Fairclough, Warrington, when the car taidded and caught a lamp post on the corner of the avenue. This caused it to swerve rouad and it dashed into the nail at the foot of Victoria- avenue. Fortunately none of the occupants were hurt. The comedian's wife was travelling in the car. and except for a slight shock she was luckily unharmed, Mr. Lane and his wife eventually transferred their luggage to a hired taxi cab, and were able to proceed. Later on in the day the damaged car was removed to a Manchester garage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
