      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>MAL</head>
        <p>MAL</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>MAGISTRATES AMUSED. BAREBACK tIDER'S FEAT AT WIDNES:.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Police Court, on Monday, William Kirkham, a youth, was summoned for furiously riding a horse along Halton-road on May 29th. Police Constable Farran said the youth was riding bareback and getting eversthing that was possible in the way of speed out of the animal. The defendant said the horse was a heavy one. It took him a quarter of an hour to get from the field to the stable.* mile away, on it. (Laughter). .</p>
        <p>Frank Unsworth, a „ greennroseer's- boy, said that if the horse had been going as fast as the policeman made out it could not have been pulled up in less than forty yards. The policeman asked the defendant if he was in for the Derby. (Laughter). The horse weighed 12cw t. It was galloping, but" not at any speed." (Laughter.) He estimated its gallop at three miles an hour. (Loud laughter). It was said to he worth i5O, but he could nearly keep no to it on his pony that was worth £3. (Laughter.) He did keep up till he saw the policeman. (Laughter). An amused Bench dismissed the case. RUNCORN'S SUNDAY TRADE. PROTEST AGAINST" PERSECU LIONS."</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Urban Council on Tuesday night, a letter was read from Messrs .1 Henderson and A Horrocks, local shopkeepers, asking the council to support their protest against forcing shops to close on Sundays. The letter stated that a great many small shopkeepers in Runcorn earned the greater part of their living on Sunday, and many were widows and old people unable to follow any other occupation. It was difficult to understand why they had been persecuted for two years under an Act which exempted those engaged in "a work of necessity or charity." Mr Posnett said the council would not add to its dignity if it tried to interfere in any way with what did not concern it.</p>
        <p>Mr R Gibbs said that these people were ratepayers for 365 eays out of the year, and if the Council could assist to protect them from those persecutions it was its duty to do so.</p>
        <p>Mr Dean said that last Sunday a visitor to Runcorn was supplied with a bottle of ginger-beer at a shop, and was immediately pounced upon by the police. The man could have gone to a public-house and had two pints of beer and nothing would have been said about it. By a practicslly unanimous vote the Council referred the question to its Parliamentary Committee. _ RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians wan held at Dutton on Tuesday, Mr J Spruce presiding.</p>
        <p>THE INSURANCE ACT.</p>
        <p>A circnlar was read from tbe National Health Insur- ance Commission inviting the attention of the Board to the provision of the National Insuarnce Act, 1911, and in particular to the terms of paragraph (b) of two schedule 1, under which exception may in certain cases be given from the provisions of the Act relating to compulsory health insurance. If the Board desired that that the Commissioners should issue a certificate of exception in respect of any daises of its employees, application should at once he made. The Clerk suggested that as the matter would entail a large amount of consideration it should be referred to one of the Committees of the Board.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. Woodward the matter was left with the Parliamentary Committee.</p>
        <p>COUNTY COUNCIL PRECEPT,_</p>
        <p>The Clerk produced the County Council precept for the half-year a total of £12,983. In connection with this the general County purposes rate had been increased from 3d to 3;cl but to meet that the general elementary purposes rate bad been decreased from sid. to 51d so that the County rate would practically be the same.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>RELIEF.</head>
        <p>RELIEF.</p>
        <p>The Master reported the number of inmates at 179, against 185 last year, and the number of vagrants relieved, 128 against 111 last se3r,</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday. Mr. J. Linaker presiding.</p>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHTING.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote enclosing a copy of a letter forwarded by them to the Mersey Power io. consenting to the erection of an overhead line from a point near Pewitt Hall Farm to the premises of the British Insulated and Helsby Cable Co. Ltd. at Helshy for the transmission of energy at a pressure of 6,300 volts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>BONE NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>THE BONE NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>_ A letter was read from the London and North-Western and Great-Western Joint Railway Companies' Superin- tendent at Shrewsbury stating with reference to the bone nuisance at Frodsham 6tation : " I think you are fully aware of all the efforts we make to avoid cause for complaint. We go out of our way to do this and incur additional expense in connection with the traffic. I think it would be well if the Council took up the matter with Messrs. Heywood and Massie. Of course, if you carry out your threat we shall have to refuse to carry the traffic and this will mean the closing of Messrs. Heywood and Massie's works. A letter was also read from Messrs. Heywood and Massie stating " We think we may safely say that our directors will agree to provide covered carts for any future passage of bones through the streets of Frodsham, and we shall certainly have no objection whatever to the unloading of the bones at the far aide of the station as at present we think if these two conditions are carried out there should be no cause for further complaints. With reference to the sewerage they were asking for definite news on that point that day. Mr. Linaker said it appeared to him that the whole thing would be remedied if they could have the bones tAkeu to Halton Station which was nearer to Messrs. Heywood and Massie's works. Mr. Harrison : It would be a great pity to close the works.</p>
        <p>Mr.,Linaker. : The nuisance is a real one and we roust make every effect to take such steps as may be necessary to remove the nuisance. If they took the bones across they would only he removing the nuisance from one side to the other. The Clerk : Providing it carries so far. Mr. Linaker (jun.) said he thought it would be better if the firm would have the bones transferred to Halton Station. He moved that the Railway Com- pany be asked to make arrsngements if possible to con- vey the bones to Halton Station. If covered carts were used, it would stop the nuisance in the streets, but they would have it just the same on the station. If the bones were removed to the jam works side they would have complaints from the jam people. (Hear, hear). The resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>ROADS A DISGRACE. Dr. Selby wrote as follows: "A gentleman who rode from London to Frodsham the other day said he never came across such a bad condition of roads as from Frodsham Bridge, through Frodsharn to Church- street and through Netherton.This condition has existed for years and I will defy anyone to find a worse bit of road in any county or on any :oral district road for the matter of that. If the road is properly made there need not be so much humbug talked about heavy motor traffic, &amp;c. The County Council seem to treat us LIKE SCHOOL CHILDREN</p>
        <p>in Frodsham and the various councils take the matter lying down. A shame ; a disgrace to any authority. Mr. Linaker, jun., spoke of the very bad condition of the road, and it was afterwards decided in view of the fact that the County Council bad promised to re-make the whole road, that they be asked to proceed with the work as soon as possible. SURVEYJR AND SANITARY INSPECTOR. The Clerk 6 übruitted a lengthy report upon the duties, salaries and remuneration of existing Sanitary and Highway Office's and his comments thereon. The Surveyors' Duties Committee reported having met and submitted certain resolutions that the High- ways Department of the Council be made distinct from from the Public Health Departments ; that the duties of the Sanitary Inspector shall comprise all the duties of on Inspector of Nuisances as prescribed by the Public Health Act ; that a permanent official at a fixed salary be appointed as Surveyor under the Public Health Acts ; that the present arrangements of the Public Health and Highways Departments with the addition of all new works under the Public Act being transferred to Messrs. Wright and Ilamlyn be con- tinued for a further period of six months from the end of June and they be paid an inclusive fee of 5 per cent on the actual cost of such new works ; that it boa sug- gestion to Messrs. Wright and Harulyn that they sign a memorandum that they will abide by the decision of the Chairman of the Council in fixing the amount of remuneration to be paid to them for their services to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>A man with sound kidneys almost forgets that he has a back— But the man with kidney trouble is always in misery. _</p>
        <p>He wakes up with backache morning after morning— Any stooping or heavy lifting sends a sharp pain darting through his loins— __</p>
        <p>The -same at -night—his back gives him no peace. An aching back is good cause to suspect the kidneys. They lie beneath the small of the back, and what is generally thought to be backache is more often kidney-ache. That is why other symptoms of kidney trouble quickly follow a bad back—dropsical swellings</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>AGED THIEF CAUGHT.</p>
        <p>Elizabeth Green. an elderly Birmingl;a-m woman, was dealt with at St. Helens on Saturday on two charges of ringing the changes. She went into the market last Saturday week and robbed a couple of stallholdera of 4s Gd and 2s. One of the stallholders followed her and gave her in charge when she was handing the money to a man, supposed to be her husband. When the police attempted to take the woman's finger-prints she fought and swore in the most violent manner. By means of the finger-prints it was found that she had been convicted at Liverpool, Warrington, Manchester, London, and all over the country for stealing, wounding, &amp;c. She had served a term of penal servitude. The bench sent her to prison for three mooths on each charge, six months altogether. Charles Reeves, who represented himself to be the woman's husband, was sent to prison for six weeks on a charge of stealing a pair of boots on the same day. TERRITORIALS' CHURCH PARADE. TRUE FOUNDATIONS OF EINIPIRE.</p>
        <p>A church parade of the West Lancashire Divisional Engineers and the 3rd West Lancashire Field Ambulance was held on Sunday morning at Victoria Park, St. Helens. Colonel Fraser commanded the Engineers, along with Major Opp.mheim, Captain Fox, Captain and Adjutant Swinton, RE., Lieut. Broom, Lieut. Dixon- Nuttall and Sergeant-Major Collings, but there was a very small muster of men. The R.A.M.C. were present in strong force under Colonel ;Jackson, Captain Merrick, Lieut. Buchan, Lieut. and Quartermasler Jukes, and Serge%nt-Major Wilson. The Engineers' band played for the hymns. The Rev. L. J. Rich (chaplain) preached, and said there was a danger that they would forget the true foundations of Empire. There was no better expres- sion of the true foundations of Empire than in the work of a French historian, who in reference to England, said that the three great foundations of society were the spirit of liberty, the domestic character and religious faith. The spirit of freedom, the love of liberty, showed itself in the determination ever to put away all that hampered and hindered the humane spirit and human love, whether in politics or their social institutions, in the determination to strive after that which was true, and good, and pure. they realised both in the home and in public life that faith in God and immortality were vital issues, and upon them the welfare and progress of the nation depended. What was true of the Empire was true of the individuals who made the Empire, and they should all strive to keep those vital issues before them, upholding freedom, the sanctity of home, and religious faith in all their deeds and works. A collection was taken for the St. Helens Hospital. Later, at headquarters, Colonel Fraser presented long- service medals to Sergeant Prescott, Sergeant Howard, Sergeant Leyshon, Corporal Caldwell, Sappers Appleton, Hilditcb, Groves, and H Lloyd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>AN INSURANCE ACT CHAMPION.</head>
        <p>AN INSURANCE ACT CHAMPION.</p>
        <p>Sir Victor Horsley, replying in " The Times "to Professor Marsh, says :—ln what way the Insurance Act interferes with the progress of preventive medicine Professor Marsh does not explain, but, oddly enough, he does refer to one disease which he says, most truly, ought to be, and can be, stamped out. That disease is tuberculosis. But, suraly, the Insurance Act is the first attempt to carry out cn a national scale this urgently-needed work of preventive medicine. Everyone interested in social reform will agree that the doctors could do more in furthering preventive measures, only public bodies think they know best, and will not bestir themselves. But the Insurance provides that approved societies, insur- ance committees, and the Commissioners can any of them compel such public bodies, bid ernaloyers,or slum owners to give redress of insanitary conditions. Surely Professar Marsh has misunderstood Mr. Lloyd George and his statute, for the Act shows that the Minister regards himself as "a servant of the public," for whose interests he has endeavoured to do more in the direc- tion ofnreventive medicine than any other Caancellor of the Exchequer the country has yet seen.</p>
