      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>AN ALLEGED DANGEROUS ROAD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council was held in IBewsey chambers on Wednesday afternoon. Mr G Mather presided and there were also present :—Rev A M Mitchell, Dr Murray, Mesvrs A Brooks, P Longton, J Backhouae, J C Sutton (clerk), B S Newcombe (m.rveyor), H H Burnage (assistant surveyor). F lierfoot (inspector), and Dr Sephton (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>ALDERMAN BENNETT AND DANGEROUS ROADS.</head>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETT AND DANGEROUS ROADS.</p>
        <p>.—„— The CLERK read a letter from Alderman Bennett in which he said he appended a copy of a letter he had sent to the County Council some days ago which was as follows :—" The recent fatal mctor accident in Warrington has prominently called attention to the various dangerous roads in the town and neighbourhood. Orie of these is the curve in the Manchester-road just by Paddington House which is very abrupt and often negotiated by light and heavy motor vehicles at a great speed and without any warning. The danger is aggra- vated by the fact that carriage entrances to several of the contiguous houses abut upon the curve and I should like to respectfully suggest to the Main Roads and Bridges Committee that danger signs should be fixed at each end of the curve and that all vehicles should be required to slow down at this point." Mr BacKirousk : Does the worthy Alderman reside- in that direction?</p>
        <p>The SURVEYOR Yes, but at the spot indicated there are no cross roads and it is no more dangerous than many other main roads of the district. If they put danger signs at every house when requested, he con- tinued,they would have to put them up at every cottage. If there were so many the motorists would not take notice of them.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : They do not take any notice of them as it is. It was decided that the Ccuncil take no action in the matter.</p>
        <p>M FAH CAL OFFICER'S_ REPORT.</p>
        <p>The MEDICAL OFFICER reported -t-t;;;C there were 26 births during the month, 38 deaths. The death-rate was equal to 30.9 per thousand as as against 30.4 for the previous three months and 39.3 for the corresponding period last year. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>HIS PAINS OF TWENTY YEARS' STANDING REMOVED IN A SINGLE NIGHT.</head>
