      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Home Rule.</head>
        <p>Home Rule.</p>
        <p>The Horde of Officials in Ireland.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Cambray of the Union Defence League has written a letter, which he appears to have addrersed to a vast number of newspapers in the country, in which he seeks to controvert one statement in the booklet I published under the title of "Home Rule in a Nutshell." May I therefore ask the courtesy of space to reply? His point is that my figures are contradicted by a recent treasury return ; my reply is that my figures were also taken from a Government return, namely, the annual report of the commissioners of income tax ; and if the two returns are not in accord that is not my fault. But there is no serious discrepancy between the two returns, and the one proves my case almost as con- clusively as the other. The treasury return on which Mr. Cambray relies distinguishes between Irish Departments and departments which have only branches in Ireland, as also between Scottish depart- ments and departments which have only branches in Scotland; and, in point of fact, the document which has aroused his enthusiasm shows that in the Scottish departments. there are 1,558 officials, of whom 418 are in receipt of a salary exceeding 160, whilst the Irish departments have no less than 4,042 officials of whom 1,240 are above the Income Tax limit : in other words, that the Irish departments have about three times as many officials as the Scottish departments, although Scotland is about the same size and has about the same population as Ireland. Moreover those figures do not include some 1,600 beneficiaries under the Trish Church Act, who, if not technically officials, are undoubtedly State stipendiaries, and are included in the estimate formed by the Income Tax Commissioners, and as the Treasury Return is of later date than the Income Tax Return, a further discrepancy is also inevitable, The net result, therefore, is that, according to the only return which was available at the time of the publica- tion of "Home Rule in a Nutshell," Ireland has five times as many Government officials as Scotland; and that according to the Treasury Return issued only a few weeks ago, and prepared on a wholly different basis, the Irish departments have only three times as many officials as the Scottish departments. Either document proves my case to demonstration and what therefore becomes of Mr. Cambray's mock heroics about the amazing inaccuracies of ministers of the Crown, members of His Majesty's Government and responsible leaders of the Irish Party " ? The "amazing inaccuracies" coma from one Government report, and Mr. Cambray's infallible figures from another.—Yours faithfully, JERMIAH MACVEAGH. House of Commons, June 3rd, 1012.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Smoke Questioo.</head>
        <p>The Smoke Questioo.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l was much interested Tn your " Whirlwind " article last week and feel myself drawn into the vortex. Though I cannot go as far as your corres- pondent in his condemnation of the general-inactivity of Health Committees, yet one cannot but think that there is a substratum of truth in it, but this inactivity I ascribe, not to deliberate refusal to deal with what I prefer to call " smoke danger " not " smoke nuisance," but to the fact that the community has not yet wakened up to the danger and with a view to bringing about this realisation, perhaps a few facts from actual medical experience will do something to help the cause. After many years general practice, half in the borough and half in the country, one thing in particu- lar has impressed itself many times on me and it is the difference, in severity and frequency of complica- tions, between cases of measles outside the borough and those inside. Whereas in the country measles never even give rise to anxiety and complications are almost nil, a very different tale must be told of the town and the nearer one gets to the centre so does the anxiety f become greater and the death rate higher. I have never myself known a death from measles in the country district, and in the last report of the Warrington Rural District Council to hand there was no record of any death from measles at all. What can be the reason? It is not that the dwellings are more sanitary, for often the country cottage with flag floor, low rooms, scarcity of window area and absence of sanitary conveniences is placed at a great disadvantage, as compared with the modern working- man's home in town. Something else accounts and in my opinion it is smoke vitiating the atmosphere, loading it with solid particles which as surely act as irritants to the delicate lung membrane a/ particles of ground glass do to the intestinal tract, which has been many times proved in criminal cases of death. Who has not bad the experience of having to let the bedroom fire go out in the depth of winter with a patient probably suffering from bronchitis or pneumonia, because the wind was in the wrong direc- tion and the chimney "smoked," intensifying the cough and giving the already weakened lungs more work to do, to expel the particles,; and the patient in particular prefers the raw cold air to exhaustion from coughing. Further any medical man must have noticed the vast difference in the expectoration of adult bronchitic patients in the town and in the country. The former is almost black, the latter rarely more than tinged with yellow or pale green. A well-known Douglas practitioner tells me that every year he is struck with the rapid change for the better in the character of the sputum of chest-affected patients who go there for their health from Lancashire industrial towns. Again the appearance of lung tissues as disclosed at post-mortems on industrial town dwellers is almostblack, far different from the normal reddish-pink of the country dweller. Should we be far wrong in ascribing these differ- 4mces to smoke?</p>
        <p>Dust does not account for everything, for country dwellings built usually right on to a dusty highway are at a great disadvantage compared with the town house with well-paved streets and frequent water- carts, therefore another factor is at work and the most probable and obvious one is smoke. The density and frequency of fog has been enormously reduced in London since the old underground railway, with its wreathing volumes of smoke, has been electrified. Sunshine is golden,darkness is dross and he who veils the sun impoverishes the nation. May your efforts in ventilating this subject result in a united action on the part of all to find a com- mercial substitute for raw coal with its death-dealing propensities, one which will not diminish the profits of the manufacturer for whom I have every sympathy, but will greatly improve the health and happiness of the people. Commercial prosperity obtained at the expense of the health of the community is ill-gotten gain and false economy. Let us then bestir ourselves, for while smoke pours from all our thousand chimney- tops there hangs a guillotine o'er every head and we are the jury committing souls to ita death-dealing blow.—Tours faithfully, Lynton Grange, Padgate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
