      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>JAMES BENNETT.</head>
        <p>JAMES BENNETT.</p>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team has opened the season in promising style, the first League match with Woolton, at the Warrington Corporation Baths on Thursday evening, resulting in a creditable victory for the home team by four goals to one. Curiously enough,the game was most keenly fought when Warrington were defend- ing the shallow end in the first half,hut the home players were superior and at the interval led by a goal (scored by Pepper) to nil. Soon after resuming Pepper regis- tered a second goal, Martin following with one for Woolston, but from this stage Warrington did most of the attacking and Ince landed two additional goals. A squadrop race between Warrington and Woolton resulted in a win for the visitors by about two yards. Considerable enthusiasm was aroused by a 25 yards handicap for school boys. The competitors put in all they knew and a capital race resulted as follows : 1, J Whalley, Parochial school ; 2, Thomas Flanaghan, Sacred Heart school; 3, II Siddall, Beamont school.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Hardy, the well-known novelist, celebra- ted his 72nd birthday at Dorchester, on Sunday, and received many ocngratulations from all parts of the country. During the day he was privately presented with the gold medal of the Royal Society of Litera- ture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>LIE ABSOLUTE.</head>
        <p>THE LIE ABSOLUTE.</p>
        <p>LADY GREENALL AND "THE RADICAL PAPER."</p>
        <p>All About a Bracelet.</p>
        <p>There is something very direct and forcible about Lady Greenall when she is giving an opponent the retort courteous, which we cannot help but admire. She leaves no one I in any doubt as to her meaning. For instance, on Tuesday of last week she was presiding in the select circle of the Women's Unionist and Tariff Reform League, and according to the faithful " Guardian "reporter, her ladyship responded to a vote of thanks in the follow- ing feeling terms : The other day the local Radical paper said she gave away a foreign-made bracelet at the Tariff Reform whist drive. It was a lie. (Hear, hear.) She gave away no foreign-made bracelet. It was an absolute lie. (Hear,hear,and applause). You will note that the new style of acknowledging a vote of thanks found considerable favour in that dis- criminating audience. Indeed, the words "It was an absolute lie" were greeted with "hear, hear and applause." Obviously, Lady Greenall had the meeting with her; they met on common ground against a common enemy. Unless we are mistaken the common enemy is "the local Radical paper," which might be construed to mean ourselves. But we never said that Lady Greenall gave away a foreign-made bracelet at the Tariff Reform I Whist Drive. We never mentioned the lady in such a connection, therefore we could not I have been guilty of either a lie, an I absolute lie, or that more modern political accessory,—a terminological inexactitude. But if we had made such a charge against her ladyship personally, we should not have expected her to retort with such fiery indignation. She has done these things before, and has not only admitted the offence but condoned it. When we discovered that her ladyship had given German clocks as prizes at the Dumping Exhibition she at once admitted the fact, and seemed proud rather than otherwise of her action. Moreover, she was not noticeably angry with the "local Radical paper," though 'tis true, she did say we were anmic. We might readily have retorted that this was "an absolute lie," and put in a medical certificate, but when one's conscience is clear of reproach such things can be pleasantly ignored. What we should like to ask is this: Having given German clocks to anti-dumpers and gloried in it, why should Lady Greenall show such annoyance if any- one charged her with giving a foreign-made bracelet to a Tariff Reformer? The first was the greater breach of political con- sistency and the second (even if true) would only go to show that Lady Greenall was con- sistent in one thing—her inconsistency.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. J. C. YOUNG</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. J. C. YOUNG</p>
        <p>Warrington has lost another link with the past and one of its most highly respected inhabitants by the death of Mr. John Clarkson Young, who passed away at his residence, I, Palmyra-square, Warrington, on Sunday. For the last two years Mr. Young had been in a feeble state of health and, although the end came somewhat suddenly, it was not unexpected. Born at Hull on May 14th, 182 S, Mr. Young came to Warring- ton at an early age. He was originally intended for the medical profession, but was attracted by the study of chemistry and very many years ago, established himself in business as a chemist in Buttermarket- street. In later years he took the degree of L.D.S, (Ireland) and practised as a dental surgeon until failing eyesight hastened his retirement about 11 years ago. Mr. Young was of an inventive turn of mind and was the patentee of several extremely useful appliances which are used to this , day in dentistry. Of a reserved and unostentatious disposition, Mr. Young could never be induced to take an active part in public work, but that did not I prevent his following the afTbirs of the town with deep' interest. A warm friendship existed between him and the late Dr. Charles White, whose recent death keenly affected Mr. Young. He was' one of the oldest pro- fessional gentleman in the town and all ho knew him will regret the loss of such a useful and revered citizen. Mrs. Young pre-deceased him about seven years ago. There were two sons, one of whom is Mr. J. Rymer Young, J.P. The other was the late Pro- fessor Young, professor of anatomy at Victoria University, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Great simplicity characterised the funeral which took place at the Cemetery at noon on Wednesday, the Rev. Dr. Mellor officiating. The mourners were: Mr and Mrs J Rymer Young, Mrs Rawlinson, Mr J Longland, Mr J S Hill, Mr Peake and Mr J F Dutton. There were also present at the graveside : Messrs F W Monks, Linmeus Greening, J Besketh, Wells and A Perris. Floral tributes were sent by the following:—" The Family," Land Company Directors, Mrs. Tunetall, Elsie, Sydney, and Norah. Mr and Mrs Peake and family, Mr and Mrs Hill, Mr and Mrs Wells, Mrs Deacon, Mrs Dutton, Mr and Mrs S P Silcock, housekeeper and maid, Mrs John Tunstall, Mr and Mrs J F Dutton, Mr and Mrs W S Dutton, Miss Kay, Mrs Mason, Mr and Mrs Dean, Mrs Mason, Hull, Mr and Mrs Whittle. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Messrs J and W Dutton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WOMEN'S CIRCLE SALE OF WORK.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CIRCLE SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>The annual sale of work in aid of the funds of the Women's Circle was held on Wednesday, in the Club Room, Bridge-street. The opening ceremony was performed by Miss Massy and amongst others present were Mrs Dodds (deputy president),Nliss BessiedDutton (treasurer), Mesdames ti) lands, Ziba Armitage, Fair- dough, F Monks, ThomaslOwen, E V Walker, W J Walker, and Miss Dadds. The stalls, which were laden with a variety of u-efut and ornamental t articles' were tastefully displalei and were in charge of the tololwing ladies : Plain Work (Mothers' Union) : Mrs Rylands and Mrs Clatworthy. Fancy Work : Mrs Armitage, Miss Morris, Mrs Law and Miss Ball.</p>
        <p>Refreshments : Mrs Dodds and daughters. Teas : Mrs Critchley and Miss Bennett. Fruit and Flowers : Mrs Kenworthy, Mrs Willett, Mrs Rathbone and Miss I3olton. Pound Stall : Mrs Wainwright and Mrs Spittle. Sweets : Mrs Elliot and Juniors. Ice-cream : Miss L Plinston and Miss Cooper. Bran-tub : Miss M Critchley and Miss E Barnett. Mrs Dodds, in calling upon Miss Massey to open the sale expressed the pleasure which the members of the Circle felt in welcoming her amongst them. Miss MASSY', who was presented with a bouquet by Miss Winifred Mary Brereton, said shw believed the Women's Circle was doing good work in a quiet manner. It was an excellent plan to gather the women together where they could spend a happy evening. Such a sale was a necessity because the subscriptions did not cover the whole of the expenses. She hoped everyone would do their best to make the sale a distinct success. (Applause). A hearty vote of thanks was accorded Miss Massy, on the propositien of Mrs. RYLAND'S, who pointed out that Miss Massy was always willing to help in any good work. Personally, she would like tn congratulate Miss Ball (the leader) on the very excellent report she had issued. Such good results were very encouraging. If only the charitable nature of the work were more gener- ally known, it would be more generously supported. It was a great pity when they knew the good work it was accomplishing. The Circle was entirely non- sectarian and non-political ; many shades of opinion were connected with the organisation, and they all work- ed with the oneobject,lt was an extremely good feature when they could meet together on common ground, and they hoped such a happy state would continue. In seconding the vote of thanks Mrs. ZIBA. Alimmtoe said that if they could only interest more people in the town in the work of the Circle, they would have more money to keep it going. She was sure if outsiders knew more about it, the work would go forward successfully. The resolution was carried unanimously. A short musical programme was arranged by Mrs Jackson Holmes and Mrs Kenworthy assisted by .Mrs Booth arid Mrs Ilaviour (violinist). The sale realised about £4O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, June 8, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 8, 1912.</p>
        <p>THE CYCLIST'S NOTE-BOOK.</p>
        <p>[By " Silver Street."]</p>
        <p>A tour through Kerry and Cork (2). One feature in the government of Ireland is very little touched upon, the excessive number of drink shops that has been allowed to develop under their present English- made laws. Kermare contains 65 shops, and 35 of them sell drink, generally on one counter, and other goods at another. These half and half shops, it will be remembered, had to be specially dealt with by Mr. Lloyd George in his great buoget. At Drimolesgue 11 out of 25, and at Deem, not many years ago, every single shop had the coveted licence. In Cork itself. one side of Princes-street south contains 14 shops. The first eilzht one after the other are public houses then a pot-shop, then two more publics, then two shops and one more tsvern. Here are 11 public-houses out of a street of 14 separate shops. Could the Irish do any worse themselves? Something went wrong with my coaster hub, and so I took it to a cycle dealer to</p>
        <p>and Monte Carlo ; the cypress, the strawberry tree or arbutus can all be cited as evidence of what the south-west cornbr of the Emerald Isle will grow. The gardener's cottage is covered in front by two fuchsia trees, one rooted on each side of the door, and the garden walk is an avenue of palms. It was on our way back that our first trouble recurred. My companion's two speed gear (Royal Sunbeam) gave a snap, and for six miles she was compelled to pedal on her :10 gear with 7 inch cranks, a very evident waste of power on a flat road with an excellent surface. It says something for the simplicity of the Sunbeam gear when I was able to pull it to pieces and find a broken spring. I sent it by post to Wolverhampton and received in two days a new spring with the bill, and at rnee put things to right again. Aid lam emphatically not a mechanic. We watched a salmon net hauled in close to the Suspension Bridge. Nine large fish were taken up, and t- gether they must have weighed 70 to SO lbs. The poaching that g, es on is somewhat painful as well as extt naive. The bhoys fill a suck with Irish spurge. To those v.ho are not botanists I may explain that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>LAN SDO WN E SUSPENSION BRIDGE, KENMAR]</head>
        <p>LAN SDO WN E SUSPENSION BRIDGE, KENMAR]</p>
        <p>repair. He had to drop the work on the machine four or five times to go and serve drink on the other counter. No wonder that Kenmare is a shabby town. Hovels, 2, a week for rent, and disgraceful in their neglect form all hut the three main streets; fowls, cows, calves, pigs, filth, offal, obstruct the streets, and if living walk in and out of the houses. Here is one, rent 2s. a week, not a whole pane of glass in the house and much of the window frame itself lost, not a drop of drinking water is laid on in any house, and the 1,200 are dependent on a few springs. The O'Sullivans sent their little errand lad (they kept a paper, fancy, sweet and tobacco shop) half a mile there and back for every drop of thinking water used in the house. The domestic water is from the rain-tub and when that fails they pay 4rl. or sd. a gallon for wash- ing water; and all this in a countly teeming with lakes and torrents that ought to be brought into service but for the awful poverty. But there is hope yet, street after street is being replaced under the con- gested Districts Board by pretty cottages Is. 9d. a week rent. This pays both interest and oinking fund on the money they cost, and anyone who sees this poor little town will surely say it is a congested district. We crossed the Lansdowne Suspension Bridge over the Kenmare river, one of the magnificent fiords , that make the geography of that part of Ireland so difficult to remember to an English scholar. Except I that the water is salt, and feels the tide twice a day it is a river, not a gulf. This bridge is 36 miles from the open sea, and nowhere is the stretch of water more than 4 miles wide from bank to bank. It was cn this day that we had our first. sight of the great Atlantic. It was just as we rounded the corner 1 of Kilmakilloge Harbour on our way to Dereen I Woods. ['he house is Lord Lansdowne's, and I Killarney itself has no beauties to equal,- the paradise that surrounds that land-locked bay. " The groves of bamboo canes 20 feet high, the clumps of New Yealand flax, the escalonia, the fuchsias., the palms, the same species as on the front at Cannes</p>
        <p>this plant belongs to the same class as the little herb whose white juice rur boys are fond of applying to watts in the belief that it will drive them away. In Ireland the spurge grows to a height of 4 or 5 feet and the sack full is stamped on and thrown into a pi el. In an hour or les-i every fish in that pool is floating on the top and the bhoys collect those that are worth taking away. The farmer at Morley's Bridge who told us about it, showed us a pool about 20 yards across frrin which four salmon and a good many trout had been thus taken a week ago.</p>
        <p>• • ' wr-1, . ilk, ~...,,,,;,:...1,..,, ',-'•,::!„t-,,,—.... , 0,0-'-,-..*.4,41,.,1,-,,,..-.. ~, '' '- • '''" "-: , .. - --: - . t.-.-i:: '4-*;,„'..: „f,"',.',14', '' ' -:;',' - --,-;:-' --. ~ ,' ' • 0-. .1 ~,, —:\ .•': ,-, ":',4:.•:-.../ r, !"..4,4,:,;;-",#,.',. 171.4...:; '-' , * - 1-,.. .-.: ''.. ." . "•• '' ' ... t-,•11t,fr:..,t,..xP: ..,,,-4Z:;',' ',' ~ ---,. , ~ „ ~..,- -'',.,:.,,::)::'i, •', ' '-.:;. ' , '7,10-,..... - t,t4i..140-,-e' • '."-.4,- —1 ~ ~,,i.5i,,,,,, • .0 :.:::„ ~ -:, „,.., ~.::.: , ..,--7,—.: 4wAsow , ..-.l.:ft.stfii7,l";-;','::;;');-.7;4;':Iii..,,, '. 51,;&lt;:•,..,..--,--'""10off' . -7;.,...; • : .‘42-'''-' ~:"°-",......";,4‘',.'';‘,,..!i2...1:,:t' , . • .. - - - „i- . '.,,"‘.' .. -•.• , • 1.4.44,`,11-: -,..";,,,r; . : ..40.....• -,4,5z.„,„.. ~..-..A..11.: -..., ~ •:,4....:": - , .. ~,,,. ....oth.l, ~. . -..:-.'41-!.:.,-;;; .:. .., ' -:"Aff,--." .:•-•.'-',..*. -.7.:- :. ''',*,--'z'.,r,: -.. ,tiv" `'.? "*. ' -1. , ' ti..l. OS!, , .4:4 .- , . '. ' s' -- ‘ -4..:4‘ ~..,j'...* ik •,. 01'1/44,4 t. 14; *.k.: , .:Vi4l,:ii::„T:-.,.•&gt;..,:'. ,' ,' „:„,iglrt''',l-.':."1-1,:`,4,'',- • ' . • ' ~34',....' -: ~,1,0,,., • , 0 «. A E.</p>
