      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>MOTOR TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>THE MOTOR TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>MR. VICTOR ROBERTS FINED FOR</p>
        <p>DANGEROUS DRIVING.</p>
        <p>LICENCE SUSPENDED FOR FIVE YEARS.</p>
        <p>Considerable interest was centred in a case, heard at the Warrington Borough Court on Thursday, which was an echo of the recent motor tragedy at Wilders- pool. The defendant was Mr. Victor Roberts, o f Grappenhall, the owner and driver of the car which collided with a governess car in the " bottle neck connecting the Wilderspool and Chester-roads, with the result that Mr. Joseph Witter Houghton, farmer, Woolston, sustained fatal injuries. The summons charged Mr. Roberts with having on May 11th driven a motor car in Chester-road in a manner which was dangerous to the public, having regard to all the circumstances. Mr. T. S Steel appeared far Mr. Roberts who pleaded zuilty. The magistrates on the bench were Mr. John Fairclough (in the chair), Alder- men Bennett, Dr Saden, Messrs W Roberts, Edwin Woods, John Upson, T Locker. A J Jolley, J Rymer Young and W T The Chief Constable detailed the facts of the case with which our readers are familiar. He added that in case there should be some question with regard to what action had been taken, it was only right that he should inform the Court that the statements of the whole of the witnesses and an account of the proceed- ings in the Coroner's Court had been placed before the Director of Public Prosecutions, with the result that the present proceedings, and no proceedings of a more serious nature, were instituted. John Homey, warehouseman in the employ of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., living at 94, Chester-road, was then called He stated that at 8.50 p.m. on May 11th, be was walking along Chester-road in the direction of home. He bad just left tbe narrow portion and on looking ahead saw a motor cycle approaching at a terrific pace and a motor car follow- ing it. He came to the conclusion that they must be racing. When the car passed him it was travelling at a very high rate of speed and in his opinion was a danger to the traffic that was or might have been there. Apart from the fact that the road ended in a bottle neck,' the mar was travelling to the danger of the public in the wider part. The Chief Constable : In the course of your experi- enca as a resident in Chester-road, have you ever seen a °sr travelling at, a greater pace? Witness: No I have not.</p>
        <p>Mr Steel : think you put the pica down at 25 miles an hour?</p>
        <p>Witness : Purely an estimate. Not more than 25 miles an hour ?—I 'would not say "not more." I simply gave an estimate. Martin Hughes, warehouseman, employed by the Firth Co., and living at 41, Hamilton-street, gave similar evidence, but stated that in his opinion the car was travelling at about 40 miles an hour, Whatever the Spf`Ad might have been, however, it was dangerous to traflio._</p>
        <p>..-...-- 3lr. Steel, addressing the Court, said Mr Roberts desired him publicly to express his very deep regret at what had occurred. He need hardly tell the Bench that since the accident Mr Roberts had been greatly distressed in his mind and he I had been under medical treatment for insomnia and loss of appetite. He (Mr Steel) could assure the Bench that the occurrence had made a very deep impression upon Mr Roberts—a deeper impression than any words of his could describe. "To show his state of mind," continued Mr Steel, " I am instructed to say that he tenders to the Bench the licence he holds. We are pre- pared to surrender and forfeit it. The effect has been so great that he has no further use for a motor licence. Mr Roberts is only a young man and his anguish of mind and his grief have already been a terrible punishment to him—a very much greater punishment than any monetary infliction—and he throws himself this morning uipTi Yon may see your onntothaeccmouenrtcy and theto Court. Having regard to his 300th, the punishment he has already undergone and the fact that he tenders to way to r wotartheipthrehaeis cliicrecoacmea,tlancheao) deal leniently with him." The Chairman : I know the case and I think you have taken a very right. course. Has there been any previous conviction? The Chief Constable: Defendant has been once previously convicted in this Court and he was then let off on payment of costs. That WAS some twelve months ago. The Chairman: The same kind of offences The Chief Constable : It was driving a car to the common danger in the vicinity of Palmyra-square. of course, it was nothing of the nature of the present charge. The magistrates retired and after they bad consulted for a short time,</p>
        <p>OFFICERSHIPS OF CUSTOMS EXCISE. AGE 19-21. SALARY, £BO-£320. There are many Higher °racks, with Salaries ranging to £1,200 per annum. 900 APPOINTMENTS LAST WEEK. producing a crop of oppor- tuTnirtuielys ftohrisAßYpeiararnitB9 for Civil Service Positions much heavier and of even Higher Quality than the average. The Civil Service Guide sent post free. ANOTHER EXAMINATION will be announced very shortly, and for it New Day, Evening, and Postal Preparatory Courses are now being arranged at</p>
        <p>SKERRY'S COLLEGE, 27, LEECE STREET, LIVERPOOL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>WAR4INGTON CYCLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>WAR4INGTON CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The run to Delatnere last Saturday was successfully carried out. On Thursday evenieg, a match with the Warrington Bowling Club was played at Arpley, the wheelers losing by 84 points. To-day, run to Sand- Bach; Wednesday, bowling at Sankey; Thursday, tun to Buckloiv Bill. WESLEYAN CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyan Cycling Club broke new ground last Saturday; 22 members ran through Comberbach to Anderton where they had the novelty of going over a salt making plant belonging to the Salt Union. Only fishery salt is turned out of this section of the works, —about 50 tons a day. Tea was obtained near Greenbank Station, but as the charke was considered excessive the name may be withheld. Then the real objective of the journey was reached ; leaving the cycles at the Monkey Lodge, Pettypool Park, the party strolled along the south side of the delightful Rookery Pool in Vale Royal Park. The water lilies were not yet out, but the rhododendrons show the same wealth of bloom as all the other flowering trees this year. Re-mounting they cycled and walked through Pettypool to the Tower Lodge, Sandiway, and had covered a good deal of the homeward run before the rain came on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Lamp time to-night 9.32, next Saturday 0 38.</head>
        <p>Lamp time to-night 9.32, next Saturday 0 38.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said : We have had this case under consideration and we have come to a unanimous con- clusion. The existing licence will be forfeited up to the end of the period now running and a further five years, and we also inflict a fine of £2O and costs. I hope it will be a wsrning to you. The above penalty is the maximum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Drama Twice Nightly. These are days of hustle and the two shows a night system, once confined to the music halls, has now found its way to the theatres. The innovation has been tried by the management of the Royal Court Theatre this week with such an encouraging degree or success that, except on special occasions, it may become a permanent institution. Although some laygoirs may resent the ruthless condensation invoiNed by the "potting" of a drama to bring it within the scope of an hour and three quarters entertainment, there is the welcome compensation that tedious inter- vals are abolished and no irrelevant and banal comic turns are allowed to interrupt the action of the story. "For Love sod the King," a romance from the pen of Mr. C. Watson Mill, makes a brisk and stirring show. Toe plot is rather orthodox in conception, for we are all m ire or less familiar with the story of the princess spirited away in babyhood and brought up in the obscurity of peasant life, only at last to come into her own. Mr. Mill has, however, invented a succession of thrilling happenings and the play has an attractive setting of regal and military display. It is a relief to turn from the crafti- ness and subtet fuge of secret conspirators to the trans- parently loyal devotion of " Jarval, the Britisher," who straightens out the tangled skein of events, only at the cost of his own life. There is a torture scene which smacks somewhat of "The Breed of the Treshams " but through it all the Britisher remains fdithful to his trust. Mr Frank Harvey makes a manly, interpid Jarval, voice and deportment alike enabling him to present the part with convincing power. To go through such trying scenes twice nightly must involve a strain, but Mr. Harvey throws himself whole-heartedly into the work and wins many a round of applause. Miss Edith Rutland finds great favour as Natila, the peasant girl, her acting combining natural vivacious- ness with flashes of emotional intensity, and the portrayal is in every way a worthy and effective one. Mr. David Curtis gives a striking study of Michael Obrenovic, the miser ; Mr. E. Dunstan is a typical prince-lover, and capital humour is provided by Mr. Oscar Power as Carl Openheina, the Prince's German friend. All the other parts are well-placed and the performance goes with admirable smoothness through- out.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S ATTRACTION. Lovers of really good dramas should not fail to visit the Royal Court Theatre next week where " The Ruin of a Countess " and 'The Nihilist " will be presented by Mr. 0. Silverstone's company. Mr. Silverstone is considered an expert in twice nightly drama production, having played this system six years ago and continually ever since. " The Ruin of a Countess," originally produced at the Theatre Royal, Sheffield, has everywhere been quoted as "far above ordinary dramas. ". It is distincily a new departure and deals with the greatest evil ever brought to these shores by the Yellow Race. It should not be missed " The Nihilist "is very well known and Mr. Silverstone has now played the villianons Prince at over 1,200 performances. Mr. Silverstone should be surrouuded by a host of friends next week for during nearly six years he was very popular as acting manager at the Royal Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. REV. A. M. MITCHELL LEAVES AS A PROTEST. MR. AWBERY ANIEF" UNFAIR ATTACKS."</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. REV. A. M. MITCHELL LEAVES AS A PROTEST. MR. AWBERY ANIEF" UNFAIR ATTACKS."</p>
        <p>Strong Letter.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Mr. E. L. Bent presiding, There were also present : Miss Broadbent, Mrs Simpson, Rev. A M Mitchell, Messrs A J Jolley (deputy-chairman), J Billinge, A Brooks, W Gill, 11 Lee, W C Brooks, 3 A Hughes, W Flanagan, M W Seton, G Mather, A Bottomley (clerk), R I Partridge (assistant clerk), 0 Moss (acting master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>BIG BUILDING SCHEME.</head>
        <p>BIG BUILDING SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Ate meeting of the Building Gommittee, the members inspected plans submitted by the following architects of the proposed building which the Guardians have decided to erect at the \Workhouse for the accommoda- tion of male and female imbecile and epileptic patients: Messrs W and S Owen, Wright and Hamlyn, A Warburton and P Siloock. An estimate of the cost of erection of the building to be carried out in accordance with plans submitted by each firm was presented as follows :—Messrs W and S Owen, total cost of build- ing, if normal prices can be assured, £11,259, add for present rise of .12 per cent, £1407, grand total £12,666 ; Messrs Wright and Hamlyn, £13,207 2s Gd ; Mr. A. Warburton, £10,500; Mr. Silcock, £14,640. The committee considered that a building to be erected in accordance with the plans submitted by Messrs W and S Owen, would be the most suitable and economical and they unanimously recommended that, provided a building could be erected in accordance with Messrs. Owens plans for a sum not excc:eding £12,661, the work be placed in their hands,to be proceeded with forthwith. Mr. A. BROOKS, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said that personally he should have been much better satisfied if the plans had been adjudicated upon under the same conditions as they dealt with tenders—that was that the committee should not have known whose plans they were dealing with until the selection had been made. (Hear, hear.) It would not, be Was certain, have made any difference in the result but it would have allayed any suspicion that favouritism would be shown. Four very excellent designs were submitted, and they indicated a consider- able amount of work and close study of the conditions that would have to obtain in such a building as the Board required. All the competitori had studied economy to a great extent. When they saw from news- paper reports that at the present time the Winwiek Asylum ,authorities were thinking of spending £l5O a bed in housing forty boys and remembered that the most costly design sent in for the work at the Warring- ton Workhouse was equal to only £9l a bed, be thought they might say that euonomy had been observed in each of the nesigos. Although the desigu of Messrs Owen was not, on paper, the most economical, the committee, after considering every point, were unanimous that it was the one they ought to accept. Mr. JoLLEY seconded. ... • _</p>
        <p>The Hey. A. M. MITCHELL said he understood it was the custom—although he did not necessarily approve of it in every case—to accept the lowest tender and he rather wondered that it had not been adhered to in connection with the proposed new building, lie did not for one moment dispute what Mr. Brooks had said; he had no doubt. the committee tried to act impartially, but the fact remained that the lowest tender had not been accepted. So far as he could gather, there had been no satisfactory exp;anation why the committee had not acted according to the usual custom. When it came to a matter of a thousand or two, it was an important consideration._</p>
        <p>i'be CHAIRMAN said the Board could gather from Mr Brooks' remarks that the committee had on certain grounds accepted practically the lowest tender, as it was thought the design chosen would tend to the most economical administration. _</p>
        <p>Mr BILLING&amp; said it was not the invariable custom to select the lowest tender.</p>
        <p>The Rev AM MITCHELL asked in what way the build- ing, according to the accepted design, would work out as a saving, as compared with the others ? Mr A BROOKS said the floorspace in the cheaper design was greater than in the one recommended, and fluor- space was the basis of cost. The building would have to be worked out at a less cost per cubic feet than Messrs Owen's design. How was tnat going to be done ? By cheaper work, and that was not desirable. The minutes were then approved. APPOINTMENTS. - • -</p>
        <p>On the proposition of -ICI.; -JOLLEY, the llosrd unanimously confirmed the appointment of Mr R lssell Partridge es first assistant clerk at a commencing salary of,£llo per annum and Mr W H Bell as second assistant clerk at a salary of £BO per annum. MR. AND MRS. AVVBERY'S SUPERANNUATION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-06-08_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>ANOTHER LIVELY SCENE.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER LIVELY SCENE.</p>
        <p>REV. A. M. MITCHELL LEAVES AS A PROTEST. The Rev A M MITcHtL then rcse to move the followingfresolution of which he bad given due notice:— "That the minute of May Bth relating to the increased superannuation allowance to be granted to Mr and Mrs Aabery be and is hereby rescinded." Mr J 131r.uNaz : Is Mr Mitchell proceeding with this resolution? The Rev A M MITCHELL: Yes.</p>
        <p>BILLINGIC: i he'd ic in mind a few moments ago to ask that the Board should go into committee. 1 have no knowledge of what the resolution means but I imagine it would be advisable that the Board should go into committee. It would appear that in all probability this is a controversial question on which there may be differences of opinion—a que.stion which affects— The Rev A M MITCHISLL : It is a question which the public should know about.</p>
        <p>Mr BILLINGE : Which affects probably character. The CHAIRMAN : Do p.m move that ? Mr BILLINOR : I move thac, the Board go into committee.</p>
        <p>Mr SETON seconded and the proposition was declared carried.</p>
        <p>The Bev A M MITCHELL: I take exception to any such motion. I submit it is not in order. The question should be taken in public by the Board. The CHAIRMAN: The Board have decided that we go into committee.</p>
        <p>The Rev A M MITCHELL: Let us have a vote on it, but I submit it is out of order. The CHAIRMAN :II don't know that it is out of order Toe Rev A M kliTcrtimr, : It is suppressing some- thing which ought to be known and which, as far as I am concerned, shall be known. If it is not known in this way it shall be known in another way. lam quite prepared to go to the Local Governmeat Board about it. The citantatArr : We will take a vote on it, before we proceed any further. The Rev A 'AI MITCHELL : First and foremost, I want to know if it is in order. I ask the Clerk. The CHAIRMAN : Perfectly in order. The CLERK I When a matter comes up like this, it is most important for my protection that you should snow that I cannot answer a member direct without the per- mission of the Chairman. I will answer any question through him. The Rev A M MITCHELL : I asked the Chairman. The CHAIRMAN : 1 rule that it is perfectly in ord.r. The same thing has taken place before and we are not going astray from our previous methods. Mrs Stiursom : I don't nee how it touches anyone's character. We are only going to say what is really true. The CHAIRMAN: That is a matter on which we shall have to wait and see.</p>
        <p>The resolution that the Board go into committee was than put to the meeting and six voted for it and six against. . The CHAIRMAN (who has a casting vote) : I declare the motion carried (to the Rev A M Mitchell). You may proceed eitti your resolution. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL: I decline to do so and I leave this meeting as a protest. I consider it a shameful proceeding, to try to burk public discussion. I shall follow the matter up. The CHAIRMAN : That is the decision of the Board. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL : I never can get justice at this Board. I consider the Beard has disgraced itself.</p>
        <p>The rev. gentleman then left the meeting. MR. AWl3Eity'S STRONG LETTER. " ATrACKS BEHIND HIS BACK." The CLERK said he had received a letter from Mr 3 F Awbery, the late master. It had been sent to him as official and he was bound to read it to the Board. It bad reference to certain remarks that had been made. Mr LEE : I think it ought to be read. Mrs SIMPSON: It is sent officially and it is meant to be made public. The CLERK : I simply mention it because of the resolution recently passed on a matter concerning Mr Awbery. The CHAIRMAN : It is a 'question of whether you resolve into committee or have the letter read publicly. Mr W C BitooKs : It is an official letter. Mr SETON moved that the letter be read in open meeting and the proposition was agreed to- The CLERK then read the letter as follows :—Hotel des Alpes, Lucerne, Switzerland. I am informed that at a recent relief meeting, in reference to a ease being assisted from the benevolent fund, that the following remark was made :—" 'rhere is no money in the Awbery Benevolent Fund. Mr. Awbery told someone in the to give it for one year, 80 train that he was on 4 going it won't amount to much." The first time I ever travelled in a train after my illness was when I left Warrington. I publicly challenge the member who made the statement to mention the party who said this. If this is not forthcoming, I can only draw one of two conclusions—that either the statement was self-inspired and not communicated, or that if the party's name was disclosed I could readily nail the lie to the counter. I wonder what underlies these indecen attacks and misrepresentations. If gossip of this sort is to be tolerated, without ascertaining first whether I ever did say such a thing and the name of the party responsible for circulat- ing it is to be suppressed, then I think all intelligent men and women will value such conduct as a gross abuse and misuse of the liberties and privileges</p>
      </div>
