        <p>with W Swanton and finally won by two chalks. 'At one time the Conservative had the score of 17-12 but he subsequently found the wrong length and at the 28th end the game was only 19-18 in his favour. Swanton got a nice double and a single and ran out the winner by 21-19, On the aggregate the Conservatives won by 30 points. -- WHITECROSS V. PENKRTH PARK. With only a 35 point win to dispose of, the White- cross men turned out on their own sward with great feelings of confidence and as a send-off A Cleave (captain Wbitecross) defeated S Glover by eleven points after 24 ends had been contested. Joe Gleave followed in the steps of big skipper and finished E Ashton by 21-11. G Warburton (Whitecross) kept the ball rolling and disposed of W Shave by 21-15. S Massey showed his great knowledge of the land and bad the odds of Joe Bowyer after the game had reached the 22nd end and finally Massey was the winner by four points. -- Ike Barlow had a stiff tussle on with J H Bowyer and indeed Bowyer was the favourite when he led by 12-6 at the 12th end. Barlow however found his land and at the 24th end he hsd a lead of 18-16. Bowyer added one chalk on the next length and with a double and single Barlow pulled tha game off with only four points to spare. H Bowyer (Penketh) was one of the only two villagers to win and he bad the advantage over P Cleave from the commencement and finally won by three points. J Bowyer was the only other Penkethite to record a win and be was successful in disposing of AThornley by 21-13 Tile remaining games were very decisive victories for the Whitecross team, only two of the last five players on the Penketh side getting their score to ten. The Whitecross team have thus won on the aggregate by 54 points, their victory on Saturday being by 89 points. The draw for the second round took place at a meet- ing of the association which was beld on Tuesday even- ing at the Pelican Hotel. Mr. Ball presided, and the treasurer (Mr. Freeman) and the secretary (Mr. Wood) sv-ola also present. The draw resulted as follows : Fearnhead v Bull's Head. Rylands v Whitecross. I 3) es : Cemetery No. 1, and Junior Conservatives. CONSOLATION COMPETITION. Senior Conservatives • Penketh. Byes : King and Queen, Cemetery No. 2, and Liberal Club. The matches will be played to-day and on the 15th inst. COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP. The draw for the fourth round in the Lancashire County Championship took place at Manchester this week and lass follows : Leigh Raileay v Warrington Cemetery on the Angel Green, Stretfora. Wigen v Roebuck at Whitefleld. Whitefield v Prestwich Conservatives on the Roebuck Fliston. Whitecress v Irlrm-o*th-Heights at Kersall Moor. The matches are to be played on Saturday next and a keen match is anticipated between the Cemetery men and Leigh Raileay. Vhitecroas should however pull it off at Kersall Moor. -- WARRINGTON WORKS COMPETITION. -- There was an exceptionally large number of spect tors on the Horse and Jockey Green on Saturday when the first round of the Warrington Works Bowling competition was continued. During the afternoon Mr. A. H. Crosfield, who bas taken a keen interest in the competitii n from its inception, paid the trundlers a visit and was very cordially welcomed. • The various teams appear to have been very fairly handicapped for the best win of the afternoon so far as points go, vtas one of 22 for Orford Shed who had Crosfleld's No. 2 as tbeir opponents. The green was in fine condition although the rains of the previous days had a tendency to make the going slightly on tne heavy side. However, this did not affect the play much and some gocd bowling was witnessed. P. and K. Boiler Shop and Greenings No. 1 teams were the first to lake possession of the sward and as the P. and K. team were only handicapped ten points ahead of their opponents a keen struggle was anticipated. -- W Alcock opened well for Greenings, for after only thirteen ends had been played he had run his score to fifteen whilst his opponent, it Sunderland had only four chalks on the card. Alcoa played a fine game and his achievement had an encouraging effect on his followers. R. Shaw, the captain of the Greening's team, was in his usual good 'cum and disposed of W Barron after fifteen ends, the P and K men having only two doubles and three singles to his credit. The next genie was also decided in favour of Greening's, W Patrick beating It Cooper 15-9. There seemed nothing much to choose between the players at the commencement and at the seventh end the score was five each. In the next five ends Patrick had added three doubles and two singles, and thus had a lead of 12-4. When, in the next three ends Cooper had brought his score to three behind that of his opponent, the game looked like having an exciting termination. Patrick found himself on a good length and added three points in the 16th and 17th ends and won by six chalks. Hickman (P and K) proved to be the first triumphant tiundler on his side and disposed of T Guamby by 15-9. At one time he had a lead of 9-2 but Guamby bowled well and at the 13th end reduced his opponents' lead to 10-8 Hickman got in the decisive woods in the next five lengths and won by half a dozen points. The P. and K. men were now only nine points behind their opponents and it was expected that J Armitage, the next man, would gain a lead for the foundrymen over J Appleton. The scores were very even from the first and at the 15th end the score was ten each. How- ever Appleton only got another single in and Armitage won by four points, the P and K men being still five points in the rear. The next game between W Blackledge (P and K) and R Clarke was a very close one and after having led by 13-8 on the 14th end Clarke eventually lost by two points after 20 ends had been played. The score was 13 each on the 19th end but Blackledge got a good double in the next length and thus reduced the Green- ings men's lead to three points on the game. E. Pickersgill (P and K) put up a good fight with W Bibby and there was much speculation as to which player the honours would finally fall. After some give and take play the scoie was ten each at the 13th end but from this juncture up to the conclusion of the game at the 17th end, Pickersgill failed to score and Bibby came off the green with a five point victory to his credit. With the scores showing a lead of eight points in favour of Greenings the and K men realized that they had a hard struggle before them to win but when J Cleave came up to expectations and dispored of C Mellington by 15-7 the foundry men bad just cause for excitement, for the score was now equal. When H. Ratcliffe (P and K) defeated E Mellington by 15-10 in the next game speculation was rife as to which team would be the ultimate winner of the game for tin P and K men were now five points ahead of their opponents. The lead was taken to nine points by R Howard, who disposed of J Green by 15-11 after 19 ends. The game had been a very close one, and the scores were equal at 3,5, 7,8, and 11, but Howard had a slight advantage and won by four chalks. A Graham reduced the P. and K. lead to three points. when he defeated W Brandwood by 15-9, after 19 ends The last game of the match between J. Fillingham and H Taylor (Greenings) was keenly watched, for it was on this game that the result of the match depended. Taylor led off well and was 7-0 at the 6th end. The foundryman ran his score to four tin fourteen ends, Taylor then only wanting one point for game. Fillingbam had added two points on the 16th length and his opponent scored his necessary one on the next length and won by eight points, the Greenings men having entered the second round with a five points win. -- Although Rylands Continuous Wire team were handi- capped at 40 points to their opponents Fletcher's No. 2 60 points they put up a good fight and some good cards were returned on both sides. ---- T Harisbury (Rylands) gained an eleven point victory over J Ashurst after fifteen ends had been played. There were only two other games which could possibly be termed runaways, these being the ones, in which J Hilton (Rylands) defeated J Kay 15-4 and J Rafferty beat T Byers (Rylands) by 15-7. A seven point victory was also recorded for 0 Harrison (Fletcher's) who beat H Higham by 15-8 after eighteen ends. One of the many good games was the one in which J Hankey (Rylands) gained a three point victory over N Woods. The game was a fairly even one from the beginning and at the 17th end the players peeled with 11 each, Woods only scored another single and with a good double and two singles in the next four lengths Hankey won by 15-12. Another light game was the one which resulted in a two point victory for W Whitfield (Fletcher's), pver Birchall. The players peeled with 4,5, 7, and TO, and at the nineteenth end Whit3eld had a lead of 14-13. added a good point on the next end and won by a coup!e. --- F Cooper (Rylands) did well to beat E Williams by two points for Williams brought up wonderfully after having been eight points behind at the 14th end when</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyans met Penketh Friends and achieved the honours by seven runs in a low scoring match, not one player placing double figures to his credit. The most humiliating defeat of the day was sustained by Cairo-street 2nd at the hands of Rylands 2nd who got them all out for a paltry 19 and then amassed 110. Gatenby and Williams were the chief destructive agent,.., while J Garrity headed the scores with 23. -- Warrington St Mary's had to bow the knee to Widnes St Mary's after a keen encounter, only 5 runs dividing the contestants. The feature of the match was the fine batting of S J Price who knocked up 29. -- Boteler Grammar School made the visit to Chester but proved unequaKto the King's School who won by the majority of 14. Woodcock, as usual, did useful service for the Botelerians, scoring 10 and accounting for five wickets, as well as bringing off a good catch. --- Lymm collapsed in unaccountable fashion at Arley The home eleven declared their innings at 116 for eight wickets and then tumbled out their opponents for an' insignificant 23. For the victors J Lees was the most successful batsman with 33, but, as the total indicates, ' none of the Lymm men made a presentable show. -- Oughtrington Park were also vanquished, their conquerors being Boughton Hall who secured a majority of 86. Carr, with a not out innings of 81, had a prominent part in the victory, while Woods (30) was the top scorer for the Park. Stockton Heath St. Thomas's and Workingmen's Mission were very evenly matched, the saints winning by 5 runs. J Seed, who made 15 for the mission, , secured the batting. To-day's fixtures in the W- arrington Sunday School League : • Walton Choir v 1331 d-street. Brick-street v Stockton Heath. Winwick Asylum Cricket Club experienced their second defeat of the season on Saturday when they had Eccleston as visitors. Winwick were all out for 87 and Eccleston made 109 for the loss of six wickets. Dawson (Winwick) compiled 35 before being out lbw. The visitors' chief scorers were J Rigby (33), J Critchley (27), R King (23), J Ankara (Eccleston), bowled five overs and five balls and took five wickets. J Halpin took 3 for 33. Hilton (Winwick) captured 4 for 43. -- Warrington Union made no mistake about their victory over Warrington Thursday. Declaring their innings at 119 for 9, the Union eleven sent their opponents to the right about for 28, Holt capturing four wickets for 15 runs and Green four for 13 runs. Holt batted brilliantly for 56, while Constable made 21 and Banner 16. Bate was the losers's best batsman , with 10.</p>
